\par
INTRODUCTION\par
\par
\par
MARCUS AURELIUS ANTONINUS was born on April 26, A.D. 121. His real name\par
was M. Annius Verus, and he was sprung of a noble family which claimed\par
descent from Numa, second King of Rome. Thus the most religious of\par
emperors came of the blood of the most pious of early kings. His father,\par
Annius Verus, had held high office in Rome, and his grandfather, of\par
the same name, had been thrice Consul. Both his parents died young, but\par
Marcus held them in loving remembrance. On his father's death Marcus\par
was adopted by his grandfather, the consular Annius Verus, and there was\par
deep love between these two. On the very first page of his book Marcus\par
gratefully declares how of his grandfather he had learned to be gentle\par
and meek, and to refrain from all anger and passion. The Emperor Hadrian\par
divined the fine character of the lad, whom he used to call not Verus\par
but Verissimus, more Truthful than his own name. He advanced Marcus to\par
equestrian rank when six years of age, and at the age of eight made him\par
a member of the ancient Salian priesthood. The boy's aunt, Annia Galeria\par
Faustina, was married to Antoninus Pius, afterwards emperor. Hence it\par
came about that Antoninus, having no son, adopted Marcus, changing his\par
name to that which he is known by, and betrothed him to his daughter\par
Faustina. His education was conducted with all care. The ablest teachers\par
were engaged for him, and he was trained in the strict doctrine of the\par
Stoic philosophy, which was his great delight. He was taught to dress\par
plainly and to live simply, to avoid all softness and luxury. His body\par
was trained to hardihood by wrestling, hunting, and outdoor games; and\par
though his constitution was weak, he showed great personal courage to\par
encounter the fiercest boars. At the same time he was kept from the\par
extravagancies of his day. The great excitement in Rome was the strife\par
of the Factions, as they were called, in the circus. The racing drivers\par
used to adopt one of four colours--red, blue, white, or green--and their\par
partisans showed an eagerness in supporting them which nothing could\par
surpass. Riot and corruption went in the train of the racing chariots;\par
and from all these things Marcus held severely aloof.\par
\par
In 140 Marcus was raised to the consulship, and in 145 his betrothal\par
was consummated by marriage. Two years later Faustina brought him a\par
daughter; and soon after the tribunate and other imperial honours were\par
conferred upon him.\par
\par
Antoninus Pius died in 161, and Marcus assumed the imperial state. He\par
at once associated with himself L. Ceionius Commodus, whom Antoninus had\par
adopted as a younger son at the same time with Marcus, giving him the\par
name of Lucius Aurelius Verus. Henceforth the two are colleagues in the\par
empire, the junior being trained as it were to succeed. No sooner was\par
Marcus settled upon the throne than wars broke out on all sides. In\par
the east, Vologeses III. of Parthia began a long-meditated revolt by\par
destroying a whole Roman Legion and invading Syria (162). Verus was sent\par
off in hot haste to quell this rising; and he fulfilled his trust by\par
plunging into drunkenness and debauchery, while the war was left to his\par
officers. Soon after Marcus had to face a more serious danger at home in\par
the coalition of several powerful tribes on the northern frontier. Chief\par
among those were the Marcomanni or Marchmen, the Quadi (mentioned in\par
this book), the Sarmatians, the Catti, the Jazyges. In Rome itself there\par
was pestilence and starvation, the one brought from the east by Verus's\par
legions, the other caused by floods which had destroyed vast quantities\par
of grain. After all had been done possible to allay famine and to supply\par
pressing needs--Marcus being forced even to sell the imperial jewels to\par
find money--both emperors set forth to a struggle which was to continue\par
more or less during the rest of Marcus's reign. During these wars, in\par
169, Verus died. We have no means of following the campaigns in detail;\par
but thus much is certain, that in the end the Romans succeeded in\par
crushing the barbarian tribes, and effecting a settlement which made the\par
empire more secure. Marcus was himself commander-in-chief, and victory\par
was due no less to his own ability than to his wisdom in choice of\par
lieutenants, shown conspicuously in the case of Pertinax. There were\par
several important battles fought in these campaigns; and one of them has\par
become celebrated for the legend of the Thundering Legion. In a battle\par
against the Quadi in 174, the day seemed to be going in favour of\par
the foe, when on a sudden arose a great storm of thunder and rain the\par
lightning struck the barbarians with terror, and they turned to rout.\par
In later days this storm was said to have been sent in answer to the\par
prayers of a legion which contained many Christians, and the name\par
Thundering Legion should be given to it on this account. The title of\par
Thundering Legion is known at an earlier date, so this part of the story\par
at least cannot be true; but the aid of the storm is acknowledged by one\par
of the scenes carved on Antonine's Column at Rome, which commemorates\par
these wars.\par
\par
The settlement made after these troubles might have been more\par
satisfactory but for an unexpected rising in the east. Avidius Cassius,\par
an able captain who had won renown in the Parthian wars, was at this\par
time chief governor of the eastern provinces. By whatever means induced,\par
he had conceived the project of proclaiming himself emperor as soon as\par
Marcus, who was then in feeble health, should die; and a report having\par
been conveyed to him that Marcus was dead, Cassius did as he had\par
planned. Marcus, on hearing the news, immediately patched up a peace and\par
returned home to meet this new peril. The emperors great grief was that\par
he must needs engage in the horrors of civil strife. He praised the\par
qualities of Cassius, and expressed a heartfelt wish that Cassius might\par
not be driven to do himself a hurt before he should have the opportunity\par
to grant a free pardon. But before he could come to the east news had\par
come to Cassius that the emperor still lived; his followers fell away\par
from him, and he was assassinated. Marcus now went to the east, and\par
while there the murderers brought the head of Cassius to him; but the\par
emperor indignantly refused their gift, nor would he admit the men to\par
his presence.\par
\par
On this journey his wife, Faustina, died. At his return the emperor\par
celebrated a triumph (176). Immediately afterwards he repaired to\par
Germany, and took up once more the burden of war. His operations were\par
followed by complete success; but the troubles of late years had been\par
too much for his constitution, at no time robust, and on March 17, 180,\par
he died in Pannonia.\par
\par
The good emperor was not spared domestic troubles. Faustina had borne\par
him several children, of whom he was passionately fond. Their innocent\par
faces may still be seen in many a sculpture gallery, recalling with odd\par
effect the dreamy countenance of their father. But they died one by\par
one, and when Marcus came to his own end only one of his sons still\par
lived--the weak and worthless Commodus. On his father's death Commodus,\par
who succeeded him, undid the work of many campaigns by a hasty and\par
unwise peace; and his reign of twelve years proved him to be a ferocious\par
and bloodthirsty tyrant. Scandal has made free with the name of Faustina\par
herself, who is accused not only of unfaithfulness, but of intriguing\par
with Cassius and egging him on to his fatal rebellion, it must be\par
admitted that these charges rest on no sure evidence; and the emperor,\par
at all events, loved her dearly, nor ever felt the slightest qualm of\par
suspicion.\par
\par
As a soldier we have seen that Marcus was both capable and successful;\par
as an administrator he was prudent and conscientious. Although steeped\par
in the teachings of philosophy, he did not attempt to remodel the world\par
on any preconceived plan. He trod the path beaten by his predecessors,\par
seeking only to do his duty as well as he could, and to keep out\par
corruption. He did some unwise things, it is true. To create a compeer\par
in empire, as he did with Verus, was a dangerous innovation which could\par
only succeed if one of the two effaced himself; and under Diocletian\par
this very precedent caused the Roman Empire to split into halves. He\par
erred in his civil administration by too much centralising. But the\par
strong point of his reign was the administration of justice. Marcus\par
sought by-laws to protect the weak, to make the lot of the slaves\par
less hard, to stand in place of father to the fatherless. Charitable\par
foundations were endowed for rearing and educating poor children. The\par
provinces were protected against oppression, and public help was given\par
to cities or districts which might be visited by calamity. The great\par
blot on his name, and one hard indeed to explain, is his treatment\par
of the Christians. In his reign Justin at Rome became a martyr to\par
his faith, and Polycarp at Smyrna, and we know of many outbreaks of\par
fanaticism in the provinces which caused the death of the faithful. It\par
is no excuse to plead that he knew nothing about the atrocities done in\par
his name: it was his duty to know, and if he did not he would have been\par
the first to confess that he had failed in his duty. But from his own\par
tone in speaking of the Christians it is clear he knew them only from\par
calumny; and we hear of no measures taken even to secure that they\par
should have a fair hearing. In this respect Trajan was better than he.\par
\par
To a thoughtful mind such a religion as that of Rome would give small\par
satisfaction. Its legends were often childish or impossible; its\par
teaching had little to do with morality. The Roman religion was in fact\par
of the nature of a bargain: men paid certain sacrifices and rites, and\par
the gods granted their favour, irrespective of right or wrong. In this\par
case all devout souls were thrown back upon philosophy, as they had\par
been, though to a less extent, in Greece. There were under the early\par
empire two rival schools which practically divided the field between\par
them, Stoicism and Epicureanism. The ideal set before each was\par
nominally much the same. The Stoics aspired to ἁπάθεια, the repression\par
of all emotion, and the Epicureans to ἀταραξία, freedom from all\par
disturbance; yet in the upshot the one has become a synonym of stubborn\par
endurance, the other for unbridled licence. With Epicureanism we have\par
nothing to do now; but it will be worth while to sketch the history and\par
tenets of the Stoic sect.\par
\par
Zeno, the founder of Stoicism, was born in Cyprus at some date unknown,\par
but his life may be said roughly to be between the years 350 and 250\par
B.C. Cyprus has been from time immemorial a meeting-place of the East\par
and West, and although we cannot grant any importance to a possible\par
strain of Phoenician blood in him (for the Phœnicians were no\par
philosophers), yet it is quite likely that through Asia Minor he may\par
have come in touch with the Far East. He studied under the cynic\par
Crates, but he did not neglect other philosophical systems. After many\par
years' study he opened his own school in a colonnade in Athens called\par
the Painted Porch, or Stoa, which gave the Stoics their name. Next to\par
Zeno, the School of the Porch owes most to Chrysippus (280--207 b.c.),\par
who organised Stoicism into a system. Of him it was said,\par
\par
'But for Chrysippus, there had been no Porch.'\par
\par
The Stoics regarded speculation as a means to an end and that end was,\par
as Zeno put it, to live consistently (ὁμολογουμένος ζῆν), or as it was\par
later explained, to live in conformity with nature (ὁμολογουμένος τῇ\par
φύσει ζῆν). This conforming of the life to nature was the Stoic idea of\par
Virtue. This dictum might easily be taken to mean that virtue consists\par
in yielding to each natural impulse; but that was very far from the\par
Stoic meaning. In order to live in accord with nature, it is necessary\par
to know what nature is; and to this end a threefold division of\par
philosophy is made—into _Physics_, dealing with the universe and its\par
laws, the problems of divine government and teleology; _Logic_, which\par
trains the mind to discern true from false; and _Ethics_, which applies\par
the knowledge thus gained and tested to practical life.\par
\par
The Stoic system of physics was materialism with an infusion of\par
pantheism. In contradiction to Plato's view that the Ideas, or\par
Prototypes, of phenomena alone really exist, the Stoics held that\par
material objects alone existed; but immanent in the material universe\par
was a spiritual force which acted through them, manifesting itself\par
under many forms, as fire, æther, spirit, soul, reason, the ruling\par
principle.\par
\par
The universe, then, is God, of whom the popular gods are manifestations;\par
while legends and myths are allegorical. The soul of man is thus an\par
emanation from the godhead, into whom it will eventually be re-absorbed.\par
The divine ruling principle makes all things work together for good,\par
but for the good of the whole. The highest good of man is consciously\par
to work with God for the common good, and this is the sense in which\par
the Stoic tried to live in accord with nature. In the individual it\par
is virtue alone which enables him to do this; as Providence rules the\par
universe, so virtue in the soul must rule man.\par
\par
In Logic, the Stoic system is noteworthy for their theory as to the\par
test of truth, the _Criterion_. They compared the new-born soul to a\par
sheet of paper ready for writing. Upon this the senses write their\par
impressions (φαντασίαι), and by experience of a number of these the\par
soul unconsciously conceives general notions (κοιναὶ ἔννοιαι) or\par
anticipations (προλήψεις). When the impression was such as to be\par
irresistible it was called (καταληπτικὴ φαντασία) one that holds fast,\par
or as they explained it, one proceeding from truth. Ideas and\par
inferences artificially produced by deduction or the like were tested\par
by this 'holding perception.' Of the Ethical application I have already\par
spoken. The highest good was the virtuous life. Virtue alone is\par
happiness, and vice is unhappiness. Carrying this theory to its\par
extreme, the Stoic said that there could be no gradations between\par
virtue and vice, though of course each has its special manifestations.\par
Moreover, nothing is good but virtue, and nothing but vice is bad.\par
Those outside things which are commonly called good or bad, such as\par
health and sickness, wealth and poverty, pleasure and pain, are to him\par
indifferent (ἀδιάφορα). All these things are merely the sphere in which\par
virtue may act. The ideal Wise Man is sufficient unto himself in all\par
things (αὐταρκής); and knowing these truths, he will be happy even when\par
stretched upon the rack. It is probable that no Stoic claimed for\par
himself that he was this Wise Man, but that each strove after it as an\par
ideal much as the Christian strives after a likeness to Christ. The\par
exaggeration in this statement was, however, so obvious, that the later\par
Stoics were driven to make a further subdivision of things indifferent\par
into what is preferable (προηγμένα) and what is undesirable\par
(ἀποπροηγμένα). They also held that for him who had not attained to the\par
perfect wisdom, certain actions were proper. (καθήκοντα) These were\par
neither virtuous nor vicious, but, like the indifferent things, held a\par
middle place.\par
\par
Two points in the Stoic system deserve special mention. One is a\par
careful distinction between things which are in our power and things\par
which are not. Desire and dislike, opinion and affection, are within\par
the power of the will; whereas health, wealth, honour, and other such\par
are generally not so. The Stoic was called upon to control his desires\par
and affections, and to guide his opinion; to bring his whole being\par
under the sway of the will or leading principle, just as the universe\par
is guided and governed by divine Providence. This is a special\par
application of the favourite Greek virtue of moderation, (σωφροσύνη)\par
and has also its parallel in Christian ethics. The second point is a\par
strong insistence on the unity of the universe, and on man's duty as\par
part of a great whole. Public spirit was the most splendid political\par
virtue of the ancient world, and it is here made cosmopolitan. It is\par
again instructive to note that Christian sages insisted on the same\par
thing. Christians are taught that they are members of a worldwide\par
brotherhood, where is neither Greek nor Hebrew, bond nor free and that\par
they live their lives as fellow-workers with God.\par
\par
Such is the system which underlies the Meditations of Marcus Aurelius.\par
Some knowledge of it is necessary to the right understanding of the\par
book, but for us the chief interest lies elsewhere. We do not come to\par
Marcus Aurelius for a treatise on Stoicism. He is no head of a school to\par
lay down a body of doctrine for students; he does not even contemplate\par
that others should read what he writes. His philosophy is not an eager\par
intellectual inquiry, but more what we should call religious feeling.\par
The uncompromising stiffness of Zeno or Chrysippus is softened and\par
transformed by passing through a nature reverent and tolerant, gentle\par
and free from guile; the grim resignation which made life possible to\par
the Stoic sage becomes in him almost a mood of aspiration. His book\par
records the innermost thoughts of his heart, set down to ease it, with\par
such moral maxims and reflections as may help him to bear the burden of\par
duty and the countless annoyances of a busy life.\par
\par
It is instructive to compare the _Meditations_ with another famous book,\par
the _Imitation of Christ_. There is the same ideal of self-control in\par
both. It should be a man's task, says the _Imitation_, 'to overcome\par
himself, and every day to be stronger than himself.' 'In withstanding of\par
the passions standeth very peace of heart.' 'Let us set the axe to the\par
root, that we being purged of our passions may have a peaceable mind.'\par
To this end there must be continual self-examination. 'If thou may not\par
continually gather thyself together, namely sometimes do it, at least\par
once a day, the morning or the evening. In the morning purpose, in the\par
evening discuss the manner, what thou hast been this day, in word, work,\par
and thought.' But while the Roman's temper is a modest self-reliance,\par
the Christian aims at a more passive mood, humbleness and meekness,\par
and reliance on the presence and personal friendship of God. The Roman\par
scrutinises his faults with severity, but without the self-contempt\par
which makes the Christian 'vile in his own sight.' The Christian, like\par
the Roman, bids 'study to withdraw thine heart from the love of things\par
visible'; but it is not the busy life of duty he has in mind so much as\par
the contempt of all worldly things, and the 'cutting away of all\par
lower delectations.' Both rate men's praise or blame at their real\par
worthlessness; 'Let not thy peace,' says the Christian, 'be in the\par
mouths of men.' But it is to God's censure the Christian appeals, the\par
Roman to his own soul. The petty annoyances of injustice or unkindness\par
are looked on by each with the same magnanimity. 'Why doth a little\par
thing said or done against thee make thee sorry? It is no new thing; it\par
is not the first, nor shall it be the last, if thou live long. At best\par
suffer patiently, if thou canst not suffer joyously.' The Christian\par
should sorrow more for other men's malice than for our own wrongs; but\par
the Roman is inclined to wash his hands of the offender. 'Study to be\par
patient in suffering and bearing other men's defaults and all manner\par
infirmities,' says the Christian; but the Roman would never have thought\par
to add, 'If all men were perfect, what had we then to suffer of other\par
men for God?' The virtue of suffering in itself is an idea which does\par
not meet us in the _Meditations_. Both alike realise that man is one of a\par
great community. 'No man is sufficient to himself,' says the Christian;\par
'we must bear together, help together, comfort together.' But while\par
he sees a chief importance in zeal, in exalted emotion that is, and\par
avoidance of lukewarmness, the Roman thought mainly of the duty to be\par
done as well as might be, and less of the feeling which should go with\par
the doing of it. To the saint as to the emperor, the world is a poor\par
thing at best. 'Verily it is a misery to live upon the earth,' says the\par
Christian; few and evil are the days of man's life, which passeth away\par
suddenly as a shadow.\par
\par
But there is one great difference between the two books we are\par
considering. The _Imitation_ is addressed to others, the _Meditations_\par
by the writer to himself. We learn nothing from the _Imitation_ of\par
the author's own life, except in so far as he may be assumed to have\par
practised his own preachings; the _Meditations_ reflect mood by mood the\par
mind of him who wrote them. In their intimacy and frankness lies their\par
great charm. These notes are not sermons; they are not even confessions.\par
There is always an air of self-consciousness in confessions; in such\par
revelations there is always a danger of unctuousness or of vulgarity for\par
the best of men. St. Augus-tine is not always clear of offence, and John\par
Bunyan himself exaggerates venial peccadilloes into heinous sins. But\par
Marcus Aurelius is neither vulgar nor unctuous; he extenuates nothing,\par
but nothing sets down in malice. He never poses before an audience; he\par
may not be profound, he is always sincere. And it is a lofty and serene\par
soul which is here disclosed before us. Vulgar vices seem to have no\par
temptation for him; this is not one tied and bound with chains which\par
he strives to break. The faults he detects in himself are often such as\par
most men would have no eyes to see. To serve the divine spirit which\par
is implanted within him, a man must 'keep himself pure from all violent\par
passion and evil affection, from all rashness and vanity, and from all\par
manner of discontent, either in regard of the gods or men': or, as he\par
says elsewhere, 'unspotted by pleasure, undaunted by pain.' Unwavering\par
courtesy and consideration are his aims. 'Whatsoever any man either\par
doth or saith, thou must be good;' 'doth any man offend? It is against\par
himself that he doth offend: why should it trouble thee?' The offender\par
needs pity, not wrath; those who must needs be corrected, should be\par
treated with tact and gentleness; and one must be always ready to learn\par
better. 'The best kind of revenge is, not to become like unto them.'\par
There are so many hints of offence forgiven, that we may believe the\par
notes followed sharp on the facts. Perhaps he has fallen short of his\par
aim, and thus seeks to call his principles to mind, and to strengthen\par
himself for the future. That these sayings are not mere talk is plain\par
from the story of Avidius Cassius, who would have usurped his imperial\par
throne. Thus the emperor faithfully carries out his own principle, that\par
evil must be overcome with good. For each fault in others, Nature (says\par
he) has given us a counteracting virtue; 'as, for example, against the\par
unthankful, it hath given goodness and meekness, as an antidote.'\par
\par
One so gentle towards a foe was sure to be a good friend; and indeed his\par
pages are full of generous gratitude to those who had served him. In his\par
First Book he sets down to account all the debts due to his kinsfolk\par
and teachers. To his grandfather he owed his own gentle spirit, to\par
his father shamefastness and courage; he learnt of his mother to be\par
religious and bountiful and single-minded. Rusticus did not work in\par
vain, if he showed his pupil that his life needed amending. Apollonius\par
taught him simplicity, reasonableness, gratitude, a love of true\par
liberty. So the list runs on; every one he had dealings with seems\par
to have given him something good, a sure proof of the goodness of his\par
nature, which thought no evil.\par
\par
If his was that honest and true heart which is the Christian ideal, this\par
is the more wonderful in that he lacked the faith which makes Christians\par
strong. He could say, it is true, 'either there is a God, and then all\par
is well; or if all things go by chance and fortune, yet mayest thou use\par
thine own providence in those things that concern thee properly; and\par
then art thou well.' Or again, 'We must needs grant that there is a\par
nature that doth govern the universe.' But his own part in the scheme\par
of things is so small, that he does not hope for any personal happiness\par
beyond what a serene soul may win in this mortal life. 'O my soul, the\par
time I trust will be, when thou shalt be good, simple, more open and\par
visible, than that body by which it is enclosed;' but this is said of\par
the calm contentment with human lot which he hopes to attain, not of a\par
time when the trammels of the body shall be cast off. For the rest, the\par
world and its fame and wealth, 'all is vanity.' The gods may perhaps\par
have a particular care for him, but their especial care is for the\par
universe at large: thus much should suffice. His gods are better than\par
the Stoic gods, who sit aloof from all human things, untroubled and\par
uncaring, but his personal hope is hardly stronger. On this point he\par
says little, though there are many allusions to death as the natural\par
end; doubtless he expected his soul one day to be absorbed into the\par
universal soul, since nothing comes out of nothing, and nothing can be\par
annihilated. His mood is one of strenuous weariness; he does his duty as\par
a good soldier, waiting for the sound of the trumpet which shall sound\par
the retreat; he has not that cheerful confidence which led Socrates\par
through a life no less noble, to a death which was to bring him into the\par
company of gods he had worshipped and men whom he had revered.\par
\par
But although Marcus Aurelius may have held intellectually that his soul\par
was destined to be absorbed, and to lose consciousness of itself, there\par
were times when he felt, as all who hold it must sometimes feel, how\par
unsatisfying is such a creed. Then he gropes blindly after something\par
less empty and vain. 'Thou hast taken ship,' he says, 'thou hast sailed,\par
thou art come to land, go out, if to another life, there also shalt\par
thou find gods, who are everywhere.' There is more in this than the\par
assumption of a rival theory for argument's sake. If worldly things\par
'be but as a dream, the thought is not far off that there may be an\par
awakening to what is real. When he speaks of death as a necessary\par
change, and points out that nothing useful and profitable can be brought\par
about without change, did he perhaps think of the change in a corn of\par
wheat, which is not quickened except it die? Nature's marvellous power\par
of recreating out of Corruption is surely not confined to bodily things.\par
Many of his thoughts sound like far-off echoes of St. Paul; and it is\par
strange indeed that this most Christian of emperors has nothing good\par
to say of the Christians. To him they are only sectaries 'violently and\par
passionately set upon opposition.\par
\par
Profound as philosophy these _Meditations_ certainly are not; but Marcus\par
Aurelius was too sincere not to see the essence of such things as\par
came within his experience. Ancient religions were for the most\par
part concerned with outward things. Do the necessary rites, and you\par
propitiate the gods; and these rites were often trivial, sometimes\par
violated right feeling or even morality. Even when the gods stood on the\par
side of righteousness, they were concerned with the act more than with\par
the intent. But Marcus Aurelius knows that what the heart is full of,\par
the man will do. 'Such as thy thoughts and ordinary cogitations are,' he\par
says, 'such will thy mind be in time.' And every page of the book shows\par
us that he knew thought was sure to issue in act. He drills his soul, as\par
it were, in right principles, that when the time comes, it may be guided\par
by them. To wait until the emergency is to be too late.\par
\par
He sees also the true essence of happiness. 'If happiness did consist\par
in pleasure, how came notorious robbers, impure abominable livers,\par
parricides, and tyrants, in so large a measure to have their part of\par
pleasures?' He who had all the world's pleasures at command can write\par
thus 'A happy lot and portion is, good inclinations of the soul, good\par
desires, good actions.'\par
\par
By the irony of fate this man, so gentle and good, so desirous of quiet\par
joys and a mind free from care, was set at the head of the Roman Empire\par
when great dangers threatened from east and west. For several years he\par
himself commanded his armies in chief. In camp before the Quadi he dates\par
the first book of his _Meditations_, and shows how he could retire within\par
himself amid the coarse clangour of arms. The pomps and glories which\par
he despised were all his; what to most men is an ambition or a dream, to\par
him was a round of weary tasks which nothing but the stern sense of duty\par
could carry him through. And he did his work well. His wars were slow\par
and tedious, but successful. With a statesman's wisdom he foresaw the\par
danger to Rome of the barbarian hordes from the north, and took measures\par
to meet it. As it was, his settlement gave two centuries of respite\par
to the Roman Empire; had he fulfilled the plan of pushing the imperial\par
frontiers to the Elbe, which seems to have been in his mind, much more\par
might have been accomplished. But death cut short his designs.\par
\par
Truly a rare opportunity was given to Marcus Aurelius of showing what\par
the mind can do in despite of circumstances. Most peaceful of warriors,\par
a magnificent monarch whose ideal was quiet happiness in home life, bent\par
to obscurity yet born to greatness, the loving father of children who\par
died young or turned out hateful, his life was one paradox. That nothing\par
might lack, it was in camp before the face of the enemy that he passed\par
away and went to his own place.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
The following is a list of the chief English translations of Marcus\par
Aurelius: (1) By Meric Casaubon, 1634; (2) Jeremy Collier, 1701; (3)\par
James Thomson, 1747; (4) R. Graves, 1792; (5) H. McCormac, 1844; (6)\par
George Long, 1862; (7) G. H. Rendall, 1898; and (8) J. Jackson, 1906.\par
Renan’s “Marc-Aurèle”—in his “History of the Origins of Christianity,”\par
which appeared in 1882—is the most vital and original book to be had\par
relating to the time of Marcus Aurelius. Pater’s “Marius the Epicurean”\par
forms another outside commentary, which is of service in the\par
imaginative attempt to create again the period.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
MARCUS AURELIUS ANTONINUS THE ROMAN EMPEROR\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
HIS FIRST BOOK\par
\par
concerning HIMSELF:\par
\par
Wherein Antoninus recordeth, What and of whom, whether Parents, Friends,\par
or Masters; by their good examples, or good advice and counsel, he had\par
learned:\par
\par
Divided into Numbers or Sections.\par
\par
ANTONINUS Book vi. Num. xlviii. Whensoever thou wilt rejoice thyself,\par
think and meditate upon those good parts and especial gifts, which thou\par
hast observed in any of them that live with thee:\par
\par
as industry in one, in another modesty, in another bountifulness, in\par
another some other thing. For nothing can so much rejoice thee, as\par
the resemblances and parallels of several virtues, eminent in the\par
dispositions of them that live with thee, especially when all at once,\par
as it were, they represent themselves unto thee. See therefore, that\par
thou have them always in a readiness.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
THE FIRST BOOK\par
\par
\par
I. Of my grandfather Verus I have learned to be gentle and meek, and to\par
refrain from all anger and passion. From the fame and memory of him that\par
begot me I have learned both shamefastness and manlike behaviour. Of my\par
mother I have learned to be religious, and bountiful; and to forbear,\par
not only to do, but to intend any evil; to content myself with a spare\par
diet, and to fly all such excess as is incidental to great wealth. Of my\par
great-grandfather, both to frequent public schools and auditories, and\par
to get me good and able teachers at home; and that I ought not to think\par
much, if upon such occasions, I were at excessive charges.\par
\par
II. Of him that brought me up, not to be fondly addicted to either of\par
the two great factions of the coursers in the circus, called Prasini,\par
and Veneti: nor in the amphitheatre partially to favour any of the\par
gladiators, or fencers, as either the Parmularii, or the Secutores.\par
Moreover, to endure labour; nor to need many things; when I have\par
anything to do, to do it myself rather than by others; not to meddle\par
with many businesses; and not easily to admit of any slander.\par
\par
III. Of Diognetus, not to busy myself about vain things, and not easily\par
to believe those things, which are commonly spoken, by such as take upon\par
them to work wonders, and by sorcerers, or prestidigitators, and\par
impostors; concerning the power of charms, and their driving out of\par
demons, or evil spirits; and the like. Not to keep quails for the game;\par
nor to be mad after such things. Not to be offended with other men's\par
liberty of speech, and to apply myself unto philosophy. Him also I must\par
thank, that ever I heard first Bacchius, then Tandasis and Marcianus,\par
and that I did write dialogues in my youth; and that I took liking to\par
the philosophers' little couch and skins, and such other things, which\par
by the Grecian discipline are proper to those who profess philosophy.\par
\par
IV. To Rusticus I am beholding, that I first entered into the conceit\par
that my life wanted some redress and cure. And then, that I did not\par
fall into the ambition of ordinary sophists, either to write tracts\par
concerning the common theorems, or to exhort men unto virtue and the\par
study of philosophy by public orations; as also that I never by way of\par
ostentation did affect to show myself an active able man, for any kind\par
of bodily exercises. And that I gave over the study of rhetoric and\par
poetry, and of elegant neat language. That I did not use to walk about\par
the house in my long robe, nor to do any such things. Moreover I learned\par
of him to write letters without any affectation, or curiosity; such as\par
that was, which by him was written to my mother from Sinuessa: and to be\par
easy and ready to be reconciled, and well pleased again with them that\par
had offended me, as soon as any of them would be content to seek unto\par
me again. To read with diligence; not to rest satisfied with a light and\par
superficial knowledge, nor quickly to assent to things commonly spoken\par
of: whom also I must thank that ever I lighted upon Epictetus his\par
_Hypomnemata_, or moral commentaries and common-factions: which also he\par
gave me of his own.\par
\par
V. From Apollonius, true liberty, and unvariable steadfastness, and not\par
to regard anything at all, though never so little, but right and reason:\par
and always, whether in the sharpest pains, or after the loss of a child,\par
or in long diseases, to be still the same man; who also was a present\par
and visible example unto me, that it was possible for the same man to\par
be both vehement and remiss: a man not subject to be vexed, and offended\par
with the incapacity of his scholars and auditors in his lectures and\par
expositions; and a true pattern of a man who of all his good gifts\par
and faculties, least esteemed in himself, that his excellent skill and\par
ability to teach and persuade others the common theorems and maxims of\par
the Stoic philosophy. Of him also I learned how to receive favours and\par
kindnesses (as commonly they are accounted:) from friends, so that I\par
might not become obnoxious unto them, for them, nor more yielding upon\par
occasion, than in right I ought; and yet so that I should not pass them\par
neither, as an unsensible and unthankful man.\par
\par
VI. Of Sextus, mildness and the pattern of a family governed with\par
paternal affection; and a purpose to live according to nature: to be\par
grave without affectation: to observe carefully the several dispositions\par
of my friends, not to be offended with idiots, nor unseasonably to set\par
upon those that are carried with the vulgar opinions, with the theorems,\par
and tenets of philosophers: his conversation being an example how a man\par
might accommodate himself to all men and companies; so that though his\par
company were sweeter and more pleasing than any flatterer's cogging and\par
fawning; yet was it at the same time most respected and reverenced: who\par
also had a proper happiness and faculty, rationally and methodically to\par
find out, and set in order all necessary determinations and instructions\par
for a man's life. A man without ever the least appearance of anger, or\par
any other passion; able at the same time most exactly to observe the\par
Stoic _Apathia_, or unpassionateness, and yet to be most tender-hearted:\par
ever of good credit; and yet almost without any noise, or rumour: very\par
learned, and yet making little show.\par
\par
VII. From Alexander the Grammarian, to be un-reprovable myself, and not\par
reproachfully to reprehend any man for a barbarism, or a solecism, or\par
any false pronunciation, but dextrously by way of answer, or testimony,\par
or confirmation of the same matter (taking no notice of the word) to\par
utter it as it should have been spoken; or by some other such close and\par
indirect admonition, handsomely and civilly to tell him of it.\par
\par
VIII. Of Fronto, to how much envy and fraud and hypocrisy the state of a\par
tyrannous king is subject unto, and how they who are commonly called\par
εὐπατρίδαι, _i.e._ nobly born, are in some sort incapable, or void\par
of natural affection.\par
\par
IX. Of Alexander the Platonic, not often nor without great necessity to\par
say, or to write to any man in a letter, 'I am not at leisure'; nor in\par
this manner still to put off those duties, which we owe to our friends\par
and acquaintances (to every one in his kind) under pretence of urgent\par
affairs.\par
\par
X. Of Catulus, not to contemn any friend's expostulation, though unjust,\par
but to strive to reduce him to his former disposition: freely and\par
heartily to speak well of all my masters upon any occasion, as it is\par
reported of Domitius, and Athenodotus: and to love my children with true\par
affection.\par
\par
XI. From my brother Severus, to be kind and loving to all them of my\par
house and family; by whom also I came to the knowledge of Thrasea and\par
Helvidius, and Cato, and Dio, and Brutus. He it was also that did put me\par
in the first conceit and desire of an equal commonwealth, administered\par
by justice and equality; and of a kingdom wherein should be regarded\par
nothing more than the good and welfare of the subjects. Of him also,\par
to observe a constant tenor, (not interrupted, with any other cares and\par
distractions,) in the study and esteem of philosophy: to be bountiful\par
and liberal in the largest measure; always to hope the best; and to\par
be confident that my friends love me. In whom I moreover observed open\par
dealing towards those whom he reproved at any time, and that his friends\par
might without all doubt or much observation know what he would, or would\par
not, so open and plain was he.\par
\par
XII. From Claudius Maximus, in all things to endeavour to have power\par
of myself, and in nothing to be carried about; to be cheerful and\par
courageous in all sudden chances and accidents, as in sicknesses: to\par
love mildness, and moderation, and gravity: and to do my business,\par
whatsoever it be, thoroughly, and without querulousness. Whatsoever\par
he said, all men believed him that as he spake, so he thought, and\par
whatsoever he did, that he did it with a good intent. His manner was,\par
never to wonder at anything; never to be in haste, and yet never\par
slow: nor to be perplexed, or dejected, or at any time unseemly, or\par
excessively to laugh: nor to be angry, or suspicious, but ever ready to\par
do good, and to forgive, and to speak truth; and all this, as one that\par
seemed rather of himself to have been straight and right, than ever to\par
have been rectified or redressed; neither was there any man that ever\par
thought himself undervalued by him, or that could find in his heart, to\par
think himself a better man than he. He would also be very pleasant and\par
gracious.\par
\par
XIII. In my father, I observed his meekness; his constancy without\par
wavering in those things, which after a due examination and\par
deliberation, he had determined. How free from all vanity he carried\par
himself in matter of honour and dignity, (as they are esteemed:) his\par
laboriousness and assiduity, his readiness to hear any man, that had\par
aught to say tending to any common good: how generally and impartially\par
he would give every man his due; his skill and knowledge, when rigour\par
or extremity, or when remissness or moderation was in season; how he did\par
abstain from all unchaste love of youths; his moderate condescending to\par
other men's occasions as an ordinary man, neither absolutely requiring\par
of his friends, that they should wait upon him at his ordinary meals,\par
nor that they should of necessity accompany him in his journeys; and\par
that whensoever any business upon some necessary occasions was to be put\par
off and omitted before it could be ended, he was ever found when he\par
went about it again, the same man that he was before. His accurate\par
examination of things in consultations, and patient hearing of others.\par
He would not hastily give over the search of the matter, as one easy to\par
be satisfied with sudden notions and apprehensions. His care to preserve\par
his friends; how neither at any time he would carry himself towards them\par
with disdainful neglect, and grow weary of them; nor yet at any time\par
be madly fond of them. His contented mind in all things, his cheerful\par
countenance, his care to foresee things afar off, and to take order for\par
the least, without any noise or clamour. Moreover how all acclamations\par
and flattery were repressed by him: how carefully he observed all things\par
necessary to the government, and kept an account of the common expenses,\par
and how patiently he did abide that he was reprehended by some for this\par
his strict and rigid kind of dealing. How he was neither a superstitious\par
worshipper of the gods, nor an ambitious pleaser of men, or studious of\par
popular applause; but sober in all things, and everywhere observant of\par
that which was fitting; no affecter of novelties: in those things which\par
conduced to his ease and convenience, (plenty whereof his fortune\par
did afford him,) without pride and bragging, yet with all freedom and\par
liberty: so that as he did freely enjoy them without any anxiety or\par
affectation when they were present; so when absent, he found no want\par
of them. Moreover, that he was never commended by any man, as either a\par
learned acute man, or an obsequious officious man, or a fine orator; but\par
as a ripe mature man, a perfect sound man; one that could not endure to\par
be flattered; able to govern both himself and others. Moreover, how much\par
he did honour all true philosophers, without upbraiding those that were\par
not so; his sociableness, his gracious and delightful conversation, but\par
never unto satiety; his care of his body within bounds and measure,\par
not as one that desired to live long, or over-studious of neatness, and\par
elegancy; and yet not as one that did not regard it: so that through his\par
own care and providence, he seldom needed any inward physic, or outward\par
applications: but especially how ingeniously he would yield to any that\par
had obtained any peculiar faculty, as either eloquence, or the knowledge\par
of the laws, or of ancient customs, or the like; and how he concurred\par
with them, in his best care and endeavour that every one of them might\par
in his kind, for that wherein he excelled, be regarded and esteemed: and\par
although he did all things carefully after the ancient customs of his\par
forefathers, yet even of this was he not desirous that men should take\par
notice, that he did imitate ancient customs. Again, how he was not\par
easily moved and tossed up and down, but loved to be constant, both in\par
the same places and businesses; and how after his great fits of headache\par
he would return fresh and vigorous to his wonted affairs. Again, that\par
secrets he neither had many, nor often, and such only as concerned\par
public matters: his discretion and moderation, in exhibiting of the\par
public sights and shows for the pleasure and pastime of the people: in\par
public buildings. congiaries, and the like. In all these things,\par
having a respect unto men only as men, and to the equity of the things\par
themselves, and not unto the glory that might follow. Never wont to\par
use the baths at unseasonable hours; no builder; never curious, or\par
solicitous, either about his meat, or about the workmanship, or colour\par
of his clothes, or about anything that belonged to external beauty.\par
In all his conversation, far from all inhumanity, all boldness, and\par
incivility, all greediness and impetuosity; never doing anything with\par
such earnestness, and intention, that a man could say of him, that\par
he did sweat about it: but contrariwise, all things distinctly, as at\par
leisure; without trouble; orderly, soundly, and agreeably. A man might\par
have applied that to him, which is recorded of Socrates, that he knew\par
how to want, and to enjoy those things, in the want whereof, most men\par
show themselves weak; and in the fruition, intemperate: but to hold out\par
firm and constant, and to keep within the compass of true moderation and\par
sobriety in either estate, is proper to a man, who hath a perfect and\par
invincible soul; such as he showed himself in the sickness of Maximus.\par
\par
XIV. From the gods I received that I had good grandfathers, and parents,\par
a good sister, good masters, good domestics, loving kinsmen, almost all\par
that I have; and that I never through haste and rashness transgressed\par
against any of them, notwithstanding that my disposition was such,\par
as that such a thing (if occasion had been) might very well have been\par
committed by me, but that It was the mercy of the gods, to prevent such\par
a concurring of matters and occasions, as might make me to incur this\par
blame. That I was not long brought up by the concubine of my father;\par
that I preserved the flower of my youth. That I took not upon me to be\par
a man before my time, but rather put it off longer than I needed. That\par
I lived under the government of my lord and father, who would take\par
away from me all pride and vainglory, and reduce me to that conceit and\par
opinion that it was not impossible for a prince to live in the court\par
without a troop of guards and followers, extraordinary apparel, such\par
and such torches and statues, and other like particulars of state and\par
magnificence; but that a man may reduce and contract himself almost to\par
the state of a private man, and yet for all that not to become the more\par
base and remiss in those public matters and affairs, wherein power and\par
authority is requisite. That I have had such a brother, who by his own\par
example might stir me up to think of myself; and by his respect and\par
love, delight and please me. That I have got ingenuous children, and\par
that they were not born distorted, nor with any other natural deformity.\par
That I was no great proficient in the study of rhetoric and poetry, and\par
of other faculties, which perchance I might have dwelt upon, if I had\par
found myself to go on in them with success. That I did by times prefer\par
those, by whom I was brought up, to such places and dignities, which\par
they seemed unto me most to desire; and that I did not put them off with\par
hope and expectation, that (since that they were yet but young) I would\par
do the same hereafter. That I ever knew Apollonius and Rusticus, and\par
Maximus. That I have had occasion often and effectually to consider and\par
meditate with myself, concerning that life which is according to nature,\par
what the nature and manner of it is: so that as for the gods and such\par
suggestions, helps and inspirations, as might be expected from them,\par
nothing did hinder, but that I might have begun long before to live\par
according to nature; or that even now that I was not yet partaker and\par
in present possession of that life, that I myself (in that I did not\par
observe those inward motions, and suggestions, yea and almost plain and\par
apparent instructions and admonitions of the gods,) was the only cause\par
of it. That my body in such a life, hath been able to hold out so long.\par
That I never had to do with Benedicta and Theodotus, yea and afterwards\par
when I fell into some fits of love, I was soon cured. That having been\par
often displeased with Rusticus, I never did him anything for which\par
afterwards I had occasion to repent. That it being so that my mother was\par
to die young, yet she lived with me all her latter years. That as often\par
as I had a purpose to help and succour any that either were poor, or\par
fallen into some present necessity, I never was answered by my officers\par
that there was not ready money enough to do it; and that I myself never\par
had occasion to require the like succour from any other. That I have\par
such a wife, so obedient, so loving, so ingenuous. That I had choice of\par
fit and able men, to whom I might commit the bringing up of my children.\par
That by dreams I have received help, as for other things, so in\par
particular, how I might stay my casting of blood, and cure my dizziness,\par
as that also that happened to thee in Cajeta, as unto Chryses when he\par
prayed by the seashore. And when I did first apply myself to philosophy,\par
that I did not fall into the hands of some sophists, or spent my time\par
either in reading the manifold volumes of ordinary philosophers, nor in\par
practising myself in the solution of arguments and fallacies, nor dwelt\par
upon the studies of the meteors, and other natural curiosities. All\par
these things without the assistance of the gods, and fortune, could not\par
have been.\par
\par
XV. In the country of the Quadi at Granua, these. Betimes in the morning\par
say to thyself, This day I shalt have to do with an idle curious man,\par
with an unthankful man, a railer, a crafty, false, or an envious man; an\par
unsociable uncharitable man. All these ill qualities have happened unto\par
them, through ignorance of that which is truly good and truly bad. But I\par
that understand the nature of that which is good, that it only is to\par
be desired, and of that which is bad, that it only is truly odious and\par
shameful: who know moreover, that this transgressor, whosoever he be, is\par
my kinsman, not by the same blood and seed, but by participation of the\par
same reason, and of the same divine particle; How can I either be\par
hurt by any of those, since it is not in their power to make me incur\par
anything that is truly reproachful? or angry, and ill affected towards\par
him, who by nature is so near unto me? for we are all born to be\par
fellow-workers, as the feet, the hands, and the eyelids; as the rows of\par
the upper and under teeth: for such therefore to be in opposition, is\par
against nature; and what is it to chafe at, and to be averse from, but\par
to be in opposition?\par
\par
XVI. Whatsoever I am, is either flesh, or life, or that which we\par
commonly call the mistress and overruling part of man; reason. Away with\par
thy books, suffer not thy mind any more to be distracted, and carried to\par
and fro; for it will not be; but as even now ready to die, think little\par
of thy flesh: blood, bones, and a skin; a pretty piece of knit and\par
twisted work, consisting of nerves, veins and arteries; think no more of\par
it, than so. And as for thy life, consider what it is; a wind; not one\par
constant wind neither, but every moment of an hour let out, and sucked\par
in again. The third, is thy ruling part; and here consider; Thou art an\par
old man; suffer not that excellent part to be brought in subjection, and\par
to become slavish: suffer it not to be drawn up and down with\par
unreasonable and unsociable lusts and motions, as it were with wires and\par
nerves; suffer it not any more, either to repine at anything now\par
present, or to fear and fly anything to come, which the destiny hath\par
appointed thee.\par
\par
XVII. Whatsoever proceeds from the gods immediately, that any man will\par
grant totally depends from their divine providence. As for those\par
things that are commonly said to happen by fortune, even those must be\par
conceived to have dependence from nature, or from that first and general\par
connection, and concatenation of all those things, which more apparently\par
by the divine providence are administered and brought to pass.\par
All things flow from thence: and whatsoever it is that is, is both\par
necessary, and conducing to the whole (part of which thou art), and\par
whatsoever it is that is requisite and necessary for the preservation of\par
the general, must of necessity for every particular nature, be good and\par
behoveful. And as for the whole, it is preserved, as by the perpetual\par
mutation and conversion of the simple elements one into another, so\par
also by the mutation, and alteration of things mixed and compounded. Let\par
these things suffice thee; let them be always unto thee, as thy general\par
rules and precepts. As for thy thirst after books, away with it with all\par
speed, that thou die not murmuring and complaining, but truly meek and\par
well satisfied, and from thy heart thankful unto the gods.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
THE SECOND BOOK\par
\par
\par
I. Remember how long thou hast already put off these things, and how\par
often a certain day and hour as it were, having been set unto thee by\par
the gods, thou hast neglected it. It is high time for thee to understand\par
the true nature both of the world, whereof thou art a part; and of that\par
Lord and Governor of the world, from whom, as a channel from the spring,\par
thou thyself didst flow: and that there is but a certain limit of time\par
appointed unto thee, which if thou shalt not make use of to calm and\par
allay the many distempers of thy soul, it will pass away and thou with\par
it, and never after return.\par
\par
II. Let it be thy earnest and incessant care as a Roman and a man to\par
perform whatsoever it is that thou art about, with true and unfeigned\par
gravity, natural affection, freedom and justice: and as for all other\par
cares, and imaginations, how thou mayest ease thy mind of them. Which\par
thou shalt do; if thou shalt go about every action as thy last action,\par
free from all vanity, all passionate and wilful aberration from reason,\par
and from all hypocrisy, and self-love, and dislike of those things,\par
which by the fates or appointment of God have happened unto thee. Thou\par
seest that those things, which for a man to hold on in a prosperous\par
course, and to live a divine life, are requisite and necessary, are not\par
many, for the gods will require no more of any man, that shall but keep\par
and observe these things.\par
\par
III. Do, soul, do; abuse and contemn thyself; yet a while and the time\par
for thee to respect thyself, will be at an end. Every man's happiness\par
depends from himself, but behold thy life is almost at an end, whiles\par
affording thyself no respect, thou dost make thy happiness to consist in\par
the souls, and conceits of other men.\par
\par
IV. Why should any of these things that happen externally, so much\par
distract thee? Give thyself leisure to learn some good thing, and cease\par
roving and wandering to and fro. Thou must also take heed of another\par
kind of wandering, for they are idle in their actions, who toil and\par
labour in this life, and have no certain scope to which to direct all\par
their motions, and desires. V. For not observing the state of another\par
man's soul, scarce was ever any man known to be unhappy. Tell whosoever\par
they be that intend not, and guide not by reason and discretion the\par
motions of their own souls, they must of necessity be unhappy.\par
\par
VI. These things thou must always have in mind: What is the nature\par
of the universe, and what is mine--in particular: This unto that what\par
relation it hath: what kind of part, of what kind of universe it is: And\par
that there is nobody that can hinder thee, but that thou mayest always\par
both do and speak those things which are agreeable to that nature,\par
whereof thou art a part.\par
\par
VII. Theophrastus, where he compares sin with sin (as after a vulgar\par
sense such things I grant may be compared:) says well and like a\par
philosopher, that those sins are greater which are committed through\par
lust, than those which are committed through anger. For he that is angry\par
seems with a kind of grief and close contraction of himself, to turn\par
away from reason; but he that sins through lust, being overcome by\par
pleasure, doth in his very sin bewray a more impotent, and unmanlike\par
disposition. Well then and like a philosopher doth he say, that he of\par
the two is the more to be condemned, that sins with pleasure, than he\par
that sins with grief. For indeed this latter may seem first to have been\par
wronged, and so in some manner through grief thereof to have been forced\par
to be angry, whereas he who through lust doth commit anything, did of\par
himself merely resolve upon that action.\par
\par
VIII. Whatsoever thou dost affect, whatsoever thou dost project, so do,\par
and so project all, as one who, for aught thou knowest, may at this very\par
present depart out of this life. And as for death, if there be any gods,\par
it is no grievous thing to leave the society of men. The gods will do\par
thee no hurt, thou mayest be sure. But if it be so that there be no\par
gods, or that they take no care of the world, why should I desire to\par
live in a world void of gods, and of all divine providence? But gods\par
there be certainly, and they take care for the world; and as for those\par
things which be truly evil, as vice and wickedness, such things they\par
have put in a man's own power, that he might avoid them if he would: and\par
had there been anything besides that had been truly bad and evil, they\par
would have had a care of that also, that a man might have avoided it.\par
But why should that be thought to hurt and prejudice a man's life in\par
this world, which cannot any ways make man himself the better, or the\par
worse in his own person? Neither must we think that the nature of the\par
universe did either through ignorance pass these things, or if not as\par
ignorant of them, yet as unable either to prevent, or better to order\par
and dispose them. It cannot be that she through want either of power or\par
skill, should have committed such a thing, so as to suffer all things\par
both good and bad, equally and promiscuously, to happen unto all both\par
good and bad. As for life therefore, and death, honour and dishonour,\par
labour and pleasure, riches and poverty, all these things happen\par
unto men indeed, both good and bad, equally; but as things which of\par
themselves are neither good nor bad; because of themselves, neither\par
shameful nor praiseworthy.\par
\par
IX. Consider how quickly all things are dissolved and resolved: the\par
bodies and substances themselves, into the matter and substance of the\par
world: and their memories into the general age and time of the world.\par
Consider the nature of all worldly sensible things; of those especially,\par
which either ensnare by pleasure, or for their irksomeness are dreadful,\par
or for their outward lustre and show are in great esteem and request,\par
how vile and contemptible, how base and corruptible, how destitute of\par
all true life and being they are.\par
\par
X. It is the part of a man endowed with a good understanding faculty, to\par
consider what they themselves are in very deed, from whose bare conceits\par
and voices, honour and credit do proceed: as also what it is to die, and\par
how if a man shall consider this by itself alone, to die, and separate\par
from it in his mind all those things which with it usually represent\par
themselves unto us, he can conceive of it no otherwise, than as of a\par
work of nature, and he that fears any work of nature, is a very child.\par
Now death, it is not only a work of nature, but also conducing to\par
nature.\par
\par
XI. Consider with thyself how man, and by what part of his, is joined\par
unto God, and how that part of man is affected, when it is said to be\par
diffused. There is nothing more wretched than that soul, which in a kind\par
of circuit compasseth all things, searching (as he saith) even the very\par
depths of the earth; and by all signs and conjectures prying into the\par
very thoughts of other men's souls; and yet of this, is not sensible,\par
that it is sufficient for a man to apply himself wholly, and to confine\par
all his thoughts and cares to the tendance of that spirit which is\par
within him, and truly and really to serve him. His service doth consist\par
in this, that a man keep himself pure from all violent passion and\par
evil affection, from all rashness and vanity, and from all manner of\par
discontent, either in regard of the gods or men. For indeed whatsoever\par
proceeds from the gods, deserves respect for their worth and excellency;\par
and whatsoever proceeds from men, as they are our kinsmen, should by us\par
be entertained, with love, always; sometimes, as proceeding from their\par
ignorance, of that which is truly good and bad, (a blindness no less,\par
than that by which we are not able to discern between white and black:)\par
with a kind of pity and compassion also.\par
\par
XII. If thou shouldst live three thousand, or as many as ten thousands\par
of years, yet remember this, that man can part with no life properly,\par
save with that little part of life, which he now lives: and that which\par
he lives, is no other, than that which at every instant he parts with.\par
That then which is longest of duration, and that which is shortest, come\par
both to one effect. For although in regard of that which is already past\par
there may be some inequality, yet that time which is now present and\par
in being, is equal unto all men. And that being it which we part with\par
whensoever we die, it doth manifestly appear, that it can be but a\par
moment of time, that we then part with. For as for that which is either\par
past or to come, a man cannot be said properly to part with it. For\par
how should a man part with that which he hath not? These two things\par
therefore thou must remember. First, that all things in the world from\par
all eternity, by a perpetual revolution of the same times and things\par
ever continued and renewed, are of one kind and nature; so that whether\par
for a hundred or two hundred years only, or for an infinite space of\par
time, a man see those things which are still the same, it can be no\par
matter of great moment. And secondly, that that life which any the\par
longest liver, or the shortest liver parts with, is for length and\par
duration the very same, for that only which is present, is that, which\par
either of them can lose, as being that only which they have; for that\par
which he hath not, no man can truly be said to lose.\par
\par
XIII. Remember that all is but opinion and conceit, for those things\par
are plain and apparent, which were spoken unto Monimus the Cynic; and as\par
plain and apparent is the use that may be made of those things, if that\par
which is true and serious in them, be received as well as that which is\par
sweet and pleasing.\par
\par
XIV. A man's soul doth wrong and disrespect itself first and especially,\par
when as much as in itself lies it becomes an aposteme, and as it were an\par
excrescency of the world, for to be grieved and displeased with anything\par
that happens in the world, is direct apostacy from the nature of the\par
universe; part of which, all particular natures of the world, are.\par
Secondly, when she either is averse from any man, or led by contrary\par
desires or affections, tending to his hurt and prejudice; such as are\par
the souls of them that are angry. Thirdly, when she is overcome by any\par
pleasure or pain. Fourthly, when she doth dissemble, and covertly and\par
falsely either doth or saith anything. Fifthly, when she doth either\par
affect or endeavour anything to no certain end, but rashly and without\par
due ratiocination and consideration, how consequent or inconsequent it\par
is to the common end. For even the least things ought not to be done,\par
without relation unto the end; and the end of the reasonable creatures\par
is, to follow and obey him, who is the reason as it were, and the law of\par
this great city, and ancient commonwealth.\par
\par
XV. The time of a man's life is as a point; the substance of it ever\par
flowing, the sense obscure; and the whole composition of the body\par
tending to corruption. His soul is restless, fortune uncertain, and fame\par
doubtful; to be brief, as a stream so are all things belonging to the\par
body; as a dream, or as a smoke, so are all that belong unto the soul.\par
Our life is a warfare, and a mere pilgrimage. Fame after life is no\par
better than oblivion. What is it then that will adhere and follow? Only\par
one thing, philosophy. And philosophy doth consist in this, for a man to\par
preserve that spirit which is within him, from all manner of contumelies\par
and injuries, and above all pains or pleasures; never to do anything\par
either rashly, or feignedly, or hypocritically: wholly to depend from\par
himself and his own proper actions: all things that happen unto him to\par
embrace contentedly, as coming from Him from whom he himself also came;\par
and above all things, with all meekness and a calm cheerfulness, to\par
expect death, as being nothing else but the resolution of those\par
elements, of which every creature is composed. And if the elements\par
themselves suffer nothing by this their perpetual conversion of one into\par
another, that dissolution, and alteration, which is so common unto all,\par
why should it be feared by any? Is not this according to nature? But\par
nothing that is according to nature can be evil.\par
\par
_Whilst I was at Carnuntum._\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
THE THIRD BOOK\par
\par
\par
I. A man must not only consider how daily his life wasteth and\par
decreaseth, but this also, that if he live long, he cannot be certain,\par
whether his understanding shall continue so able and sufficient,\par
for either discreet consideration, in matter of businesses; or for\par
contemplation: it being the thing, whereon true knowledge of things both\par
divine and human, doth depend. For if once he shall begin to dote,\par
his respiration, nutrition, his imaginative, and appetitive, and other\par
natural faculties, may still continue the same: he shall find no want of\par
them. But how to make that right use of himself that he should, how\par
to observe exactly in all things that which is right and just, how to\par
redress and rectify all wrong, or sudden apprehensions and imaginations,\par
and even of this particular, whether he should live any longer or no, to\par
consider duly; for all such things, wherein the best strength and vigour\par
of the mind is most requisite; his power and ability will be past and\par
gone. Thou must hasten therefore; not only because thou art every day\par
nearer unto death than other, but also because that intellective faculty\par
in thee, whereby thou art enabled to know the true nature of things, and\par
to order all thy actions by that knowledge, doth daily waste and decay:\par
or, may fail thee before thou die.\par
\par
II. This also thou must observe, that whatsoever it is that naturally\par
doth happen to things natural, hath somewhat in itself that is pleasing\par
and delightful: as a great loaf when it is baked, some parts of it\par
cleave as it were, and part asunder, and make the crust of it rugged and\par
unequal, and yet those parts of it, though in some sort it be against\par
the art and intention of baking itself, that they are thus cleft and\par
parted, which should have been and were first made all even and uniform,\par
they become it well nevertheless, and have a certain peculiar property,\par
to stir the appetite. So figs are accounted fairest and ripest then,\par
when they begin to shrink, and wither as it were. So ripe olives, when\par
they are next to putrefaction, then are they in their proper beauty. The\par
hanging down of grapes--the brow of a lion, the froth of a foaming wild\par
boar, and many other like things, though by themselves considered, they\par
are far from any beauty, yet because they happen naturally, they both\par
are comely, and delightful; so that if a man shall with a profound mind\par
and apprehension, consider all things in the world, even among all those\par
things which are but mere accessories and natural appendices as it were,\par
there will scarce appear anything unto him, wherein he will not find\par
matter of pleasure and delight. So will he behold with as much pleasure\par
the true _rictus_ of wild beasts, as those which by skilful painters and\par
other artificers are imitated. So will he be able to perceive the proper\par
ripeness and beauty of old age, whether in man or woman: and whatsoever\par
else it is that is beautiful and alluring in whatsoever is, with chaste\par
and continent eyes he will soon find out and discern. Those and many\par
other things will he discern, not credible unto every one, but unto them\par
only who are truly and familiarly acquainted, both with nature itself,\par
and all natural things.\par
\par
III. Hippocrates having cured many sicknesses, fell sick himself and\par
died. The Chaldeans and Astrologians having foretold the deaths of\par
divers, were afterwards themselves surprised by the fates. Alexander and\par
Pompeius, and Caius Cæsar, having destroyed so many towns, and cut\par
off in the field so many thousands both of horse and foot, yet they\par
themselves at last were fain to part with their own lives. Heraclitus\par
having written so many natural tracts concerning the last and general\par
conflagration of the world, died afterwards all filled with water\par
within, and all bedaubed with dirt and dung without. Lice killed\par
Democritus; and Socrates, another sort of vermin, wicked ungodly men.\par
How then stands the case? Thou hast taken ship, thou hast sailed, thou\par
art come to land, go out, if to another life, there also shalt thou find\par
gods, who are everywhere. If all life and sense shall cease, then shalt\par
thou cease also to be subject to either pains or pleasures; and to serve\par
and tend this vile cottage; so much the viler, by how much that which\par
ministers unto it doth excel; the one being a rational substance, and a\par
spirit, the other nothing but earth and blood.\par
\par
IV. Spend not the remnant of thy days in thoughts and fancies concerning\par
other men, when it is not in relation to some common good, when by it\par
thou art hindered from some other better work. That is, spend not thy\par
time in thinking, what such a man doth, and to what end: what he saith,\par
and what he thinks, and what he is about, and such other things or\par
curiosities, which make a man to rove and wander from the care and\par
observation of that part of himself, which is rational, and overruling.\par
See therefore in the whole series and connection of thy thoughts, that\par
thou be careful to prevent whatsoever is idle and impertinent: but\par
especially, whatsoever is curious and malicious: and thou must use\par
thyself to think only of such things, of which if a man upon a sudden\par
should ask thee, what it is that thou art now thinking, thou mayest\par
answer This, and That, freely and boldly, that so by thy thoughts it may\par
presently appear that in all thee is sincere, and peaceable; as becometh\par
one that is made for society, and regards not pleasures, nor gives way\par
to any voluptuous imaginations at all: free from all contentiousness,\par
envy, and suspicion, and from whatsoever else thou wouldest blush to\par
confess thy thoughts were set upon. He that is such, is he surely that\par
doth not put off to lay hold on that which is best indeed, a very priest\par
and minister of the gods, well acquainted and in good correspondence\par
with him especially that is seated and placed within himself, as in\par
a temple and sacrary: to whom also he keeps and preserves himself\par
unspotted by pleasure, undaunted by pain; free from any manner of wrong,\par
or contumely, by himself offered unto himself: not capable of any evil\par
from others: a wrestler of the best sort, and for the highest prize,\par
that he may not be cast down by any passion or affection of his own;\par
deeply dyed and drenched in righteousness, embracing and accepting with\par
his whole heart whatsoever either happeneth or is allotted unto him. One\par
who not often, nor without some great necessity tending to some public\par
good, mindeth what any other, either speaks, or doth, or purposeth: for\par
those things only that are in his own power, or that are truly his own,\par
are the objects of his employments, and his thoughts are ever taken\par
up with those things, which of the whole universe are by the fates or\par
Providence destinated and appropriated unto himself. Those things that\par
are his own, and in his own power, he himself takes order, for that they\par
be good: and as for those that happen unto him, he believes them to be\par
so. For that lot and portion which is assigned to every one, as it is\par
unavoidable and necessary, so is it always profitable. He remembers\par
besides that whatsoever partakes of reason, is akin unto him, and that\par
to care for all men generally, is agreeing to the nature of a man: but\par
as for honour and praise, that they ought not generally to be admitted\par
and accepted of from all, but from such only, who live according to\par
nature. As for them that do not, what manner of men they be at home,\par
or abroad; day or night, how conditioned themselves with what manner of\par
conditions, or with men of what conditions they moil and pass away\par
the time together, he knoweth, and remembers right well, he therefore\par
regards not such praise and approbation, as proceeding from them, who\par
cannot like and approve themselves.\par
\par
V. Do nothing against thy will, nor contrary to the community, nor\par
without due examination, nor with reluctancy. Affect not to set out thy\par
thoughts with curious neat language. Be neither a great talker, nor a\par
great undertaker. Moreover, let thy God that is in thee to rule over\par
thee, find by thee, that he hath to do with a man; an aged man; a\par
sociable man; a Roman; a prince; one that hath ordered his life, as\par
one that expecteth, as it were, nothing but the sound of the trumpet,\par
sounding a retreat to depart out of this life with all expedition. One\par
who for his word or actions neither needs an oath, nor any man to be a\par
witness.\par
\par
VI. To be cheerful, and to stand in no need, either of other men's help\par
or attendance, or of that rest and tranquillity, which thou must be\par
beholding to others for. Rather like one that is straight of himself, or\par
hath ever been straight, than one that hath been rectified.\par
\par
VII. If thou shalt find anything in this mortal life better than\par
righteousness, than truth, temperance, fortitude, and in general better\par
than a mind contented both with those things which according to right\par
and reason she doth, and in those, which without her will and knowledge\par
happen unto thee by the providence; if I say, thou canst find out\par
anything better than this, apply thyself unto it with thy whole heart,\par
and that which is best wheresoever thou dost find it, enjoy freely. But\par
if nothing thou shalt find worthy to be preferred to that spirit which\par
is within thee; if nothing better than to subject unto thee thine own\par
lusts and desires, and not to give way to any fancies or imaginations\par
before thou hast duly considered of them, nothing better than to\par
withdraw thyself (to use Socrates his words) from all sensuality, and\par
submit thyself unto the gods, and to have care of all men in general: if\par
thou shalt find that all other things in comparison of this, are but\par
vile, and of little moment; then give not way to any other thing, which\par
being once though but affected and inclined unto, it will no more be in\par
thy power without all distraction as thou oughtest to prefer and to\par
pursue after that good, which is thine own and thy proper good. For it\par
is not lawful, that anything that is of another and inferior kind and\par
nature, be it what it will, as either popular applause, or honour, or\par
riches, or pleasures; should be suffered to confront and contest as it\par
were, with that which is rational, and operatively good. For all these\par
things, if once though but for a while, they begin to please, they\par
presently prevail, and pervert a man's mind, or turn a man from the\par
right way. Do thou therefore I say absolutely and freely make choice of\par
that which is best, and stick unto it. Now, that they say is best, which\par
is most profitable. If they mean profitable to man as he is a rational\par
man, stand thou to it, and maintain it; but if they mean profitable, as\par
he is a creature, only reject it; and from this thy tenet and conclusion\par
keep off carefully all plausible shows and colours of external\par
appearance, that thou mayest be able to discern things rightly.\par
\par
VIII. Never esteem of anything as profitable, which shall ever constrain\par
thee either to break thy faith, or to lose thy modesty; to hate any man,\par
to suspect, to curse, to dissemble, to lust after anything, that\par
requireth the secret of walls or veils. But he that preferreth before\par
all things his rational part and spirit, and the sacred mysteries of\par
virtue which issueth from it, he shall never lament and exclaim, never\par
sigh; he shall never want either solitude or company: and which is\par
chiefest of all, he shall live without either desire or fear. And as for\par
life, whether for a long or short time he shall enjoy his soul thus\par
compassed about with a body, he is altogether indifferent. For if even\par
now he were to depart, he is as ready for it, as for any other action,\par
which may be performed with modesty and decency. For all his life long,\par
this is his only care, that his mind may always be occupied in such\par
intentions and objects, as are proper to a rational sociable creature.\par
\par
IX. In the mind that is once truly disciplined and purged, thou canst\par
not find anything, either foul or impure, or as it were festered:\par
nothing that is either servile, or affected: no partial tie; no\par
malicious averseness; nothing obnoxious; nothing concealed. The life of\par
such an one, death can never surprise as imperfect; as of an actor, that\par
should die before he had ended, or the play itself were at an end, a man\par
might speak.\par
\par
X. Use thine opinative faculty with all honour and respect, for in\par
her indeed is all: that thy opinion do not beget in thy understanding\par
anything contrary to either nature, or the proper constitution of a\par
rational creature. The end and object of a rational constitution is, to\par
do nothing rashly, to be kindly affected towards men, and in all things\par
willingly to submit unto the gods. Casting therefore all other things\par
aside, keep thyself to these few, and remember withal that no man\par
properly can be said to live more than that which is now present, which\par
is but a moment of time. Whatsoever is besides either is already past,\par
or uncertain. The time therefore that any man doth live, is but a\par
little, and the place where he liveth, is but a very little corner of\par
the earth, and the greatest fame that can remain of a man after his\par
death, even that is but little, and that too, such as it is whilst it\par
is, is by the succession of silly mortal men preserved, who likewise\par
shall shortly die, and even whiles they live know not what in very deed\par
they themselves are: and much less can know one, who long before is dead\par
and gone.\par
\par
XI. To these ever-present helps and mementoes, let one more be added,\par
ever to make a particular description and delineation as it were of\par
every object that presents itself to thy mind, that thou mayest wholly\par
and throughly contemplate it, in its own proper nature, bare and naked;\par
wholly, and severally; divided into its several parts and quarters: and\par
then by thyself in thy mind, to call both it, and those things of which\par
it doth consist, and in which it shall be resolved, by their own proper\par
true names, and appellations. For there is nothing so effectual to beget\par
true magnanimity, as to be able truly and methodically to examine and\par
consider all things that happen in this life, and so to penetrate\par
into their natures, that at the same time, this also may concur in our\par
apprehensions: what is the true use of it? and what is the true nature\par
of this universe, to which it is useful? how much in regard of the\par
universe may it be esteemed? how much in regard of man, a citizen of the\par
supreme city, of which all other cities in the world are as it were but\par
houses and families?\par
\par
XII. What is this, that now my fancy is set upon? of what things doth\par
it consist? how long can it last? which of all the virtues is the proper\par
virtue for this present use? as whether meekness, fortitude, truth,\par
faith, sincerity, contentation, or any of the rest? Of everything\par
therefore thou must use thyself to say, This immediately comes from God,\par
this by that fatal connection, and concatenation of things, or (which\par
almost comes to one) by some coincidental casualty. And as for this, it\par
proceeds from my neighbour, my kinsman, my fellow: through his ignorance\par
indeed, because he knows not what is truly natural unto him: but I know\par
it, and therefore carry myself towards him according to the natural law\par
of fellowship; that is kindly, and justly. As for those things that of\par
themselves are altogether indifferent, as in my best judgment I conceive\par
everything to deserve more or less, so I carry myself towards it.\par
\par
XIII. If thou shalt intend that which is present, following the rule of\par
right and reason carefully, solidly, meekly, and shalt not intermix\par
any other businesses, but shall study this only to preserve thy spirit\par
unpolluted, and pure, and shall cleave unto him without either hope\par
or fear of anything, in all things that thou shalt either do or speak,\par
contenting thyself with heroical truth, thou shalt live happily; and\par
from this, there is no man that can hinder thee.\par
\par
XIV. As physicians and chirurgeons have always their instruments ready\par
at hand for all sudden cures; so have thou always thy dogmata in a\par
readiness for the knowledge of things, both divine and human: and\par
whatsoever thou dost, even in the smallest things that thou dost, thou\par
must ever remember that mutual relation, and connection that is between\par
these two things divine, and things human. For without relation unto\par
God, thou shalt never speed in any worldly actions; nor on the other\par
side in any divine, without some respect had to things human.\par
\par
XV. Be not deceived; for thou shalt never live to read thy moral\par
commentaries, nor the acts of the famous Romans and Grecians; nor those\par
excerpta from several books; all which thou hadst provided and laid\par
up for thyself against thine old age. Hasten therefore to an end, and\par
giving over all vain hopes, help thyself in time if thou carest for\par
thyself, as thou oughtest to do.\par
\par
XVI. To steal, to sow, to buy, to be at rest, to see what is to be done\par
(which is not seen by the eyes, but by another kind of sight:) what\par
these words mean, and how many ways to be understood, they do not\par
understand. The body, the soul, the understanding. As the senses\par
naturally belong to the body, and the desires and affections to the\par
soul, so do the dogmata to the understanding.\par
\par
XVII. To be capable of fancies and imaginations, is common to man and\par
beast. To be violently drawn and moved by the lusts and desires of the\par
soul, is proper to wild beasts and monsters, such as Phalaris and Nero\par
were. To follow reason for ordinary duties and actions is common to them\par
also, who believe not that there be any gods, and for their advantage\par
would make no conscience to betray their own country; and who when once\par
the doors be shut upon them, dare do anything. If therefore all things\par
else be common to these likewise, it follows, that for a man to like and\par
embrace all things that happen and are destinated unto him, and not to\par
trouble and molest that spirit which is seated in the temple of his own\par
breast, with a multitude of vain fancies and imaginations, but to keep\par
him propitious and to obey him as a god, never either speaking anything\par
contrary to truth, or doing anything contrary to justice, is the only\par
true property of a good man. And such a one, though no man should\par
believe that he liveth as he doth, either sincerely and conscionably,\par
or cheerful and contentedly; yet is he neither with any man at all angry\par
for it, nor diverted by it from the way that leadeth to the end of his\par
life, through which a man must pass pure, ever ready to depart, and\par
willing of himself without any compulsion to fit and accommodate himself\par
to his proper lot and portion.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
THE FOURTH BOOK\par
\par
\par
I. That inward mistress part of man if it be in its own true natural\par
temper, is towards all worldly chances and events ever so disposed and\par
affected, that it will easily turn and apply itself to that which may\par
be, and is within its own power to compass, when that cannot be which at\par
first it intended. For it never doth absolutely addict and apply itself\par
to any one object, but whatsoever it is that it doth now intend and\par
prosecute, it doth prosecute it with exception and reservation; so that\par
whatsoever it is that falls out contrary to its first intentions, even\par
that afterwards it makes its proper object. Even as the fire when it\par
prevails upon those things that are in his way; by which things indeed a\par
little fire would have been quenched, but a great fire doth soon turn to\par
its own nature, and so consume whatsoever comes in his way: yea by those\par
very things it is made greater and greater.\par
\par
II. Let nothing be done rashly, and at random, but all things according\par
to the most exact and perfect rules of art.\par
\par
III. They seek for themselves private retiring\par
places, as country villages, the sea-shore, mountains; yea thou thyself\par
art wont to long much after such places. But all this thou must know\par
proceeds from simplicity in the highest degree. At what time soever thou\par
wilt, it is in thy power to retire into thyself, and to be at rest, and\par
free from all businesses. A man cannot any whither retire better than\par
to his own soul; he especially who is beforehand provided of such\par
things within, which whensoever he doth withdraw himself to look in, may\par
presently afford unto him perfect ease and tranquillity. By tranquillity\par
I understand a decent orderly disposition and carriage, free from\par
all confusion and tumultuousness. Afford then thyself this retiring\par
continually, and thereby refresh and renew thyself. Let these precepts\par
be brief and fundamental, which as soon as thou dost call them to mind,\par
may suffice thee to purge thy soul throughly, and to send thee away well\par
pleased with those things whatsoever they be, which now again after this\par
short withdrawing of thy soul into herself thou dost return unto. For\par
what is it that thou art offended at? Can it be at the wickedness of\par
men, when thou dost call to mind this conclusion, that all reasonable\par
creatures are made one for another? and that it is part of justice to\par
bear with them? and that it is against their wills that they offend?\par
and how many already, who once likewise prosecuted their enmities,\par
suspected, hated, and fiercely contended, are now long ago stretched\par
out, and reduced unto ashes? It is time for thee to make an end. As for\par
those things which among the common chances of the world happen unto\par
thee as thy particular lot and portion, canst thou be displeased with\par
any of them, when thou dost call that our ordinary dilemma to mind,\par
either a providence, or Democritus his atoms; and with it, whatsoever we\par
brought to prove that the whole world is as it were one city? And as for\par
thy body, what canst thou fear, if thou dost consider that thy mind and\par
understanding, when once it hath recollected itself, and knows its own\par
power, hath in this life and breath (whether it run smoothly and gently,\par
or whether harshly and rudely), no interest at all, but is altogether\par
indifferent: and whatsoever else thou hast heard and assented unto\par
concerning either pain or pleasure? But the care of thine honour and\par
reputation will perchance distract thee? How can that be, if thou\par
dost look back, and consider both how quickly all things that are, are\par
forgotten, and what an immense chaos of eternity was before, and will\par
follow after all things: and the vanity of praise, and the inconstancy\par
and variableness of human judgments and opinions, and the narrowness of\par
the place, wherein it is limited and circumscribed? For the whole earth\par
is but as one point; and of it, this inhabited part of it, is but a very\par
little part; and of this part, how many in number, and what manner of\par
men are they, that will commend thee? What remains then, but that thou\par
often put in practice this kind of retiring of thyself, to this little\par
part of thyself; and above all things, keep thyself from distraction,\par
and intend not anything vehemently, but be free and consider all things,\par
as a man whose proper object is Virtue, as a man whose true nature is\par
to be kind and sociable, as a citizen, as a mortal creature. Among\par
other things, which to consider, and look into thou must use to withdraw\par
thyself, let those two be among the most obvious and at hand. One, that\par
the things or objects themselves reach not unto the soul, but stand\par
without still and quiet, and that it is from the opinion only which is\par
within, that all the tumult and all the trouble doth proceed. The next,\par
that all these things, which now thou seest, shall within a very little\par
while be changed, and be no more: and ever call to mind, how many\par
changes and alterations in the world thou thyself hast already been an\par
eyewitness of in thy time. This world is mere change, and this life,\par
opinion.\par
\par
IV. If to understand and to be reasonable be common unto all men, then\par
is that reason, for which we are termed reasonable, common unto all. If\par
reason is general, then is that reason also, which prescribeth what is\par
to be done and what not, common unto all. If that, then law. If law,\par
then are we fellow-citizens. If so, then are we partners in some one\par
commonweal. If so, then the world is as it were a city. For which other\par
commonweal is it, that all men can be said to be members of? From this\par
common city it is, that understanding, reason, and law is derived unto\par
us, for from whence else? For as that which in me is earthly I have from\par
some common earth; and that which is moist from some other element is\par
imparted; as my breath and life hath its proper fountain; and that\par
likewise which is dry and fiery in me: (for there is nothing which doth\par
not proceed from something; as also there is nothing that can be reduced\par
unto mere nothing:) so also is there some common beginning from whence\par
my understanding hath proceeded.\par
\par
V. As generation is, so also death, a secret of nature's wisdom: a\par
mixture of elements, resolved into the same elements again, a thing\par
surely which no man ought to be ashamed of: in a series of other fatal\par
events and consequences, which a rational creature is subject unto,\par
not improper or incongruous, nor contrary to the natural and proper\par
constitution of man himself.\par
\par
VI. Such and such things, from such and such causes, must of necessity\par
proceed. He that would not have such things to happen, is as he that\par
would have the fig-tree grow without any sap or moisture. In sum,\par
remember this, that within a very little while, both thou and he shall\par
both be dead, and after a little while more, not so much as your names\par
and memories shall be remaining.\par
\par
VII. Let opinion be taken away, and no man will think himself wronged.\par
If no man shall think himself wronged, then is there no more any such\par
thing as wrong. That which makes not man himself the worse, cannot\par
make his life the worse, neither can it hurt him either inwardly\par
or outwardly. It was expedient in nature that it should be so, and\par
therefore necessary.\par
\par
VIII. Whatsoever doth happen in the world, doth happen justly, and so if\par
thou dost well take heed, thou shalt find it. I say not only in right\par
order by a series of inevitable consequences, but according to justice\par
and as it were by way of equal distribution, according to the true worth\par
of everything. Continue then to take notice of it, as thou hast begun,\par
and whatsoever thou dost, do it not without this proviso, that it be a\par
thing of that nature that a good man (as the word good is properly\par
taken) may do it. This observe carefully in every action.\par
\par
IX. Conceit no such things, as he that wrongeth thee conceiveth,\par
or would have thee to conceive, but look into the matter itself, and see\par
what it is in very truth.\par
\par
X. These two rules, thou must have always in a readiness. First, do\par
nothing at all, but what reason proceeding from that regal and supreme\par
part, shall for the good and benefit of men, suggest unto thee. And\par
secondly, if any man that is present shall be able to rectify thee or to\par
turn thee from some erroneous persuasion, that thou be always ready to\par
change thy mind, and this change to proceed, not from any respect of any\par
pleasure or credit thereon depending, but always from some probable\par
apparent ground of justice, or of some public good thereby to be\par
furthered; or from some other such inducement.\par
\par
XI. Hast thou reason? I have. Why then makest thou not use of it? For if\par
thy reason do her part, what more canst thou require?\par
\par
XII. As a part hitherto thou hast had a particular subsistence: and now\par
shalt thou vanish away into the common substance of Him, who first begot\par
thee, or rather thou shalt be resumed again into that original rational\par
substance, out of which all others have issued, and are propagated.\par
Many small pieces of frankincense are set upon the same altar, one drops\par
first and is consumed, another after; and it comes all to one.\par
\par
XIII. Within ten days, if so happen, thou shalt be esteemed a god of\par
them, who now if thou shalt return to the dogmata and to the honouring\par
of reason, will esteem of thee no better than of a mere brute, and of an\par
ape.\par
\par
XIV. Not as though thou hadst thousands of years to live. Death hangs\par
over thee: whilst yet thou livest, whilst thou mayest, be good.\par
\par
XV. Now much time and leisure doth he gain, who is not curious to know\par
what his neighbour hath said, or hath done, or hath attempted, but only\par
what he doth himself, that it may be just and holy? or to express it in\par
Agathos' words, Not to look about upon the evil conditions of others,\par
but to run on straight in the line, without any loose and extravagant\par
agitation.\par
\par
XVI. He who is greedy of credit and reputation after his death, doth\par
not consider, that they themselves by whom he is remembered, shall soon\par
after every one of them be dead; and they likewise that succeed those;\par
until at last all memory, which hitherto by the succession of men\par
admiring and soon after dying hath had its course, be quite extinct.\par
But suppose that both they that shall remember thee, and thy memory\par
with them should be immortal, what is that to thee? I will not say to\par
thee after thou art dead; but even to thee living, what is thy praise?\par
But only for a secret and politic consideration, which we call\par
οἰκονομίαν, or dispensation. For as for that, that it is the gift of\par
nature, whatsoever is commended in thee, what might be objected from\par
thence, let that now that we are upon another consideration be omitted\par
as unseasonable. That which is fair and goodly, whatsoever it be, and\par
in what respect soever it be, that it is fair and goodly, it is so of\par
itself, and terminates in itself, not admitting praise as a part or\par
member: that therefore which is praised, is not thereby made either\par
better or worse. This I understand even of those things, that are\par
commonly called fair and good, as those which are commended either for\par
the matter itself, or for curious workmanship. As for that which is\par
truly good, what can it stand in need of more than either justice or\par
truth; or more than either kindness and modesty? Which of all those,\par
either becomes good or fair, because commended; or dispraised suffers\par
any damage? Doth the emerald become worse in itself, or more vile if it\par
be not commended? Doth gold, or ivory, or purple? Is there anything\par
that doth though never so common, as a knife, a flower, or a tree?\par
\par
XVII. If so be that the souls remain after death (say they that will not\par
believe it); how is the air from all eternity able to contain them? How\par
is the earth (say I) ever from that time able to Contain the bodies\par
of them that are buried? For as here the change and resolution of dead\par
bodies into another kind of subsistence (whatsoever it be;) makes place\par
for other dead bodies: so the souls after death transferred into the\par
air, after they have conversed there a while, are either by way of\par
transmutation, or transfusion, or conflagration, received again into\par
that original rational substance, from which all others do proceed:\par
and so give way to those souls, who before coupled and associated unto\par
bodies, now begin to subsist single. This, upon a supposition that the\par
souls after death do for a while subsist single, may be answered. And\par
here, (besides the number of bodies, so buried and contained by the\par
earth), we may further consider the number of several beasts, eaten\par
by us men, and by other creatures. For notwithstanding that such a\par
multitude of them is daily consumed, and as it were buried in the bodies\par
of the eaters, yet is the same place and body able to contain them, by\par
reason of their conversion, partly into blood, partly into air and fire.\par
What in these things is the speculation of truth? to divide things into\par
that which is passive and material; and that which is active and formal.\par
\par
XVIII. Not to wander out of the way, but upon every motion and desire,\par
to perform that which is just: and ever to be careful to attain to the\par
true natural apprehension of every fancy, that presents itself.\par
\par
XIX. Whatsoever is expedient unto thee, O World, is expedient unto me;\par
nothing can either be 'unseasonable unto me, or out of date, which unto\par
thee is seasonable. Whatsoever thy seasons bear, shall ever by me be\par
esteemed as happy fruit, and increase. O Nature! from thee are all\par
things, in thee all things subsist, and to thee all tend. Could he say\par
of Athens, Thou lovely city of Cecrops; and shalt not thou say of the\par
world, Thou lovely city of God?\par
\par
XX. They will say commonly, Meddle not with many things, if thou wilt\par
live cheerfully. Certainly there is nothing better, than for a man\par
to confine himself to necessary actions; to such and so many only, as\par
reason in a creature that knows itself born for society, will command\par
and enjoin. This will not only procure that cheerfulness, which from the\par
goodness, but that also, which from the paucity of actions doth usually\par
proceed. For since it is so, that most of those things, which we either\par
speak or do, are unnecessary; if a man shall cut them off, it must needs\par
follow that he shall thereby gain much leisure, and save much trouble,\par
and therefore at every action a man must privately by way of admonition\par
suggest unto himself, What? may not this that now I go about, be of the\par
number of unnecessary actions? Neither must he use himself to cut off\par
actions only, but thoughts and imaginations also, that are unnecessary\par
for so will unnecessary consequent actions the better be prevented and\par
cut off.\par
\par
XXI. Try also how a good man's life; (of one, who is well pleased with\par
those things whatsoever, which among the common changes and chances of\par
this world fall to his own lot and share; and can live well contented\par
and fully satisfied in the justice of his own proper present action,\par
and in the goodness of his disposition for the future:) will agree with\par
thee. Thou hast had experience of that other kind of life: make now\par
trial of this also. Trouble not thyself any more henceforth, reduce\par
thyself unto perfect simplicity. Doth any man offend? It is against\par
himself that he doth offend: why should it trouble thee? Hath anything\par
happened unto thee? It is well, whatsoever it be, it is that which\par
of all the common chances of the world from the very beginning in the\par
series of all other things that have, or shall happen, was destinated\par
and appointed unto thee. To comprehend all in a few words, our life is\par
short; we must endeavour to gain the present time with best discretion\par
and justice. Use recreation with sobriety.\par
\par
XXII. Either this world is a κόσμος or comely piece, because all\par
disposed and governed by certain order: or if it be a mixture, though\par
confused, yet still it is a comely piece. For is it possible that in\par
thee there should be any beauty at all, and that in the whole world\par
there should be nothing but disorder and confusion? and all things in it\par
too, by natural different properties one from another differenced and\par
distinguished; and yet all through diffused, and by natural sympathy,\par
one to another united, as they are?\par
\par
XXIII. A black or malign disposition, an effeminate disposition; an\par
hard inexorable disposition, a wild inhuman disposition, a sheepish\par
disposition, a childish disposition; a blockish, a false, a scurril, a\par
fraudulent, a tyrannical: what then? If he be a stranger in the world,\par
that knows not the things that are in it; why not be a stranger as well,\par
that wonders at the things that are done in it?\par
\par
XXIV. He is a true fugitive, that flies from reason, by which men are\par
sociable. He blind, who cannot see with the eyes of his understanding.\par
He poor, that stands in need of another, and hath not in himself all\par
things needful for this life. He an aposteme of the world, who by being\par
discontented with those things that happen unto him in the world,\par
doth as it were apostatise, and separate himself from common nature's\par
rational administration. For the same nature it is that brings this\par
unto thee, whatsoever it be, that first brought thee into the world. He\par
raises sedition in the city, who by irrational actions withdraws his own\par
soul from that one and common soul of all rational creatures.\par
\par
XXV. There is, who without so much as a coat; and there is, who without\par
so much as a book, doth put philosophy in practice. I am half naked,\par
neither have I bread to eat, and yet I depart not from reason, saith\par
one. But I say; I want the food of good teaching, and instructions, and\par
yet I depart not from reason.\par
\par
XXVI. What art and profession soever thou hast learned, endeavour to\par
affect it, and comfort thyself in it; and pass the remainder of thy life\par
as one who from his whole heart commits himself and whatsoever belongs\par
unto him, unto the gods: and as for men, carry not thyself either\par
tyrannically or servilely towards any.\par
\par
XXVII. Consider in my mind, for example's sake, the times of Vespasian:\par
thou shalt see but the same things: some marrying, some bringing up\par
children, some sick, some dying, some fighting, some feasting, some\par
merchandising, some tilling, some flattering, some boasting, some\par
suspecting, some undermining, some wishing to die, some fretting and\par
murmuring at their present estate, some wooing, some hoarding, some\par
seeking after magistracies, and some after kingdoms. And is not that\par
their age quite over, and ended? Again, consider now the times of\par
Trajan. There likewise thou seest the very self-same things, and that\par
age also is now over and ended. In the like manner consider other\par
periods, both of times and of whole nations, and see how many men, after\par
they had with all their might and main intended and prosecuted some one\par
worldly thing or other did soon after drop away, and were resolved into\par
the elements. But especially thou must call to mind them, whom thou\par
thyself in thy lifetime hast known much distracted about vain things,\par
and in the meantime neglecting to do that, and closely and unseparably\par
(as fully satisfied with it) to adhere unto it, which their own proper\par
constitution did require. And here thou must remember, that thy carriage\par
in every business must be according to the worth and due proportion of\par
it, for so shalt thou not easily be tired out and vexed, if thou shalt\par
not dwell upon small matters longer than is fitting.\par
\par
XXVIII. Those words which once were common and ordinary, are now become\par
obscure and obsolete; and so the names of men once commonly known and\par
famous, are now become in a manner obscure and obsolete names. Camillus,\par
Cæso, Volesius, Leonnatus; not long after, Scipio, Cato, then Augustus,\par
then Adrianus, then Antoninus Pius: all these in a short time will\par
be out of date, and, as things of another world as it were, become\par
fabulous. And this I say of them, who once shined as the wonders of\par
their ages, for as for the rest, no sooner are they expired, than with\par
them all their fame and memory. And what is it then that shall always be\par
remembered? all is vanity. What is it that we must bestow our care and\par
diligence upon? even upon this only: that our minds and wills be just;\par
that our actions be charitable; that our speech be never deceitful, or\par
that our understanding be not subject to error; that our inclination be\par
always set to embrace whatsoever shall happen unto us, as necessary,\par
as usual, as ordinary, as flowing from such a beginning, and such a\par
fountain, from which both thou thyself and all things are.\par
Willingly therefore, and wholly surrender up thyself unto that fatal\par
concatenation, yielding up thyself unto the fates, to be disposed of at\par
their pleasure.\par
\par
XXIX. Whatsoever is now present, and from day to day hath its existence;\par
all objects of memories, and the minds and memories themselves,\par
incessantly consider, all things that are, have their being by change\par
and alteration. Use thyself therefore often to meditate upon this, that\par
the nature of the universe delights in nothing more, than in altering\par
those things that are, and in making others like unto them. So that we\par
may say, that whatsoever is, is but as it were the seed of that which\par
shall be. For if thou think that that only is seed, which either the\par
earth or the womb receiveth, thou art very simple.\par
\par
XXX. Thou art now ready to die, and yet hast thou not attained to\par
that perfect simplicity: thou art yet subject to many troubles and\par
perturbations; not yet free from all fear and suspicion of external\par
accidents; nor yet either so meekly disposed towards all men, as thou\par
shouldest; or so affected as one, whose only study and only wisdom is,\par
to be just in all his actions.\par
\par
XXXI. Behold and observe, what is the state of their rational part; and\par
those that the world doth account wise, see what things they fly and are\par
afraid of; and what things they hunt after.\par
\par
XXXII. In another man's mind and understanding thy evil Cannot subsist,\par
nor in any proper temper or distemper of the natural constitution of thy\par
body, which is but as it were the coat or cottage of thy soul. Wherein\par
then, but in that part of thee, wherein the conceit, and apprehension\par
of any misery can subsist? Let not that part therefore admit any such\par
conceit, and then all is well. Though thy body which is so near it\par
should either be cut or burnt, or suffer any corruption or putrefaction,\par
yet let that part to which it belongs to judge of these, be still at\par
rest; that is, let her judge this, that whatsoever it is, that equally\par
may happen to a wicked man, and to a good man, is neither good nor evil.\par
For that which happens equally to him that lives according to nature,\par
and to him that doth not, is neither according to nature, nor against\par
it; and by consequent, neither good nor bad.\par
\par
XXXIII. Ever consider and think upon the world as being but one living\par
substance, and having but one soul, and how all things in the world, are\par
terminated into one sensitive power; and are done by one general motion\par
as it were, and deliberation of that one soul; and how all things that\par
are, concur in the cause of one another's being, and by what manner of\par
connection and concatenation all things happen.\par
\par
XXXIV. What art thou, that better and divine part excepted, but as\par
Epictetus said well, a wretched soul, appointed to carry a carcass up\par
and down?\par
\par
XXXV. To suffer change can be no hurt; as no benefit it is, by change to\par
attain to being. The age and time of the world is as it were a flood and\par
swift current, consisting of the things that are brought to pass in\par
the world. For as soon as anything hath appeared, and is passed away,\par
another succeeds, and that also will presently out of sight.\par
\par
XXXVI. Whatsoever doth happen in the world, is, in the course of nature,\par
as usual and ordinary as a rose in the spring, and fruit in summer. Of\par
the same nature is sickness and death; slander, and lying in wait, and\par
whatsoever else ordinarily doth unto fools use to be occasion either\par
of joy or sorrow. That, whatsoever it is, that comes after, doth always\par
very naturally, and as it were familiarly, follow upon that which was\par
before. For thou must consider the things of the world, not as a loose\par
independent number, consisting merely of necessary events; but as a\par
discreet connection of things orderly and harmoniously disposed. There\par
is then to be seen in the things of the world, not a bare succession,\par
but an admirable correspondence and affinity.\par
\par
XXXVII. Let that of Heraclitus never be out of thy mind, that the death\par
of earth, is water, and the death of water, is air; and the death of\par
air, is fire; and so on the contrary. Remember him also who was\par
ignorant whither the way did lead, and how that reason being the thing\par
by which all things in the world are administered, and which men are\par
continually and most inwardly conversant with: yet is the thing, which\par
ordinarily they are most in opposition with, and how those things which\par
daily happen among them, cease not daily to be strange unto them, and\par
that we should not either speak, or do anything as men in their sleep,\par
by opinion and bare imagination: for then we think we speak and do, and\par
that we must not be as children, who follow their father's example; for\par
best reason alleging their bare καθότι παρειλήφαμεν; or, as by\par
successive tradition from our forefathers we have received it.\par
\par
XXXVIII. Even as if any of the gods should tell thee, Thou shalt\par
certainly die to-morrow, or next day, thou wouldst not, except thou wert\par
extremely base and pusillanimous, take it for a great benefit, rather\par
to die the next day after, than to-morrow; (for alas, what is the\par
difference!) so, for the same reason, think it no great matter to die\par
rather many years after, than the very next day.\par
\par
XXXIX. Let it be thy perpetual meditation, how many physicians who\par
once looked so grim, and so theatrically shrunk their brows upon their\par
patients, are dead and gone themselves. How many astrologers, after that\par
in great ostentation they had foretold the death of some others, how\par
many philosophers after so many elaborate tracts and volumes concerning\par
either mortality or immortality; how many brave captains and commanders,\par
after the death and slaughter of so many; how many kings and tyrants,\par
after they had with such horror and insolency abused their power upon\par
men's lives, as though themselves had been immortal; how many, that\par
I may so speak, whole cities both men and towns: Helice, Pompeii,\par
Herculaneum, and others innumerable are dead and gone. Run them over\par
also, whom thou thyself, one after another, hast known in thy time\par
to drop away. Such and such a one took care of such and such a one's\par
burial, and soon after was buried himself. So one, so another: and all\par
things in a short time. For herein lieth all indeed, ever to look upon\par
all worldly things, as things for their continuance, that are but for a\par
day: and for their worth, most vile, and contemptible, as for example,\par
What is man? That which but the other day when he was conceived was vile\par
snivel; and within few days shall be either an embalmed carcass, or mere\par
ashes. Thus must thou according to truth and nature, throughly consider\par
how man's life is but for a very moment of time, and so depart meek and\par
contented: even as if a ripe olive falling should praise the ground that\par
bare her, and give thanks to the tree that begat her.\par
\par
XL. Thou must be like a promontory of the sea, against which though\par
the waves beat continually, yet it both itself stands, and about it are\par
those swelling waves stilled and quieted.\par
\par
XLI. Oh, wretched I, to whom this mischance is happened! nay, happy I,\par
to whom this thing being happened, I can continue without grief; neither\par
wounded by that which is present, nor in fear of that which is to come.\par
For as for this, it might have happened unto any man, but any man having\par
such a thing befallen him, could not have continued without grief. Why\par
then should that rather be an unhappiness, than this a happiness? But\par
however, canst thou, O man! term that unhappiness, which is no mischance\par
to the nature of man I Canst thou think that a mischance to the nature\par
of man, which is not contrary to the end and will of his nature? What\par
then hast thou learned is the will of man's nature? Doth that then which\par
hath happened unto thee, hinder thee from being just? or magnanimous? or\par
temperate? or wise? or circumspect? or true? or modest? or free? or from\par
anything else of all those things in the present enjoying and possession\par
whereof the nature of man, (as then enjoying all that is proper unto\par
her,) is fully satisfied? Now to conclude; upon all occasion of sorrow\par
remember henceforth to make use of this dogma, that whatsoever it is\par
that hath happened unto thee, is in very deed no such thing of itself,\par
as a misfortune; but that to bear it generously, is certainly great\par
happiness.\par
\par
XLII. It is but an ordinary coarse one, yet it is a good effectual\par
remedy against the fear of death, for a man to consider in his mind the\par
examples of such, who greedily and covetously (as it were) did for a\par
long time enjoy their lives. What have they got more, than they whose\par
deaths have been untimely? Are not they themselves dead at the last?\par
as Cadiciant's, Fabius, Julianus Lepidus, or any other who in their\par
lifetime having buried many, were at the last buried themselves. The\par
whole space of any man's life, is but little; and as little as it is,\par
with what troubles, with what manner of dispositions, and in the society\par
of how wretched a body must it be passed! Let it be therefore unto thee\par
altogether as a matter of indifferency. For if thou shalt look backward;\par
behold, what an infinite chaos of time doth present itself unto thee;\par
and as infinite a chaos, if thou shalt look forward. In that which is\par
so infinite, what difference can there be between that which liveth but\par
three days, and that which liveth three ages?\par
\par
XLIII. Let thy course ever be the most compendious way. The most\par
compendious, is that which is according to nature: that is, in all both\par
words and deeds, ever to follow that which is most sound and perfect.\par
For such a resolution will free a man from all trouble, strife,\par
dissembling, and ostentation.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
THE FIFTH BOOK\par
\par
\par
I. In the morning when thou findest thyself unwilling to rise, consider\par
with thyself presently, it is to go about a man's work that I am stirred\par
up. Am I then yet unwilling to go about that, for which I myself was\par
born and brought forth into this world? Or was I made for this, to\par
lay me down, and make much of myself in a warm bed? 'O but this is\par
pleasing.' And was it then for this that thou wert born, that thou\par
mightest enjoy pleasure? Was it not in very truth for this, that thou\par
mightest always be busy and in action? Seest thou not how all things\par
in the world besides, how every tree md plant, how sparrows and ants,\par
spiders and bees: how all in their kind are intent as it were orderly to\par
perform whatsoever (towards the preservation of this orderly universe)\par
naturally doth become and belong unto thin? And wilt not thou do that,\par
which belongs unto a man to do? Wilt not thou run to do that, which thy\par
nature doth require? 'But thou must have some rest.' Yes, thou must.\par
Nature hath of that also, as well as of eating and drinking, allowed\par
thee a certain stint. But thou guest beyond thy stint, and beyond that\par
which would suffice, and in matter of action, there thou comest short of\par
that which thou mayest. It must needs be therefore, that thou dost not\par
love thyself, for if thou didst, thou wouldst also love thy nature, and\par
that which thy nature doth propose unto herself as her end. Others,\par
as many as take pleasure in their trade and profession, can even pine\par
themselves at their works, and neglect their bodies and their food for\par
it; and doest thou less honour thy nature, than an ordinary mechanic\par
his trade; or a good dancer his art? than a covetous man his silver, and\par
vainglorious man applause? These to whatsoever they take an affection,\par
can be content to want their meat and sleep, to further that every one\par
which he affects: and shall actions tending to the common good of\par
human society, seem more vile unto thee, or worthy of less respect and\par
intention?\par
\par
II. How easy a thing is it for a man to put off from him all turbulent\par
adventitious imaginations, and presently to be in perfect rest and\par
tranquillity!\par
\par
III. Think thyself fit and worthy to speak, or to do anything that is\par
according to nature, and let not the reproach, or report of some that\par
may ensue upon it, ever deter thee. If it be right and honest to be\par
spoken or done, undervalue not thyself so much, as to be discouraged\par
from it. As for them, they have their own rational over-ruling part, and\par
their own proper inclination: which thou must not stand and look\par
about to take notice of, but go on straight, whither both thine own\par
particular, and the common nature do lead thee; and the way of both\par
these, is but one.\par
\par
IV. I continue my course by actions according to nature, until I\par
fall and cease, breathing out my last breath into that air, by which\par
continually breathed in I did live; and falling upon that earth, out of\par
whose gifts and fruits my father gathered his seed, my mother her\par
blood, and my nurse her milk, out of which for so many years I have\par
been provided, both of meat and drink. And lastly, which beareth me that\par
tread upon it, and beareth with me that so many ways do abuse it, or\par
so freely make use of it, so many ways to so many ends.\par
\par
V. No man can admire thee for thy sharp acute language, such is thy\par
natural disability that way. Be it so: yet there be many other good\par
things, for the want of which thou canst not plead the want or natural\par
ability. Let them be seen in thee, which depend wholly from thee;\par
sincerity, gravity, laboriousness, contempt of pleasures; be not\par
querulous, be Content with little, be kind, be free; avoid all\par
superfluity, all vain prattling; be magnanimous. Doest not thou\par
perceive, how many things there be, which notwithstanding any pretence\par
of natural indisposition and unfitness, thou mightest have performed and\par
exhibited, and yet still thou doest voluntarily continue drooping\par
downwards? Or wilt thou say that it is through defect of thy natural\par
constitution, that thou art constrained to murmur, to be base and\par
wretched to flatter; now to accuse, and now to please, and pacify thy\par
body: to be vainglorious, to be so giddy-headed., and unsettled in thy\par
thoughts? nay (witnesses be the Gods) of all these thou mightest have\par
been rid long ago: only, this thou must have been contented with, to\par
have borne the blame of one that is somewhat slow and dull, wherein thou\par
must so exercise thyself, as one who neither doth much take to heart\par
this his natural defect, nor yet pleaseth himself in it.\par
\par
VI. Such there be, who when they have done a good turn to any, are ready\par
to set them on the score for it, and to require retaliation. Others\par
there be, who though they stand not upon retaliation, to require any,\par
yet they think with themselves nevertheless, that such a one is their\par
debtor, and they know as their word is what they have done. Others again\par
there be, who when they have done any such thing, do not so much as\par
know what they have done; but are like unto the vine, which beareth her\par
grapes, and when once she hath borne her own proper fruit, is contented\par
and seeks for no further recompense. As a horse after a race, and a\par
hunting dog when he hath hunted, and a bee when she hath made her honey,\par
look not for applause and commendation; so neither doth that man that\par
rightly doth understand his own nature when he hath done a good turn:\par
but from one doth proceed to do another, even as the vine after she hath\par
once borne fruit in her own proper season, is ready for another time.\par
Thou therefore must be one of them, who what they do, barely do it\par
without any further thought, and are in a manner insensible of what they\par
do. 'Nay but,' will some reply perchance, 'this very thing a rational\par
man is bound unto, to understand what it is, that he doeth.' For it\par
is the property, say they, of one that is naturally sociable, to be\par
sensible, that he doth operate sociably: nay, and to desire, that the\par
party him self that is sociably dealt with, should be sensible of it\par
too. I answer, That which thou sayest is true indeed, but the true\par
meaning of that which is said, thou dost not understand. And therefore\par
art thou one of those first, whom I mentioned. For they also are led by\par
a probable appearance of reason. But if thou dost desire to understand\par
truly what it is that is said, fear not that thou shalt therefore give\par
over any sociable action.\par
\par
VII. The form of the Athenians' prayer did run thus: 'O rain, rain, good\par
Jupiter, upon all the grounds and fields that belong to the Athenians.'\par
Either we should not pray at all, or thus absolutely and freely; and not\par
every one for himself in particular alone.\par
\par
VIII. As we say commonly, The physician hath prescribed unto this man,\par
riding; unto another, cold baths; unto a third, to go barefoot: so it\par
is alike to say, The nature of the universe hath prescribed unto this\par
man sickness, or blindness, or some loss, or damage or some such thing.\par
For as there, when we say of a physician, that he hath prescribed\par
anything, our meaning is, that he hath appointed this for that, as\par
subordinate and conducing to health: so here, whatsoever doth happen\par
unto any, is ordained unto him as a thing subordinate unto the fates,\par
and therefore do we say of such things, that they do συμβαίνειν, that\par
is, happen, or fall together; as of square stones, when either in\par
walls, or pyramids in a certain position they fit one another, and\par
agree as it were in an harmony, the masons say, that they do\par
συμβαίνειν; as if thou shouldest say, fall together: so that in the\par
general, though the things be divers that make it, yet the consent or\par
harmony itself is but one. And as the whole world is made up of all the\par
particular bodies of the world, one perfect and complete body, of the\par
same nature that particular bodies; so is the destiny of particular\par
causes and events one general one, of the same nature that particular\par
causes are. What I now say, even they that are mere idiots are not\par
ignorant of: for they say commonly τοῦτο ἔφερεν ἀυτῷ, that is, This\par
his destiny hath brought upon him. This therefore is by the fates\par
properly and particularly brought upon this, as that unto this in\par
particular is by the physician prescribed. These therefore let us\par
accept of in like manner, as we do those that are prescribed unto us\par
our physicians. For them also in themselves shall We find to contain\par
many harsh things, but we nevertheless, in hope of health, and\par
recovery, accept of them. Let the fulfilling and accomplishment of\par
those things which the common nature hath determined, be unto thee as\par
thy health. Accept then, and be pleased with whatsoever doth happen,\par
though otherwise harsh and un-pleasing, as tending to that end, to the\par
health and welfare of the universe, and to Jove's happiness and\par
prosperity. For this whatsoever it be, should not have been produced,\par
had it not conduced to the good of the universe. For neither doth any\par
ordinary particular nature bring anything to pass, that is not to\par
whatsoever is within the sphere of its own proper administration and\par
government agreeable and subordinate. For these two considerations then\par
thou must be well pleased with anything that doth happen unto thee.\par
First, because that for thee properly it was brought to pass, and unto\par
thee it was prescribed; and that from the very beginning by the series\par
and connection of the first causes, it hath ever had a reference unto\par
thee. And secondly, because the good success and perfect welfare, and\par
indeed the very continuance of Him, that is the Administrator of the\par
whole, doth in a manner depend on it. For the whole (because whole,\par
therefore entire and perfect) is maimed, and mutilated, if thou shalt\par
cut off anything at all, whereby the coherence, and contiguity as of\par
parts, so of causes, is maintained and preserved. Of which certain it\par
is, that thou doest (as much as lieth in thee) cut off, and in some\par
sort violently take somewhat away, as often as thou art displeased with\par
anything that happeneth.\par
\par
IX. Be not discontented, be not disheartened, be not out of hope, if\par
often it succeed not so well with thee punctually and precisely to do\par
all things according to the right dogmata, but being once cast off,\par
return unto them again: and as for those many and more frequent\par
occurrences, either of worldly distractions, or human infirmities, which\par
as a man thou canst not but in some measure be subject unto, be not thou\par
discontented with them; but however, love and affect that only which\par
thou dust return unto: a philosopher's life, and proper occupation after\par
the most exact manner. And when thou dust return to thy philosophy,\par
return not unto it as the manner of some is, after play and liberty as\par
it were, to their schoolmasters and pedagogues; but as they that have\par
sore eyes to their sponge and egg: or as another to his cataplasm; or\par
as others to their fomentations: so shalt not thou make it a matter of\par
ostentation at all to obey reason but of ease and comfort. And\par
remember that philosophy requireth nothing of thee, but what thy\par
nature requireth, and wouldest thou thyself desire anything that is\par
not according to nature? for which of these sayest thou; that which is\par
according to nature or against it, is of itself more kind and pleasing?\par
Is it not for that respect especially, that pleasure itself is to so\par
many men's hurt and overthrow, most prevalent, because esteemed commonly\par
most kind, and natural? But consider well whether magnanimity rather,\par
and true liberty, and true simplicity, and equanimity, and holiness;\par
whether these be not most kind and natural? And prudency itself, what\par
more kind and amiable than it, when thou shalt truly consider with\par
thyself, what it is through all the proper objects of thy rational\par
intellectual faculty currently to go on without any fall or stumble?\par
As for the things of the world, their true nature is in a manner so\par
involved with obscurity, that unto many philosophers, and those no\par
mean ones, they seemed altogether incomprehensible, and the Stoics\par
themselves, though they judge them not altogether incomprehensible,\par
yet scarce and not without much difficulty, comprehensible, so that\par
all assent of ours is fallible, for who is he that is infallible in his\par
conclusions? From the nature of things, pass now unto their subjects\par
and matter: how temporary, how vile are they I such as may be in the\par
power and possession of some abominable loose liver, of some common\par
strumpet, of some notorious oppressor and extortioner. Pass from thence\par
to the dispositions of them that thou doest ordinarily converse with,\par
how hardly do we bear, even with the most loving and amiable! that I may\par
not say, how hard it is for us to bear even with our own selves, in such\par
obscurity, and impurity of things: in such and so continual a flux both\par
of the substances and time; both of the motions themselves, and things\par
moved; what it is that we can fasten upon; either to honour, and respect\par
especially; or seriously, and studiously to seek after; I cannot so much\par
as conceive For indeed they are things contrary.\par
\par
X. Thou must comfort thyself in the expectation of thy natural\par
dissolution, and in the meantime not grieve at the delay; but rest\par
contented in those two things. First, that nothing shall happen unto\par
thee, which is not according to the nature of the universe. Secondly,\par
that it is in thy power, to do nothing against thine own proper God, and\par
inward spirit. For it is not in any man's power to constrain thee to\par
transgress against him.\par
\par
XI. What is the use that now at this present I make of my soul? Thus\par
from time to time and upon all occasions thou must put this question to\par
thyself; what is now that part of mine which they call the rational\par
mistress part, employed about? Whose soul do I now properly possess? a\par
child's? or a youth's? a woman's? or a tyrant's? some brute, or some\par
wild beast's soul?\par
\par
XII. What those things are in themselves, which by the greatest part are\par
esteemed good, thou mayest gather even from this. For if a man shall\par
hear things mentioned as good, which are really good indeed, such as are\par
prudence, temperance, justice, fortitude, after so much heard and\par
conceived, he cannot endure to hear of any more, for the word good is\par
properly spoken of them. But as for those which by the vulgar are\par
esteemed good, if he shall hear them mentioned as good, he doth hearken\par
for more. He is well contented to hear, that what is spoken by the\par
comedian, is but familiarly and popularly spoken, so that even the\par
vulgar apprehend the difference. For why is it else, that this offends\par
not and needs not to be excused, when virtues are styled good: but that\par
which is spoken in commendation of wealth, pleasure, or honour, we\par
entertain it only as merrily and pleasantly spoken? Proceed therefore,\par
and inquire further, whether it may not be that those things also which\par
being mentioned upon the stage were merrily, and with great applause of\par
the multitude, scoffed at with this jest, that they that possessed them\par
had not in all the world of their own, (such was their affluence and\par
plenty) so much as a place where to avoid their excrements. Whether, I\par
say, those ought not also in very deed to be much respected, and\par
esteemed of, as the only things that are truly good.\par
\par
XIII. All that I consist of, is either form or matter. No corruption\par
can reduce either of these unto nothing: for neither did I of nothing\par
become a subsistent creature. Every part of mine then will by mutation\par
be disposed into a certain part of the whole world, and that in time\par
into another part; and so _in infinitum;_ by which kind of mutation, I\par
also became what I am, and so did they that begot me, and they before\par
them, and so upwards _in infinitum_. For so we may be allowed to speak,\par
though the age and government of the world, be to some certain periods\par
of time limited, and confined.\par
\par
XIV. Reason, and rational power, are faculties which content themselves\par
with themselves, and their own proper operations. And as for their first\par
inclination and motion, that they take from themselves. But their\par
progress is right to the end and object, which is in their way, as it\par
were, and lieth just before them: that is, which is feasible and\par
possible, whether it be that which at the first they proposed to\par
themselves, or no. For which reason also such actions are termed\par
κατορθώσεις, to intimate the directness of the way, by which they are\par
achieved. Nothing must be thought to belong to a man, which doth not\par
belong unto him as he is a man. These, the event of purposes, are not\par
things required in a man. The nature of man doth not profess any such\par
things. The final ends and consummations of actions are nothing at all\par
to a man's nature. The end therefore of a man, or the _summum bonum_\par
whereby that end is fulfilled, cannot consist in the consummation of\par
actions purposed and intended. Again, concerning these outward worldly\par
things, were it so that any of them did properly belong unto man, then\par
would it not belong unto man, to condemn them and to stand in opposition\par
with them. Neither would he be praiseworthy that can live without them;\par
or he good, (if these were good indeed) who of his own accord doth\par
deprive himself of any of them. But we see contrariwise, that the more a\par
man doth withdraw himself from these wherein external pomp and greatness\par
doth consist, or any other like these; or the better he doth bear with\par
the loss of these, the better he is accounted.\par
\par
XV. Such as thy thoughts and ordinary cogitations are, such will thy\par
mind be in time. For the soul doth as it were receive its tincture from\par
the fancies, and imaginations. Dye it therefore and thoroughly soak it\par
with the assiduity of these cogitations. As for example. Wheresoever\par
thou mayest live, there it is in thy power to live well and happy. But\par
thou mayest live at the Court, there then also mayest thou live well and\par
happy. Again, that which everything is made for, he is also made unto\par
that, and cannot but naturally incline unto it. That which anything\par
doth naturally incline unto, therein is his end. Wherein the end of\par
everything doth consist, therein also doth his good and benefit consist.\par
Society therefore is the proper good of a rational creature. For that we\par
are made for society, it hath long since been demonstrated. Or can any\par
man make any question of this, that whatsoever is naturally worse and\par
inferior, is ordinarily subordinated to that which is better? and that\par
those things that are best, are made one for another? And those things\par
that have souls, are better than those that have none? and of those that\par
have, those best that have rational souls?\par
\par
XVI. To desire things impossible is the part of a mad man. But it is a\par
thing impossible, that wicked man should not commit some such things.\par
Neither doth anything happen to any man, which in the ordinary course\par
of nature as natural unto him doth not happen. Again, the same things\par
happen unto others also. And truly, if either he that is ignorant that\par
such a thing hath happened unto him, or he that is ambitious to be\par
commended for his magnanimity, can be patient, and is not grieved: is it\par
not a grievous thing, that either ignorance, or a vain desire to please\par
and to be commended, should be more powerful and effectual than true\par
prudence? As for the things themselves, they touch not the soul, neither\par
can they have any access unto it: neither can they of themselves any\par
ways either affect it, or move it. For she herself alone can affect and\par
move herself, and according as the dogmata and opinions are, which she\par
doth vouchsafe herself; so are those things which, as accessories, have\par
any co-existence with her.\par
\par
XVII. After one consideration, man is nearest unto us; as we are bound\par
to do them good, and to bear with them. But as he may oppose any of our\par
true proper actions, so man is unto me but as a thing indifferent: even\par
as the sun, or the wind, or some wild beast. By some of these it may be,\par
that some operation or other of mine, may be hindered; however, of my\par
mind and resolution itself, there can be no let or impediment, by reason\par
of that ordinary constant both exception (or reservation wherewith it\par
inclineth) and ready conversion of objects; from that which may not be,\par
to that which may be, which in the prosecution of its inclinations, as\par
occasion serves, it doth observe. For by these the mind doth turn and\par
convert any impediment whatsoever, to be her aim and purpose. So that\par
what before was the impediment, is now the principal object of her\par
working; and that which before was in her way, is now her readiest way.\par
\par
XVIII. Honour that which is chiefest and most powerful in the world, and\par
that is it, which makes use of all things, and governs all things. So\par
also in thyself; honour that which is chiefest, and most powerful; and\par
is of one kind and nature with that which we now spake of. For it is the\par
very same, which being in thee, turneth all other things to its own use,\par
and by whom also thy life is governed.\par
\par
XIX. That which doth not hurt the city itself; cannot hurt any citizen.\par
This rule thou must remember to apply and make use of upon every conceit\par
and apprehension of wrong. If the whole city be not hurt by this,\par
neither am I certainly. And if the whole be not, why should I make it\par
my private grievance? consider rather what it is wherein he is overseen\par
that is thought to have done the wrong. Again, often meditate how\par
swiftly all things that subsist, and all things that are done in the\par
world, are carried away, and as it were conveyed out of sight: for both\par
the substance themselves, we see as a flood, are in a continual flux;\par
and all actions in a perpetual change; and the causes themselves,\par
subject to a thousand alterations, neither is there anything almost,\par
that may ever be said to be now settled and constant. Next unto this,\par
and which follows upon it, consider both the infiniteness of the time\par
already past, and the immense vastness of that which is to come, wherein\par
all things are to be resolved and annihilated. Art not thou then a\par
very fool, who for these things, art either puffed up with pride, or\par
distracted with cares, or canst find in thy heart to make such moans as\par
for a thing that would trouble thee for a very long time? Consider the\par
whole universe whereof thou art but a very little part, and the whole\par
age of the world together, whereof but a short and very momentary\par
portion is allotted unto thee, and all the fates and destinies together,\par
of which how much is it that comes to thy part and share! Again: another\par
doth trespass against me. Let him look to that. He is master of his own\par
disposition, and of his own operation. I for my part am in the meantime\par
in possession of as much, as the common nature would have me to possess:\par
and that which mine own nature would have me do, I do.\par
\par
XX. Let not that chief commanding part of thy soul be ever subject to\par
any variation through any corporal either pain or pleasure, neither\par
suffer it to be mixed with these, but let it both circumscribe itself,\par
and confine those affections to their own proper parts and members.\par
But if at any time they do reflect and rebound upon the mind and\par
understanding (as in an united and compacted body it must needs;) then\par
must thou not go about to resist sense and feeling, it being natural.\par
However let not thy understanding to this natural sense and feeling,\par
which whether unto our flesh pleasant or painful, is unto us nothing\par
properly, add an opinion of either good or bad and all is well.\par
\par
XXI. To live with the Gods. He liveth with the Gods, who at all times\par
affords unto them the spectacle of a soul, both contented and well\par
pleased with whatsoever is afforded, or allotted unto her; and\par
performing whatsoever is pleasing to that Spirit, whom (being part of\par
himself) Jove hath appointed to every man as his overseer and governor.\par
\par
XXII. Be not angry neither with him whose breath, neither with him whose\par
arm holes, are offensive. What can he do? such is his breath naturally,\par
and such are his arm holes; and from such, such an effect, and such\par
a smell must of necessity proceed. 'O, but the man (sayest thou) hath\par
understanding in him, and might of himself know, that he by standing\par
near, cannot choose but offend.' And thou also (God bless thee!) hast\par
understanding. Let thy reasonable faculty, work upon his reasonable\par
faculty; show him his fault, admonish him. If he hearken unto thee, thou\par
hast cured him, and there will be no more occasion of anger.\par
\par
XXIII. 'Where there shall neither roarer be, nor harlot.' Why so? As\par
thou dost purpose to live, when thou hast retired thyself to some such\par
place, where neither roarer nor harlot is: so mayest thou here. And if\par
they will not suffer thee, then mayest thou leave thy life rather than\par
thy calling, but so as one that doth not think himself anyways wronged.\par
Only as one would say, Here is a smoke; I will out of it. And what a\par
great matter is this! Now till some such thing force me out, I will\par
continue free; neither shall any man hinder me to do what I will, and\par
my will shall ever be by the proper nature of a reasonable and sociable\par
creature, regulated and directed.\par
\par
XXIV. That rational essence by which the universe is governed, is for\par
community and society; and therefore hath it both made the things that\par
are worse, for the best, and hath allied and knit together those\par
which are best, as it were in an harmony. Seest thou not how it hath\par
sub-ordinated, and co-ordinated? and how it hath distributed unto\par
everything according to its worth? and those which have the pre-eminency\par
and superiority above all, hath it united together, into a mutual\par
consent and agreement.\par
\par
XXV. How hast thou carried thyself hitherto towards the Gods? towards\par
thy parents? towards thy brethren? towards thy wife? towards thy\par
children? towards thy masters? thy foster-fathers? thy friends? thy\par
domestics? thy servants? Is it so with thee, that hitherto thou hast\par
neither by word or deed wronged any of them? Remember withal through how\par
many things thou hast already passed, and how many thou hast been able\par
to endure; so that now the legend of thy life is full, and thy charge is\par
accomplished. Again, how many truly good things have certainly by thee\par
been discerned? how many pleasures, how many pains hast thou passed over\par
with contempt? how many things eternally glorious hast thou despised?\par
towards how many perverse unreasonable men hast thou carried thyself\par
kindly, and discreetly?\par
\par
XXVI. Why should imprudent unlearned souls trouble that which is\par
both learned, and prudent? And which is that that is so? she that\par
understandeth the beginning and the end, and hath the true knowledge of\par
that rational essence, that passeth through all things subsisting, and\par
through all ages being ever the same, disposing and dispensing as it\par
were this universe by certain periods of time.\par
\par
XXVII. Within a very little while, thou wilt be either ashes, or a\par
sceletum; and a name perchance; and perchance, not so much as a name.\par
And what is that but an empty sound, and a rebounding echo? Those things\par
which in this life are dearest unto us, and of most account, they are in\par
themselves but vain, putrid, contemptible. The most weighty and serious,\par
if rightly esteemed, but as puppies, biting one another: or untoward\par
children, now laughing and then crying. As for faith, and modesty, and\par
justice, and truth, they long since, as one of the poets hath it, have\par
abandoned this spacious earth, and retired themselves unto heaven. What\par
is it then that doth keep thee here, if things sensible be so mutable\par
and unsettled? and the senses so obscure, and so fallible? and our souls\par
nothing but an exhalation of blood? and to be in credit among such,\par
be but vanity? What is it that thou dost stay for? an extinction, or a\par
translation; either of them with a propitious and contented mind. But\par
still that time come, what will content thee? what else, but to worship\par
and praise the Gods; and to do good unto men. To bear with them, and\par
to forbear to do them any wrong. And for all external things belonging\par
either to this thy wretched body, or life, to remember that they are\par
neither thine, nor in thy power.\par
\par
XXVIII. Thou mayest always speed, if thou wilt but make choice of the\par
right way; if in the course both of thine opinions and actions, thou\par
wilt observe a true method. These two things be common to the souls, as\par
of God, so of men, and of every reasonable creature, first that in their\par
own proper work they cannot be hindered by anything: and secondly, that\par
their happiness doth consist in a disposition to, and in the practice of\par
righteousness; and that in these their desire is terminated.\par
\par
XXIX. If this neither be my wicked act, nor an act anyways depending\par
from any wickedness of mine, and that by it the public is not hurt; what\par
doth it concern me? And wherein can the public be hurt? For thou must\par
not altogether be carried by conceit and common opinion: as for help\par
thou must afford that unto them after thy best ability, and as occasion\par
shall require, though they sustain damage, but in these middle or\par
worldly things; but however do not thou conceive that they are truly\par
hurt thereby: for that is not right. But as that old foster-father\par
in the comedy, being now to take his leave doth with a great deal of\par
ceremony, require his foster-child's rhombus, or rattle-top, remembering\par
nevertheless that it is but a rhombus; so here also do thou likewise.\par
For indeed what is all this pleading and public bawling for at the\par
courts? O man, hast thou forgotten what those things are! yea but they\par
are things that others much care for, and highly esteem of. Wilt thou\par
therefore be a fool too? Once I was; let that suffice.\par
\par
XXX. Let death surprise me when it will, and where it will, I may be\par
εὔμοιρος, or a happy man, nevertheless.\par
\par
For he is a happy man, who in his lifetime dealeth unto himself a happy\par
lot and portion. A happy lot and portion is, good inclinations of the\par
soul, good desires, good actions.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
THE SIXTH BOOK\par
\par
\par
I. The matter itself, of which the universe doth consist, is of itself\par
very tractable and pliable. That rational essence that doth govern it,\par
hath in itself no cause to do evil. It hath no evil in itself; neither\par
can it do anything that is evil: neither can anything be hurt by it. And\par
all things are done and determined according to its will and prescript.\par
\par
II. Be it all one unto thee, whether half frozen or well warm; whether\par
only slumbering, or after a full sleep; whether discommended or\par
commended thou do thy duty: or whether dying or doing somewhat else; for\par
that also 'to die,' must among the rest be reckoned as one of the duties\par
and actions of our lives.\par
\par
III. Look in, let not either the proper quality, or the true worth of\par
anything pass thee, before thou hast fully apprehended it.\par
\par
IV. All substances come soon to their change, and either they shall\par
be resolved by way of exhalation (if so be that all things shall be\par
reunited into one substance), or as others maintain, they shall be\par
scattered and dispersed. As for that Rational Essence by which all\par
things are governed, as it best understandeth itself, both its own\par
disposition, and what it doth, and what matter it hath to do with and\par
accordingly doth all things; so we that do not, no wonder, if we wonder\par
at many things, the reasons whereof we cannot comprehend.\par
\par
V. The best kind of revenge is, not to become like unto them.\par
\par
VI. Let this be thy only joy, and thy only comfort, from one sociable\par
kind action without intermission to pass unto another, God being ever in\par
thy mind.\par
\par
VII. The rational commanding part, as it alone can stir up and turn\par
itself; so it maketh both itself to be, and everything that happeneth,\par
to appear unto itself, as it will itself.\par
\par
VIII. According to the nature of the universe all things particular are\par
determined, not according to any other nature, either about compassing\par
and containing; or within, dispersed and contained; or without,\par
depending. Either this universe is a mere confused mass, and an\par
intricate context of things, which shall in time be scattered and\par
dispersed again: or it is an union consisting of order, and administered\par
by Providence. If the first, why should I desire to continue any longer\par
in this fortuit confusion and commixtion? or why should I take care for\par
anything else, but that as soon as may be I may be earth again? And\par
why should I trouble myself any more whilst I seek to please the Gods?\par
Whatsoever I do, dispersion is my end, and will come upon me whether I\par
will or no. But if the latter be, then am not I religious in vain;\par
then will I be quiet and patient, and put my trust in Him, who is the\par
Governor of all.\par
\par
IX. Whensoever by some present hard occurrences thou art constrained to\par
be in some sort troubled and vexed, return unto thyself as soon as may\par
be, and be not out of tune longer than thou must needs. For so shalt\par
thou be the better able to keep thy part another time, and to maintain\par
the harmony, if thou dost use thyself to this continually; once out,\par
presently to have recourse unto it, and to begin again.\par
\par
X. If it were that thou hadst at one time both a stepmother, and\par
a natural mother living, thou wouldst honour and respect her also;\par
nevertheless to thine own natural mother would thy refuge, and recourse\par
be continually. So let the court and thy philosophy be unto thee. Have\par
recourse unto it often, and comfort thyself in her, by whom it is that\par
those other things are made tolerable unto thee, and thou also in those\par
things not intolerable unto others.\par
\par
XI. How marvellous useful it is for a man to represent unto himself\par
meats, and all such things that are for the mouth, under a right\par
apprehension and imagination! as for example: This is the carcass of a\par
fish; this of a bird; and this of a hog. And again more generally; This\par
phalernum, this excellent highly commended wine, is but the bare juice\par
of an ordinary grape. This purple robe, but sheep's hairs, dyed with\par
the blood of a shellfish. So for coitus, it is but the attrition of an\par
ordinary base entrail, and the excretion of a little vile snivel, with\par
a certain kind of convulsion: according to Hippocrates his opinion. How\par
excellent useful are these lively fancies and representations of things,\par
thus penetrating and passing through the objects, to make their true\par
nature known and apparent! This must thou use all thy life long, and\par
upon all occasions: and then especially, when matters are apprehended\par
as of great worth and respect, thy art and care must be to uncover\par
them, and to behold their vileness, and to take away from them all those\par
serious circumstances and expressions, under which they made so grave\par
a show. For outward pomp and appearance is a great juggler; and then\par
especially art thou most in danger to be beguiled by it, when (to\par
a man's thinking) thou most seemest to be employed about matters of\par
moment.\par
\par
XII. See what Crates pronounceth concerning Xenocrates himself.\par
\par
XIII. Those things which the common sort of people do admire, are most\par
of them such things as are very general, and may be comprehended under\par
things merely natural, or naturally affected and qualified: as stones,\par
wood, figs, vines, olives. Those that be admired by them that are more\par
moderate and restrained, are comprehended under things animated: as\par
flocks and herds. Those that are yet more gentle and curious, their\par
admiration is commonly confined to reasonable creatures only; not in\par
general as they are reasonable, but as they are capable of art, or of\par
some craft and subtile invention: or perchance barely to reasonable\par
creatures; as they that delight in the possession of many slaves. But\par
he that honours a reasonable soul in general, as it is reasonable and\par
naturally sociable, doth little regard anything else: and above all\par
things is careful to preserve his own, in the continual habit and\par
exercise both of reason and sociableness: and thereby doth co-operate\par
with him, of whose nature he doth also participate; God.\par
\par
XIV. Some things hasten to be, and others to be no more. And even\par
whatsoever now is, some part thereof hath already perished. Perpetual\par
fluxes and alterations renew the world, as the perpetual course of time\par
doth make the age of the world (of itself infinite) to appear always\par
fresh and new. In such a flux and course of all things, what of these\par
things that hasten so fast away should any man regard, since among all\par
there is not any that a man may fasten and fix upon? as if a man would\par
settle his affection upon some ordinary sparrow living by him, who is no\par
sooner seen, than out of sight. For we must not think otherwise of our\par
lives, than as a mere exhalation of blood, or of an ordinary respiration\par
of air. For what in our common apprehension is, to breathe in the air\par
and to breathe it out again, which we do daily: so much is it and no\par
more, at once to breathe out all thy respirative faculty into that\par
common air from whence but lately (as being but from yesterday, and\par
to-day), thou didst first breathe it in, and with it, life.\par
\par
XV. Not vegetative spiration, it is not surely (which plants have) that\par
in this life should be so dear unto us; nor sensitive respiration, the\par
proper life of beasts, both tame and wild; nor this our imaginative\par
faculty; nor that we are subject to be led and carried up and down by\par
the strength of our sensual appetites; or that we can gather, and live\par
together; or that we can feed: for that in effect is no better, than\par
that we can void the excrements of our food. What is it then that should\par
be dear unto us? to hear a clattering noise? if not that, then neither\par
to be applauded by the tongues of men. For the praises of many tongues,\par
is in effect no better than the clattering of so many tongues. If then\par
neither applause, what is there remaining that should be dear unto thee?\par
This I think: that in all thy motions and actions thou be moved,\par
and restrained according to thine own true natural constitution and\par
Construction only. And to this even ordinary arts and professions do\par
lead us. For it is that which every art doth aim at, that whatsoever it\par
is, that is by art effected and prepared, may be fit for that work that\par
it is prepared for. This is the end that he that dresseth the vine, and\par
he that takes upon him either to tame colts, or to train up dogs,\par
doth aim at. What else doth the education of children, and all learned\par
professions tend unto? Certainly then it is that, which should be dear\par
unto us also. If in this particular it go well with thee, care not for\par
the obtaining of other things. But is it so, that thou canst not but\par
respect other things also? Then canst not thou truly be free? then canst\par
thou not have self-content: then wilt thou ever be subject to passions.\par
For it is not possible, but that thou must be envious, and jealous, and\par
suspicious of them whom thou knowest can bereave thee of such things;\par
and again, a secret underminer of them, whom thou seest in present\par
possession of that which is dear unto thee. To be short, he must of\par
necessity be full of confusion within himself, and often accuse the\par
Gods, whosoever stands in need of these things. But if thou shalt\par
honour and respect thy mind only, that will make thee acceptable\par
towards thyself, towards thy friends very tractable; and conformable\par
and concordant with the Gods; that is, accepting with praises whatsoever\par
they shall think good to appoint and allot unto thee.\par
\par
XVI. Under, above, and about, are the motions of the elements; but\par
the motion of virtue, is none of those motions, but is somewhat more\par
excellent and divine. Whose way (to speed and prosper in it) must be\par
through a way, that is not easily comprehended.\par
\par
XVII. Who can choose but wonder at them? They will not speak well of\par
them that are at the same time with them, and live with them; yet they\par
themselves are very ambitious, that they that shall follow, whom they\par
have never seen, nor shall ever see, should speak well of them. As if\par
a man should grieve that he hath not been commended by them, that lived\par
before him.\par
\par
XVIII. Do not ever conceive anything impossible to man, which by thee\par
cannot, or not without much difficulty be effected; but whatsoever in\par
general thou canst Conceive possible and proper unto any man, think that\par
very possible unto thee also.\par
\par
XIX. Suppose that at the palestra somebody hath all to-torn thee with\par
his nails, and hath broken thy head. Well, thou art wounded. Yet thou\par
dost not exclaim; thou art not offended with him. Thou dost not suspect\par
him for it afterwards, as one that watcheth to do thee a mischief. Yea\par
even then, though thou dost thy best to save thyself from him, yet not\par
from him as an enemy. It is not by way of any suspicious indignation,\par
but by way of gentle and friendly declination. Keep the same mind and\par
disposition in other parts of thy life also. For many things there be,\par
which we must conceit and apprehend, as though we had had to do with an\par
antagonist at the palestra. For as I said, it is very possible for us to\par
avoid and decline, though we neither suspect, nor hate.\par
\par
XX. If anybody shall reprove me, and shall make it apparent unto me,\par
that in any either opinion or action of mine I do err, I will most\par
gladly retract. For it is the truth that I seek after, by which I am\par
sure that never any man was hurt; and as sure, that he is hurt that\par
continueth in any error, or ignorance whatsoever.\par
\par
XXI. I for my part will do what belongs unto me; as for other things,\par
whether things unsensible or things irrational; or if rational, yet\par
deceived and ignorant of the true way, they shall not trouble or\par
distract me. For as for those creatures which are not endued with reason\par
and all other things and-matters of the world whatsoever I freely, and\par
generously, as one endued with reason, of things that have none, make\par
use of them. And as for men, towards them as naturally partakers of the\par
same reason, my care is to carry myself sociably. But whatsoever it is\par
that thou art about, remember to call upon the Gods. And as for the time\par
how long thou shalt live to do these things, let it be altogether\par
indifferent unto thee, for even three such hours are sufficient.\par
\par
XXII. Alexander of Macedon, and he that dressed his mules, when once\par
dead both came to one. For either they were both resumed into those\par
original rational essences from whence all things in the world are\par
propagated; or both after one fashion were scattered into atoms.\par
\par
XXIII Consider how many different things, whether they concern our\par
bodies, or our souls, in a moment of time come to pass in every one of\par
us, and so thou wilt not wonder if many more things or rather all things\par
that are done, can at one time subsist, and coexist in that both one and\par
general, which we call the world.\par
\par
XXIV. if any should put this question unto thee, how this word Antoninus\par
is written, wouldst thou not presently fix thine intention upon it, and\par
utter out in order every letter of it? And if any shall begin to gainsay\par
thee, and quarrel with thee about it; wilt thou quarrel with him again,\par
or rather go on meekly as thou hast begun, until thou hast numbered out\par
every letter? Here then likewise remember, that every duty that belongs\par
unto a man doth consist of some certain letters or numbers as it were,\par
to which without any noise or tumult keeping thyself thou must orderly\par
proceed to thy proposed end, forbearing to quarrel with him that would\par
quarrel and fall out with thee.\par
\par
XXV. Is it not a cruel thing to forbid men to affect those things, which\par
they conceive to agree best with their own natures, and to tend most\par
to their own proper good and behoof? But thou after a sort deniest them\par
this liberty, as often as thou art angry with them for their sins. For\par
surely they are led unto those sins whatsoever they be, as to\par
their proper good and commodity. But it is not so (thou wilt object\par
perchance). Thou therefore teach them better, and make it appear unto\par
them: but be not thou angry with them.\par
\par
XXVI. Death is a cessation from the impression of the senses, the\par
tyranny of the passions, the errors of the mind, and the servitude of\par
the body.\par
\par
XXVII. If in this kind of life thy body be able to hold out, it is a\par
shame that thy soul should faint first, and give over, take heed, lest\par
of a philosopher thou become a mere Cæsar in time, and receive a new\par
tincture from the court. For it may happen if thou dost not take heed.\par
Keep thyself therefore, truly simple, good, sincere, grave, free\par
from all ostentation, a lover of that which is just, religious, kind,\par
tender-hearted, strong and vigorous to undergo anything that becomes\par
thee. Endeavour to continue such, as philosophy (hadst thou wholly and\par
constantly applied thyself unto it) would have made, and secured thee.\par
Worship the Gods, procure the welfare of men, this life is short.\par
Charitable actions, and a holy disposition, is the only fruit of this\par
earthly life.\par
\par
XXVIII. Do all things as becometh the disciple of Antoninus Pius.\par
Remember his resolute constancy in things that were done by him\par
according to reason, his equability in all things, his sanctity; the\par
cheerfulness of his countenance, his sweetness, and how free he was from\par
all vainglory; how careful to come to the true and exact knowledge of\par
matters in hand, and how he would by no means give over till he did\par
fully, and plainly understand the whole state of the business; and how\par
patiently, and without any contestation he would bear with them, that\par
did unjustly condemn him: how he would never be over-hasty in anything,\par
nor give ear to slanders and false accusations, but examine and observe\par
with best diligence the several actions and dispositions of men. Again,\par
how he was no backbiter, nor easily frightened, nor suspicious, and in\par
his language free from all affectation and curiosity: and how easily he\par
would content himself with few things, as lodging, bedding, clothing,\par
and ordinary nourishment, and attendance. How able to endure labour, how\par
patient; able through his spare diet to continue from morning to evening\par
without any necessity of withdrawing before his accustomed hours to\par
the necessities of nature: his uniformity and constancy in matter of\par
friendship. How he would bear with them that with all boldness and\par
liberty opposed his opinions; and even rejoice if any man could better\par
advise him: and lastly, how religious he was without superstition. All\par
these things of him remember, that whensoever thy last hour shall\par
come upon thee, it may find thee, as it did him, ready for it in the\par
possession of a good conscience.\par
\par
XXIX. Stir up thy mind, and recall thy wits again from thy natural\par
dreams, and visions, and when thou art perfectly awoken, and canst\par
perceive that they were but dreams that troubled thee, as one newly\par
awakened out of another kind of sleep look upon these worldly things\par
with the same mind as thou didst upon those, that thou sawest in thy\par
sleep.\par
\par
XXX. I consist of body and soul. Unto my body all things are\par
indifferent, for of itself it cannot affect one thing more than another\par
with apprehension of any difference; as for my mind, all things which\par
are not within the verge of her own operation, are indifferent unto her,\par
and for her own operations, those altogether depend of her; neither\par
does she busy herself about any, but those that are present; for as\par
for future and past operations, those also are now at this present\par
indifferent unto her.\par
\par
XXXI. As long as the foot doth that which belongeth unto it to do, and\par
the hand that which belongs unto it, their labour, whatsoever it be, is\par
not unnatural. So a man as long as he doth that which is proper unto\par
a man, his labour cannot be against nature; and if it be not against\par
nature, then neither is it hurtful unto him. But if it were so that\par
happiness did consist in pleasure: how came notorious robbers, impure\par
abominable livers, parricides, and tyrants, in so large a measure to\par
have their part of pleasures?\par
\par
XXXII. Dost thou not see, how even those that profess mechanic arts,\par
though in some respect they be no better than mere idiots, yet they\par
stick close to the course of their trade, neither can they find in\par
their heart to decline from it: and is it not a grievous thing that\par
an architect, or a physician shall respect the course and mysteries of\par
their profession, more than a man the proper course and condition of his\par
own nature, reason, which is common to him and to the Gods?\par
\par
XXXIII. Asia, Europe; what are they, but as corners of the whole world;\par
of which the whole sea, is but as one drop; and the great Mount Athos,\par
but as a clod, as all present time is but as one point of eternity. All,\par
petty things; all things that are soon altered, soon perished. And all\par
things come from one beginning; either all severally and particularly\par
deliberated and resolved upon, by the general ruler and governor of all;\par
or all by necessary consequence. So that the dreadful hiatus of a gaping\par
lion, and all poison, and all hurtful things, are but (as the thorn and\par
the mire) the necessary consequences of goodly fair things. Think not\par
of these therefore, as things contrary to those which thou dost much\par
honour, and respect; but consider in thy mind the true fountain of all.\par
\par
XXXIV He that seeth the things that are now, hath Seen all that either\par
was ever, or ever shall be, for all things are of one kind; and all like\par
one unto another. Meditate often upon the connection of all things in\par
the world; and upon the mutual relation that they have one unto another.\par
For all things are after a sort folded and involved one within another,\par
and by these means all agree well together. For one thing is consequent\par
unto another, by local motion, by natural conspiration and agreement,\par
and by substantial union, or, reduction of all substances into one.\par
\par
XXXV. Fit and accommodate thyself to that estate and to those\par
occurrences, which by the destinies have been annexed unto thee; and\par
love those men whom thy fate it is to live with; but love them truly. An\par
instrument, a tool, an utensil, whatsoever it be, if it be fit for the\par
purpose it was made for, it is as it should be though he perchance that\par
made and fitted it, be out of sight and gone. But in things natural,\par
that power which hath framed and fitted them, is and abideth within them\par
still: for which reason she ought also the more to be respected, and we\par
are the more obliged (if we may live and pass our time according to her\par
purpose and intention) to think that all is well with us, and according\par
to our own minds. After this manner also, and in this respect it is,\par
that he that is all in all doth enjoy his happiness.\par
\par
XXXVI. What things soever are not within the proper power and\par
jurisdiction of thine own will either to compass or avoid, if thou shalt\par
propose unto thyself any of those things as either good, or evil; it\par
must needs be that according as thou shalt either fall into that which\par
thou dost think evil, or miss of that which thou dost think good, so\par
wilt thou be ready both to complain of the Gods, and to hate those men,\par
who either shall be so indeed, or shall by thee be suspected as the\par
cause either of thy missing of the one, or falling into the other. And\par
indeed we must needs commit many evils, if we incline to any of these\par
things, more or less, with an opinion of any difference. But if we mind\par
and fancy those things only, as good and bad, which wholly depend of our\par
own wills, there is no more occasion why we should either murmur against\par
the Gods, or be at enmity with any man.\par
\par
XXXVII. We all work to one effect, some willingly, and with a rational\par
apprehension of what we do: others without any such knowledge. As I\par
think Heraclitus in a place speaketh of them that sleep, that even they\par
do work in their kind, and do confer to the general operations of the\par
world. One man therefore doth co-operate after one sort, and another\par
after another sort; but even he that doth murmur, and to his power doth\par
resist and hinder; even he as much as any doth co-operate. For of such\par
also did the world stand in need. Now do thou consider among which of\par
these thou wilt rank thyself. For as for him who is the Administrator\par
of all, he will make good use of thee whether thou wilt or no, and make\par
thee (as a part and member of the whole) so to co-operate with him,\par
that whatsoever thou doest, shall turn to the furtherance of his own\par
counsels, and resolutions. But be not thou for shame such a part of the\par
whole, as that vile and ridiculous verse (which Chrysippus in a place\par
doth mention) is a part of the comedy. XXXVIII. Doth either the sun take\par
upon him to do that which belongs to the rain? or his son Aesculapius\par
that, which unto the earth doth properly belong? How is it with every\par
one of the stars in particular? Though they all differ one from another,\par
and have their several charges and functions by themselves, do they not\par
all nevertheless concur and co-operate to one end?\par
\par
XXXIX. If so be that the Gods have deliberated in particular of those\par
things that should happen unto me, I must stand to their deliberation,\par
as discrete and wise. For that a God should be an imprudent God, is a\par
thing hard even to conceive: and why should they resolve to do me hurt?\par
for what profit either unto them or the universe (which they specially\par
take care for) could arise from it? But if so be that they have not\par
deliberated of me in particular, certainly they have of the whole in\par
general, and those things which in consequence and coherence of this\par
general deliberation happen unto me in particular, I am bound to embrace\par
and accept of. But if so be that they have not deliberated at all (which\par
indeed is very irreligious for any man to believe: for then let us\par
neither sacrifice, nor pray, nor respect our oaths, neither let us any\par
more use any of those things, which we persuaded of the presence and\par
secret conversation of the Gods among us, daily use and practise:)\par
but, I say, if so be that they have not indeed either in general, or\par
particular deliberated of any of those things, that happen unto us\par
in this world; yet God be thanked, that of those things that\par
concern myself, it is lawful for me to deliberate myself, and all my\par
deliberation is but concerning that which may be to me most profitable.\par
Now that unto every one is most profitable, which is according to his\par
own constitution and nature. And my nature is, to be rational in all my\par
actions and as a good, and natural member of a city and commonwealth,\par
towards my fellow members ever to be sociably and kindly disposed and\par
affected. My city and country as I am Antoninus, is Rome; as a man, the\par
whole world. Those things therefore that are expedient and profitable to\par
those cities, are the only things that are good and expedient for me.\par
\par
XL. Whatsoever in any kind doth happen to any one, is expedient to the\par
whole. And thus much to content us might suffice, that it is expedient\par
for the whole in general. But yet this also shalt thou generally\par
perceive, if thou dost diligently take heed, that whatsoever doth happen\par
to any one man or men.... And now I am content that the word expedient,\par
should more generally be understood of those things which we otherwise\par
call middle things, or things indifferent; as health, wealth, and the\par
like.\par
\par
XLI. As the ordinary shows of the theatre and of other such places,\par
when thou art presented with them, affect thee; as the same things still\par
seen, and in the same fashion, make the sight ingrateful and tedious;\par
so must all the things that we see all our life long affect us. For all\par
things, above and below, are still the same, and from the same causes.\par
When then will there be an end?\par
\par
XLII. Let the several deaths of men of all sorts, and of all sorts of\par
professions, and of all sort of nations, be a perpetual object of thy\par
thoughts,... so that thou mayst even come down to Philistio, Phœbus,\par
and Origanion. Pass now to other generations. Thither shall we after\par
many changes, where so many brave orators are; where so many grave\par
philosophers; Heraclitus, Pythagoras, Socrates. Where so many heroes of\par
the old times; and then so many brave captains of the latter times; and\par
so many kings. After all these, where Eudoxus, Hipparchus, Archimedes;\par
where so many other sharp, generous, industrious, subtile, peremptory\par
dispositions; and among others, even they, that have been the greatest\par
scoffers and deriders of the frailty and brevity of this our human life;\par
as Menippus, and others, as many as there have been such as he. Of all\par
these consider, that they long since are all dead, and gone. And what do\par
they suffer by it! Nay they that have not so much as a name remaining,\par
what are they the worse for it? One thing there is, and that only, which\par
is worth our while in this world, and ought by us much to be esteemed;\par
and that is, according to truth and righteousness, meekly and lovingly\par
to converse with false, and unrighteous men.\par
\par
XLIII. When thou wilt comfort and cheer thyself, call to mind the\par
several gifts and virtues of them, whom thou dost daily converse with;\par
as for example, the industry of the one; the modesty of another; the\par
liberality of a third; of another some other thing. For nothing can so\par
much rejoice thee, as the resemblances and parallels of several virtues,\par
visible and eminent in the dispositions of those who live with thee;\par
especially when, all at once, as near as may be, they represent\par
themselves unto thee. And therefore thou must have them always in a\par
readiness.\par
\par
XLIV. Dost thou grieve that thou dost weigh but so many pounds, and not\par
three hundred rather? Just as much reason hast thou to grieve that\par
thou must live but so many years, and not longer. For as for bulk and\par
substance thou dost content thyself with that proportion of it that is\par
allotted unto thee, so shouldst thou for time.\par
\par
XLV. Let us do our best endeavours to persuade them; but however, if\par
reason and justice lead thee to it, do it, though they be never so much\par
against it. But if any shall by force withstand thee, and hinder thee in\par
it, convert thy virtuous inclination from one object unto another, from\par
justice to contented equanimity, and cheerful patience: so that what in\par
the one is thy hindrance, thou mayst make use of it for the exercise of\par
another virtue: and remember that it was with due exception, and\par
reservation, that thou didst at first incline and desire. For thou didst\par
not set thy mind upon things impossible. Upon what then? that all thy\par
desires might ever be moderated with this due kind of reservation. And\par
this thou hast, and mayst always obtain, whether the thing desired be in\par
thy power or no. And what do I care for more, if that for which I was\par
born and brought forth into the world (to rule all my desires with\par
reason and discretion) may be?\par
\par
XLVI. The ambitious supposeth another man's act, praise and applause, to\par
be his own happiness; the voluptuous his own sense and feeling; but he\par
that is wise, his own action.\par
\par
XLVII. It is in thy power absolutely to exclude all manner of conceit\par
and opinion, as concerning this matter; and by the same means, to\par
exclude all grief and sorrow from thy soul. For as for the things and\par
objects themselves, they of themselves have no such power, whereby to\par
beget and force upon us any opinion at all.\par
\par
XLVIII. Use thyself when any man speaks unto thee, so to hearken unto\par
him, as that in the interim thou give not way to any other thoughts;\par
that so thou mayst (as far as is possible) seem fixed and fastened to\par
his very soul, whosoever he be that speaks unto thee.\par
\par
XLIX. That which is not good for the bee-hive, cannot be good for the\par
bee.\par
\par
L. Will either passengers, or patients, find fault and complain, either\par
the one if they be well carried, or the others if well cured? Do they\par
take care for any more than this; the one, that their shipmaster may\par
bring them safe to land, and the other, that their physician may effect\par
their recovery?\par
\par
LI. How many of them who came into the world at the same time when I\par
did, are already gone out of it?\par
\par
LII. To them that are sick of the jaundice, honey seems bitter; and to\par
them that are bitten by a mad dog, the water terrible; and to children,\par
a little ball seems a fine thing. And why then should I be angry? or\par
do I think that error and false opinion is less powerful to make men\par
transgress, than either choler, being immoderate and excessive, to cause\par
the jaundice; or poison, to cause rage?\par
\par
LIII. No man can hinder thee to live as thy nature doth require. Nothing\par
can happen unto thee, but what the common good of nature doth require.\par
\par
LIV. What manner of men they be whom they seek to please, and what to\par
get, and by what actions: how soon time will cover and bury all things,\par
and how many it hath already buried!\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
THE SEVENTH BOOK\par
\par
\par
I. What is wickedness? It is that which many time and often thou hast\par
already seen and known in the world. And so oft as anything doth happen\par
that might otherwise trouble thee, let this memento presently come to\par
thy mind, that it is that which thou hast already often Seen and known.\par
Generally, above and below, thou shalt find but the same things. The\par
very same things whereof ancient stories, middle age stories, and fresh\par
stories are full whereof towns are full, and houses full. There is\par
nothing that is new. All things that are, are both usual and of little\par
continuance.\par
\par
II. What fear is there that thy dogmata, or philosophical resolutions\par
and conclusions, should become dead in thee, and lose their proper\par
power and efficacy to make thee live happy, as long as those proper\par
and correlative fancies, and representations of things on which they\par
mutually depend (which continually to stir up and revive is in thy\par
power,) are still kept fresh and alive? It is in my power concerning\par
this thing that is happened, what soever it be, to conceit that which is\par
right and true. If it be, why then am I troubled? Those things that are\par
without my understanding, are nothing to it at all: and that is it only,\par
which doth properly concern me. Be always in this mind, and thou wilt be\par
right.\par
\par
III. That which most men would think themselves most happy for, and\par
would prefer before all things, if the Gods would grant it unto them\par
after their deaths, thou mayst whilst thou livest grant unto thyself; to\par
live again. See the things of the world again, as thou hast already seen\par
them. For what is it else to live again? Public shows and solemnities\par
with much pomp and vanity, stage plays, flocks and herds; conflicts\par
and contentions: a bone thrown to a company of hungry curs; a bait for\par
greedy fishes; the painfulness, and continual burden-bearing of wretched\par
ants, the running to and fro of terrified mice: little puppets drawn up\par
and down with wires and nerves: these be the objects of the world among\par
all these thou must stand steadfast, meekly affected, and free from all\par
manner of indignation; with this right ratiocination and apprehension;\par
that as the worth is of those things which a man doth affect, so is in\par
very deed every man's worth more or less.\par
\par
IV. Word after word, every one by itself, must the things that are\par
spoken be conceived and understood; and so the things that are done,\par
purpose after purpose, every one by itself likewise. And as in matter of\par
purposes and actions, we must presently see what is the proper use and\par
relation of every one; so of words must we be as ready, to consider of\par
every one what is the true meaning, and signification of it according to\par
truth and nature, however it be taken in common use.\par
\par
V. Is my reason, and understanding sufficient for this, or no? If it be\par
sufficient, without any private applause, or public ostentation as of an\par
instrument, which by nature I am provided of, I will make use of it for\par
the work in hand, as of an instrument, which by nature I am provided of.\par
if it be not, and that otherwise it belong not unto me particularly as\par
a private duty, I will either give it over, and leave it to some other\par
that can better effect it: or I will endeavour it; but with the help\par
of some other, who with the joint help of my reason, is able to bring\par
somewhat to pass, that will now be seasonable and useful for the common\par
good. For whatsoever I do either by myself, or with some other, the\par
only thing that I must intend, is, that it be good and expedient for\par
the public. For as for praise, consider how many who once were much\par
commended, are now already quite forgotten, yea they that commended\par
them, how even they themselves are long since dead and gone. Be not\par
therefore ashamed, whensoever thou must use the help of others. For\par
whatsoever it be that lieth upon thee to effect, thou must propose it\par
unto thyself, as the scaling of walls is unto a soldier. And what if\par
thou through either lameness or some other impediment art not able to\par
reach unto the top of the battlements alone, which with the help of\par
another thou mayst; wilt thou therefore give it over, or go about it\par
with less courage and alacrity, because thou canst not effect it all\par
alone?\par
\par
VI. Let not things future trouble thee. For if necessity so require that\par
they come to pass, thou shalt (whensoever that is) be provided for them\par
with the same reason, by which whatsoever is now present, is made both\par
tolerable and acceptable unto thee. All things are linked and knitted\par
together, and the knot is sacred, neither is there anything in the\par
world, that is not kind and natural in regard of any other thing, or,\par
that hath not some kind of reference and natural correspondence with\par
whatsoever is in the world besides. For all things are ranked together,\par
and by that decency of its due place and order that each particular\par
doth observe, they all concur together to the making of one and the same\par
κόσμος or world: as if you said, a comely piece, or an orderly\par
composition. For all things throughout, there is but one and the same\par
order; and through all things, one and the same God, the same substance\par
and the same law. There is one common reason, and one common truth, that\par
belongs unto all reasonable creatures, for neither is there save one\par
perfection of all creatures that are of the same kind, and partakers of\par
the same reason.\par
\par
VII. Whatsoever is material, doth soon vanish away into the common\par
substance of the whole; and whatsoever is formal, or, whatsoever doth\par
animate that which is material, is soon resumed into the common reason\par
of the whole; and the fame and memory of anything, is soon swallowed up\par
by the general age and duration of the whole.\par
\par
VIII. To a reasonable creature, the same action is both according\par
to nature, and according to reason.\par
\par
IX. Straight of itself, not made straight.\par
\par
X. As several members in one body united, so are reasonable creatures\par
in a body divided and dispersed, all made and prepared for one common\par
operation. And this thou shalt apprehend the better, if thou shalt use\par
thyself often to say to thyself, I am μέλος, or a member of the mass and\par
body of reasonable substances. But if thou shalt say I am μέρος, or\par
a part, thou dost not yet love men from thy heart. The joy that thou\par
takest in the exercise of bounty, is not yet grounded upon a due\par
ratiocination and right apprehension of the nature of things. Thou dost\par
exercise it as yet upon this ground barely, as a thing convenient and\par
fitting; not, as doing good to thyself, when thou dost good unto others.\par
\par
XI. Of things that are external, happen what will to that which can\par
suffer by external accidents. Those things that suffer let them complain\par
themselves, if they will; as for me, as long as I conceive no such\par
thing, that that which is happened is evil, I have no hurt; and it is in\par
my power not to conceive any such thing.\par
\par
XII. Whatsoever any man either doth or saith, thou must be good; not for\par
any man's sake, but for thine own nature's sake; as if either gold, or\par
the emerald, or purple, should ever be saying to themselves, Whatsoever\par
any man either doth or saith, I must still be an emerald, and I must\par
keep my colour.\par
\par
XIII. This may ever be my comfort and security: my understanding, that\par
ruleth over all, will not of itself bring trouble and vexation upon\par
itself. This I say; it will not put itself in any fear, it will not lead\par
itself into any concupiscence. If it be in the power of any other to\par
compel it to fear, or to grieve, it is free for him to use his power.\par
But sure if itself do not of itself, through some false opinion or\par
supposition incline itself to any such disposition; there is no fear.\par
For as for the body, why should I make the grief of my body, to be the\par
grief of my mind? If that itself can either fear or complain, let it.\par
But as for the soul, which indeed, can only be truly sensible of either\par
fear or grief; to which only it belongs according to its different\par
imaginations and opinions, to admit of either of these, or of their\par
contraries; thou mayst look to that thyself, that it suffer nothing.\par
Induce her not to any such opinion or persuasion. The understanding\par
is of itself sufficient unto itself, and needs not (if itself doth not\par
bring itself to need) any other thing besides itself, and by consequent\par
as it needs nothing, so neither can it be troubled or hindered by\par
anything, if itself doth not trouble and hinder itself.\par
\par
XIV. What is εὐδαιμονία, or happiness: but ἀγαθὸς δαίμων, or, a good\par
dæmon, or spirit? What then dost thou do here, O opinion? By the Gods\par
I adjure thee, that thou get thee gone, as thou earnest: for I need thee\par
not. Thou earnest indeed unto me according to thy ancient wonted manner.\par
It is that, that all men have ever been subject unto. That thou camest\par
therefore I am not angry with thee, only begone, now that I have found\par
thee what thou art.\par
\par
XV. Is any man so foolish as to fear change, to which all things that\par
once were not owe their being? And what is it, that is more pleasing and\par
more familiar to the nature of the universe? How couldst thou thyself\par
use thy ordinary hot baths, should not the wood that heateth them first\par
be changed? How couldst thou receive any nourishment from those things\par
that thou hast eaten, if they should not be changed? Can anything\par
else almost (that is useful and profitable) be brought to pass without\par
change? How then dost not thou perceive, that for thee also, by death,\par
to come to change, is a thing of the very same nature, and as necessary\par
for the nature of the universe?\par
\par
XVI. Through the substance of the universe, as through a torrent pass\par
all particular bodies, being all of the same nature, and all joint\par
workers with the universe itself as in one of our bodies so many\par
members among themselves. How many such as Chrysippus, how many such\par
as Socrates, how many such as Epictetus, hath the age of the world\par
long since swallowed up and devoured? Let this, be it either men or\par
businesses, that thou hast occasion to think of, to the end that thy\par
thoughts be not distracted and thy mind too earnestly set upon anything,\par
upon every such occasion presently come to thy mind. Of all my thoughts\par
and cares, one only thing shall be the object, that I myself do nothing\par
which to the proper constitution of man, (either in regard of the\par
thing itself, or in regard of the manner, or of the time of doing,)\par
is contrary. The time when thou shalt have forgotten all things, is\par
at hand. And that time also is at hand, when thou thyself shalt be\par
forgotten by all. Whilst thou art, apply thyself to that especially\par
which unto man as he is a mart, is most proper and agreeable, and that\par
is, for a man even to love them that transgress against him. This shall\par
be, if at the same time that any such thing doth happen, thou call\par
to mind, that they are thy kinsmen; that it is through ignorance and\par
against their wills that they sin; and that within a very short while\par
after, both thou and he shall be no more. But above all things, that he\par
hath not done thee any hurt; for that by him thy mind and understanding\par
is not made worse or more vile than it was before.\par
\par
XVII. The nature of the universe, of the common substance of all things\par
as it were of so much wax hath now perchance formed a horse; and then,\par
destroying that figure, hath new tempered and fashioned the matter of it\par
into the form and substance of a tree: then that again into the form and\par
substance of a man: and then that again into some other. Now every one\par
of these doth subsist but for a very little while. As for dissolution,\par
if it be no grievous thing to the chest or trunk, to be joined together;\par
why should it be more grievous to be put asunder?\par
\par
XVIII. An angry countenance is much against nature, and it is oftentimes\par
the proper countenance of them that are at the point of death. But were\par
it so, that all anger and passion were so thoroughly quenched in thee,\par
that it were altogether impossible to kindle it any more, yet herein\par
must not thou rest satisfied, but further endeavour by good consequence\par
of true ratiocination, perfectly to conceive and understand, that all\par
anger and passion is against reason. For if thou shalt not be sensible\par
of thine innocence; if that also shall be gone from thee, the comfort of\par
a good conscience, that thou doest all things according to reason: what\par
shouldest thou live any longer for? All things that now thou seest,\par
are but for a moment. That nature, by which all things in the world are\par
administered, will soon bring change and alteration upon them, and then\par
of their substances make other things like unto them: and then soon\par
after others again of the matter and substance of these: that so by\par
these means, the world may still appear fresh and new.\par
\par
XIX. Whensoever any man doth trespass against other, presently consider\par
with thyself what it was that he did suppose to be good, what to be\par
evil, when he did trespass. For this when thou knowest, thou wilt pity\par
him thou wilt have no occasion either to wonder, or to be angry. For\par
either thou thyself dust yet live in that error and ignorance, as that\par
thou dust suppose either that very thing that he doth, or some other\par
like worldly thing, to be good; and so thou art bound to pardon him if\par
he have done that which thou in the like case wouldst have done thyself.\par
Or if so be that thou dost not any more suppose the same things to be\par
good or evil, that he doth; how canst thou but be gentle unto him that\par
is in an error?\par
\par
XX. Fancy not to thyself things future, as though they were present\par
but of those that are present, take some aside, that thou takest most\par
benefit of, and consider of them particularly, how wonderfully thou\par
wouldst want them, if they were not present. But take heed withal, lest\par
that whilst thou dust settle thy contentment in things present, thou\par
grow in time so to overprize them, as that the want of them (whensoever\par
it shall so fall out) should be a trouble and a vexation unto thee. Wind\par
up thyself into thyself. Such is the nature of thy reasonable\par
commanding part, as that if it exercise justice, and have by that means\par
tranquillity within itself, it doth rest fully satisfied with itself\par
without any other thing.\par
\par
XXI. Wipe off all opinion stay the force and violence of unreasonable\par
lusts and affections: circumscribe the present time examine whatsoever\par
it be that is happened, either to thyself or to another: divide all\par
present objects, either in that which is formal or material think of the\par
last hour. That which thy neighbour hath committed, where the guilt of\par
it lieth, there let it rest. Examine in order whatsoever is spoken. Let\par
thy mind penetrate both into the effects, and into the causes. Rejoice\par
thyself with true simplicity, and modesty; and that all middle things\par
between virtue and vice are indifferent unto thee. Finally, love\par
mankind; obey God.\par
\par
XXII. All things (saith he) are by certain order and appointment. And\par
what if the elements only.\par
\par
It will suffice to remember, that all things in general are by certain\par
order and appointment: or if it be but few. And as concerning death,\par
that either dispersion, or the atoms, or annihilation, or extinction,\par
or translation will ensue. And as concerning pain, that that which is\par
intolerable is soon ended by death; and that which holds long must needs\par
be tolerable; and that the mind in the meantime (which is all in all)\par
may by way of interclusion, or interception, by stopping all manner of\par
commerce and sympathy with the body, still retain its own tranquillity.\par
Thy understanding is not made worse by it. As for those parts that\par
suffer, let them, if they can, declare their grief themselves. As for\par
praise and commendation, view their mind and understanding, what estate\par
they are in; what kind of things they fly, and what things they seek\par
after: and that as in the seaside, whatsoever was before to be seen,\par
is by the continual succession of new heaps of sand cast up one upon\par
another, soon hid and covered; so in this life, all former things by\par
those which immediately succeed.\par
\par
XXIII. Out of Plato. 'He then whose mind is endowed with true\par
magnanimity, who hath accustomed himself to the contemplation both of\par
all times, and of all things in general; can this mortal life (thinkest\par
thou) seem any great matter unto him? It is not possible, answered he.\par
Then neither will such a one account death a grievous thing? By no\par
means.'\par
\par
XXIV. Out of Antisthenes. 'It is a princely thing to do well, and to be\par
ill-spoken of. It is a shameful thing that the face should be subject\par
unto the mind, to be put into what shape it will, and to be dressed by\par
it as it will; and that the mind should not bestow so much care upon\par
herself, as to fashion herself, and to dress herself as best becometh\par
her.'\par
\par
XXV. Out of several poets and comics. 'It will but little avail thee,\par
to turn thine anger and indignation upon the things themselves that have\par
fallen across unto thee. For as for them, they are not sensible of it,\par
&c. Thou shalt but make thyself a laughing-stock; both unto the Gods and\par
men, &c. Our life is reaped like a ripe ear of corn; one is yet\par
standing and another is down, &c. But if so be that I and my children be\par
neglected by the gods, there is some reason even for that, &c. As long\par
as right and equity is of my side, &c. Not to lament with them, not to\par
tremble, &c.'\par
\par
XXVI. Out of Plato. 'My answer, full of justice and equity, should be\par
this: Thy speech is not right, O man! if thou supposest that he that is\par
of any worth at all, should apprehend either life or death, as a matter\par
of great hazard and danger; and should not make this rather his only\par
care, to examine his own actions, whether just or unjust: whether\par
actions of a good, or of a wicked man, &c. For thus in very truth stands\par
the case, O ye men of Athens. What place or station soever a man either\par
hath chosen to himself, judging it best for himself; or is by lawful\par
authority put and settled in, therein do I think (all appearance of\par
danger notwithstanding) that he should continue, as one who feareth\par
neither death, nor anything else, so much as he feareth to commit\par
anything that is vicious and shameful, &c. But, O noble sir, consider\par
I pray, whether true generosity and true happiness, do not consist in\par
somewhat else rather, than in the preservation either of our, or other\par
men's lives. For it is not the part of a man that is a man indeed, to\par
desire to live long or to make much of his life whilst he liveth: but\par
rather (he that is such) will in these things wholly refer himself unto\par
the Gods, and believing that which every woman can tell him, that no man\par
can escape death; the only thing that he takes thought and care for is\par
this, that what time he liveth, he may live as well and as virtuously\par
as he can possibly, &c. To look about, and with the eyes to follow the\par
course of the stars and planets as though thou wouldst run with them;\par
and to mind perpetually the several changes of the elements one into\par
another. For such fancies and imaginations, help much to purge away\par
the dross and filth of this our earthly life,' &c. That also is a fine\par
passage of Plato's, where he speaketh of worldly things in these words:\par
'Thou must also as from some higher place look down, as it were, upon\par
the things of this world, as flocks, armies, husbandmen's labours,\par
marriages, divorces, generations, deaths: the tumults of courts and\par
places of judicatures; desert places; the several nations of barbarians,\par
public festivals, mournings, fairs, markets.' How all things upon earth\par
are pell-mell; and how miraculously things contrary one to another,\par
concur to the beauty and perfection of this universe.\par
\par
XXVII. To look back upon things of former ages, as upon the manifold\par
changes and conversions of several monarchies and commonwealths. We\par
may also foresee things future, for they shall all be of the same kind;\par
neither is it possible that they should leave the tune, or break the\par
concert that is now begun, as it were, by these things that are now done\par
and brought to pass in the world. It comes all to one therefore, whether\par
a man be a spectator of the things of this life but forty years, or\par
whether he see them ten thousand years together: for what shall he\par
see more? 'And as for those parts that came from the earth, they shall\par
return unto the earth again; and those that came from heaven, they\par
also shall return unto those heavenly places.' Whether it be a mere\par
dissolution and unbinding of the manifold intricacies and entanglements\par
of the confused atoms; or some such dispersion of the simple and\par
incorruptible elements... 'With meats and drinks and divers charms, they\par
seek to divert the channel, that they might not die. Yet must we needs\par
endure that blast of wind that cometh from above, though we toil and\par
labour never so much.'\par
\par
XXVIII. He hath a stronger body, and is a better wrestler than I. What\par
then? Is he more bountiful? is he more modest? Doth he bear all adverse\par
chances with more equanimity: or with his neighbour's offences with more\par
meekness and gentleness than I?\par
\par
XXIX. Where the matter may be effected agreeably to that reason, which\par
both unto the Gods and men is common, there can be no just cause of\par
grief or sorrow. For where the fruit and benefit of an action well begun\par
and prosecuted according to the proper constitution of man may be reaped\par
and obtained, or is sure and certain, it is against reason that any\par
damage should there be suspected. In all places, and at all times, it is\par
in thy power religiously to embrace whatsoever by God's appointment is\par
happened unto thee, and justly to converse with those men, whom thou\par
hast to do with, and accurately to examine every fancy that presents\par
itself, that nothing may slip and steal in, before thou hast rightly\par
apprehended the true nature of it.\par
\par
XXX. Look not about upon other men's minds and understandings; but look\par
right on forwards whither nature, both that of the universe, in those\par
things that happen unto thee; and thine in particular, in those things\par
that are done by thee: doth lead, and direct thee. Now every one is\par
bound to do that, which is consequent and agreeable to that end which\par
by his true natural constitution he was ordained unto. As for all other\par
things, they are ordained for the use of reasonable creatures: as in all\par
things we see that that which is worse and inferior, is made for\par
that which is better. Reasonable creatures, they are ordained one for\par
another. That therefore which is chief in every man's constitution, is,\par
that he intend the common good. The second is, that he yield not to any\par
lusts and motions of the flesh. For it is the part and privilege of the\par
reasonable and intellective faculty, that she can so bound herself,\par
as that neither the sensitive, nor the appetitive faculties, may not\par
anyways prevail upon her. For both these are brutish. And therefore over\par
both she challengeth mastery, and cannot anyways endure, if in her right\par
temper, to be subject unto either. And this indeed most justly. For\par
by nature she was ordained to command all in the body. The third\par
thing proper to man by his constitution, is, to avoid all rashness and\par
precipitancy; and not to be subject to error. To these things then, let\par
the mind apply herself and go straight on, without any distraction about\par
other things, and she hath her end, and by consequent her happiness.\par
\par
XXXI. As one who had lived, and were now to die by right, whatsoever is\par
yet remaining, bestow that wholly as a gracious overplus upon a virtuous\par
life. Love and affect that only, whatsoever it be that happeneth, and is\par
by the fates appointed unto thee. For what can be more reasonable? And\par
as anything doth happen unto thee by way of cross, or calamity, call\par
to mind presently and set before thine eyes, the examples of some other\par
men, to whom the self-same thing did once happen likewise. Well, what\par
did they? They grieved; they wondered; they complained. And where are\par
they now? All dead and gone. Wilt thou also be like one of them?\par
Or rather leaving to men of the world (whose life both in regard of\par
themselves, and them that they converse with, is nothing but mere\par
mutability; or men of as fickle minds, as fickle bodies; ever changing\par
and soon changed themselves) let it be thine only care and study, how to\par
make a right use of all such accidents. For there is good use to be made\par
of them, and they will prove fit matter for thee to work upon, if it\par
shall be both thy care and thy desire, that whatsoever thou doest, thou\par
thyself mayst like and approve thyself for it. And both these, see,\par
that thou remember well, according as the diversity of the matter of\par
the action that thou art about shall require. Look within; within is the\par
fountain of all good. Such a fountain, where springing waters can never\par
fail, so thou dig still deeper and deeper.\par
\par
XXXII. Thou must use thyself also to keep thy body fixed and steady;\par
free from all loose fluctuant either motion, or posture. And as upon thy\par
face and looks, thy mind hath easily power over them to keep them to\par
that which is grave and decent; so let it challenge the same power over\par
the whole body also. But so observe all things in this kind, as that it\par
be without any manner of affectation.\par
\par
XXXIII. The art of true living in this world is more like a wrestler's,\par
than a dancer's practice. For in this they both agree, to teach a man\par
whatsoever falls upon him, that he may be ready for it, and that nothing\par
may cast him down.\par
\par
XXXIV. Thou must continually ponder and consider with thyself, what\par
manner of men they be, and for their minds and understandings what is\par
their present estate, whose good word and testimony thou dost desire.\par
For then neither wilt thou see cause to complain of them that offend\par
against their wills; or find any want of their applause, if once\par
thou dost but penetrate into the true force and ground both of their\par
opinions, and of their desires. 'No soul (saith he) is willingly bereft\par
of the truth,' and by consequent, neither of justice, or temperance, or\par
kindness, and mildness; nor of anything that is of the same kind. It is\par
most needful that thou shouldst always remember this. For so shalt thou\par
be far more gentle and moderate towards all men.\par
\par
XXXV. What pain soever thou art in, let this presently come to thy mind,\par
that it is not a thing whereof thou needest to be ashamed, neither is it\par
a thing whereby thy understanding, that hath the government of all,\par
can be made worse. For neither in regard of the substance of it, nor\par
in regard of the end of it (which is, to intend the common good) can\par
it alter and corrupt it. This also of Epicurus mayst thou in most pains\par
find some help of, that it is 'neither intolerable, nor eternal;' so\par
thou keep thyself to the true bounds and limits of reason and give not\par
way to opinion. This also thou must consider, that many things there be,\par
which oftentimes unsensibly trouble and vex thee, as not armed against\par
them with patience, because they go not ordinarily under the name of\par
pains, which in very deed are of the same nature as pain; as to slumber\par
unquietly, to suffer heat, to want appetite: when therefore any of these\par
things make thee discontented, check thyself with these words: Now hath\par
pain given thee the foil; thy courage hath failed thee.\par
\par
XXXVI. Take heed lest at any time thou stand so affected, though towards\par
unnatural evil men, as ordinary men are commonly one towards another.\par
\par
XXXVII. How know we whether Socrates were so eminent indeed, and of so\par
extraordinary a disposition? For that he died more gloriously, that he\par
disputed with the Sophists more subtilty; that he watched in the frost\par
more assiduously; that being commanded to fetch innocent Salaminius, he\par
refused to do it more generously; all this will not serve. Nor that he\par
walked in the streets, with much gravity and majesty, as was objected\par
unto him by his adversaries: which nevertheless a man may well doubt of,\par
whether it were so or no, or, which above all the rest, if so be that\par
it were true, a man would well consider of, whether commendable, or\par
dis-commendable. The thing therefore that we must inquire into, is this;\par
what manner of soul Socrates had: whether his disposition was such; as\par
that all that he stood upon, and sought after in this world, was barely\par
this, that he might ever carry himself justly towards men, and holily\par
towards the Gods. Neither vexing himself to no purpose at the wickedness\par
of others, nor yet ever condescending to any man's evil fact, or evil\par
intentions, through either fear, or engagement of friendship. Whether of\par
those things that happened unto him by God's appointment, he neither did\par
wonder at any when it did happen, or thought it intolerable in the trial\par
of it. And lastly, whether he never did suffer his mind to sympathise\par
with the senses, and affections of the body. For we must not think that\par
Nature hath so mixed and tempered it with the body, as that she hath not\par
power to circumscribe herself, and by herself to intend her own ends and\par
occasions.\par
\par
XXXVIII. For it is a thing very possible, that a man should be a very\par
divine man, and yet be altogether unknown. This thou must ever be\par
mindful of, as of this also, that a man's true happiness doth consist\par
in very few things. And that although thou dost despair, that thou shalt\par
ever be a good either logician, or naturalist, yet thou art never the\par
further off by it from being either liberal, or modest, or charitable,\par
or obedient unto God.\par
\par
XXXIX. Free from all compulsion in all cheerfulness and alacrity thou\par
mayst run out thy time, though men should exclaim against thee never so\par
much, and the wild beasts should pull in sunder the poor members of thy\par
pampered mass of flesh. For what in either of these or the like cases\par
should hinder the mind to retain her own rest and tranquillity,\par
consisting both in the right judgment of those things that happen unto\par
her, and in the ready use of all present matters and occasions? So that\par
her judgment may say, to that which is befallen her by way of cross:\par
this thou art in very deed, and according to thy true nature:\par
notwithstanding that in the judgment of opinion thou dust appear\par
otherwise: and her discretion to the present object; thou art that,\par
which I sought for. For whatsoever it be, that is now present, shall\par
ever be embraced by me as a fit and seasonable object, both for my\par
reasonable faculty, and for my sociable, or charitable inclination to\par
work upon. And that which is principal in this matter, is that it may be\par
referred either unto the praise of God, or to the good of men. For\par
either unto God or man, whatsoever it is that doth happen in the world\par
hath in the ordinary course of nature its proper reference; neither is\par
there anything, that in regard of nature is either new, or reluctant and\par
intractable, but all things both usual and easy.\par
\par
XL. Then hath a man attained to the estate of perfection in his life and\par
conversation, when he so spends every day, as if it were his last day:\par
never hot and vehement in his affections, nor yet so cold and stupid as\par
one that had no sense; and free from all manner of dissimulation.\par
\par
XLI. Can the Gods, who are immortal, for the continuance of so many ages\par
bear without indignation with such and so many sinners, as have ever\par
been, yea not only so, but also take such care for them, that they want\par
nothing; and dust thou so grievously take on, as one that could bear\par
with them no longer; thou that art but for a moment of time? yea thou\par
that art one of those sinners thyself? A very ridiculous thing it is,\par
that any man should dispense with vice and wickedness in himself, which\par
is in his power to restrain; and should go about to suppress it in\par
others, which is altogether impossible.\par
\par
XLII. What object soever, our reasonable and sociable faculty doth meet\par
with, that affords nothing either for the satisfaction of reason, or for\par
the practice of charity, she worthily doth think unworthy of herself.\par
\par
XLIII. When thou hast done well, and another is benefited by thy action,\par
must thou like a very fool look for a third thing besides, as that\par
it may appear unto others also that thou hast done well, or that thou\par
mayest in time, receive one good turn for another? No man useth to be\par
weary of that which is beneficial unto him. But every action according\par
to nature, is beneficial. Be not weary then of doing that which is\par
beneficial unto thee, whilst it is so unto others.\par
\par
XLIV. The nature of the universe did once certainly before it was\par
created, whatsoever it hath done since, deliberate and so resolve upon\par
the creation of the world. Now since that time, whatsoever it is, that\par
is and happens in the world, is either but a consequent of that one and\par
first deliberation: or if so be that this ruling rational part of the\par
world, takes any thought and care of things particular, they are surely\par
his reasonable and principal creatures, that are the proper object of\par
his particular care and providence. This often thought upon, will much\par
conduce to thy tranquillity.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
THE EIGHTH BOOK\par
\par
\par
I. This also, among other things, may serve to keep thee from vainglory;\par
if thou shalt consider, that thou art now altogether incapable of the\par
commendation of one, who all his life long, or from his youth at least,\par
hath lived a philosopher's life. For both unto others, and to thyself\par
especially, it is well known, that thou hast done many things contrary\par
to that perfection of life. Thou hast therefore been confounded in thy\par
course, and henceforth it will be hard for thee to recover the title and\par
credit of a philosopher. And to it also is thy calling and profession\par
repugnant. If therefore thou dost truly understand, what it is that is\par
of moment indeed; as for thy fame and credit, take no thought or care\par
for that: let it suffice thee if all the rest of thy life, be it more or\par
less, thou shalt live as thy nature requireth, or according to the true\par
and natural end of thy making. Take pains therefore to know what it is\par
that thy nature requireth, and let nothing else distract thee. Thou\par
hast already had sufficient experience, that of those many things that\par
hitherto thou hast erred and wandered about, thou couldst not find\par
happiness in any of them. Not in syllogisms, and logical subtilties, not\par
in wealth, not in honour and reputation, not in pleasure. In none of all\par
these. Wherein then is it to be found? In the practice of those things,\par
which the nature of man, as he is a man, doth require. How then shall\par
he do those things? if his dogmata, or moral tenets and opinions (from\par
which all motions and actions do proceed), be right and true. Which be\par
those dogmata? Those that concern that which is good or evil, as that\par
there is nothing truly good and beneficial unto man, but that which\par
makes him just, temperate, courageous, liberal; and that there is\par
nothing truly evil and hurtful unto man, but that which causeth the\par
contrary effects.\par
\par
II. Upon every action that thou art about, put this question to thyself;\par
How will this when it is done agree with me? Shall I have no occasion\par
to repent of it? Yet a very little while and I am dead and gone; and\par
all things are at end. What then do I care for more than this, that my\par
present action whatsoever it be, may be the proper action of one that\par
is reasonable; whose end is, the common good; who in all things is ruled\par
and governed by the same law of right and reason, by which God Himself\par
is.\par
\par
III. Alexander, Caius, Pompeius; what are these to Diogenes, Heraclitus,\par
and Socrates? These penetrated into the true nature of things; into all\par
causes, and all subjects: and upon these did they exercise their power\par
and authority. But as for those, as the extent of their error was, so\par
far did their slavery extend.\par
\par
IV. What they have done, they will still do, although thou shouldst hang\par
thyself. First; let it not trouble thee. For all things both good and\par
evil: come to pass according to the nature and general condition of the\par
universe, and within a very little while, all things will be at an\par
end; no man will be remembered: as now of Africanus (for example) and\par
Augustus it is already come to pass. Then secondly; fix thy mind upon\par
the thing itself; look into it, and remembering thyself, that thou art\par
bound nevertheless to be a good man, and what it is that thy nature\par
requireth of thee as thou art a man, be not diverted from what thou art\par
about, and speak that which seemeth unto thee most just: only speak it\par
kindly, modestly, and without hypocrisy.\par
\par
V. That which the nature of the universe doth busy herself about, is;\par
that which is here, to transfer it thither, to change it, and thence\par
again to take it away, and to carry it to another place. So that thou\par
needest not fear any new thing. For all things are usual and ordinary;\par
and all things are disposed by equality.\par
\par
VI. Every particular nature hath content, when in its own proper course\par
it speeds. A reasonable nature doth then speed, when first in matter of\par
fancies and imaginations, it gives no consent to that which is either\par
false uncertain. Secondly, when in all its motions and resolutions it\par
takes its level at the common good only, and that it desireth nothing,\par
and flieth from nothing, bet what is in its own power to compass or\par
avoid. And lastly, when it willingly and gladly embraceth, whatsoever is\par
dealt and appointed unto it by the common nature. For it is part of it;\par
even as the nature of any one leaf, is part of the common nature of all\par
plants and trees. But that the nature of a leaf, is part of a nature\par
both unreasonable and unsensible, and which in its proper end may be\par
hindered; or, which is servile and slavish: whereas the nature of man is\par
part of a common nature which cannot be hindered, and which is both\par
reasonable and just. From whence also it is, that according to the\par
worth of everything, she doth make such equal distribution of all\par
things, as of duration, substance form, operation, and of events and\par
accidents. But herein consider not whether thou shalt find this equality\par
in everything absolutely and by itself; but whether in all the\par
particulars of some one thing taken together, and compared with all the\par
particulars of some other thing, and them together likewise.\par
\par
VII. Thou hast no time nor opportunity to read. What then? Hast thou\par
not time and opportunity to exercise thyself, not to wrong thyself; to\par
strive against all carnal pleasures and pains, and to get the upper hand\par
of them; to contemn honour and vainglory; and not only, not to be angry\par
with them, whom towards thee thou doest find unsensible and unthankful;\par
but also to have a care of them still, and of their welfare?\par
\par
VIII. Forbear henceforth to complain of the trouble of a courtly life,\par
either in public before others, or in private by thyself.\par
\par
IX. Repentance is an inward and self-reprehension for the neglect or\par
omission of somewhat that was profitable. Now whatsoever is good, is\par
also profitable, and it is the part of an honest virtuous man to set by\par
it, and to make reckoning of it accordingly. But never did any honest\par
virtuous man repent of the neglect or omission of any carnal pleasure:\par
no carnal pleasure then is either good or profitable.\par
\par
X. This, what is it in itself, and by itself, according to its proper\par
constitution? What is the substance of it? What is the matter, or proper\par
use? What is the form or efficient cause? What is it for in this world,\par
and how long will it abide? Thus must thou examine all things, that\par
present themselves unto thee.\par
\par
XI. When thou art hard to be stirred up and awaked out of thy sleep,\par
admonish thyself and call to mind, that, to perform actions tending to\par
the common good is that which thine own proper constitution, and\par
that which the nature of man do require. But to sleep, is common to\par
unreasonable creatures also. And what more proper and natural, yea what\par
more kind and pleasing, than that which is according to nature?\par
\par
XII. As every fancy and imagination presents itself unto thee, consider\par
(if it be possible) the true nature, and the proper qualities of it, and\par
reason with thyself about it.\par
\par
XIII. At thy first encounter with any one, say presently to thyself:\par
This man, what are his opinions concerning that which is good or evil?\par
as concerning pain, pleasure, and the causes of both; concerning honour,\par
and dishonour, concerning life and death? thus and thus. Now if it be\par
no wonder that a man should have such and such opinions, how can it be\par
a wonder that he should do such and such things? I will remember then,\par
that he cannot but do as he doth, holding those opinions that he doth.\par
Remember, that as it is a shame for any man to wonder that a fig tree\par
should bear figs, so also to wonder that the world should bear anything,\par
whatsoever it is which in the ordinary course of nature it may bear.\par
To a physician also and to a pilot it is a shame either for the one to\par
wonder, that such and such a one should have an ague; or for the other,\par
that the winds should prove Contrary.\par
\par
XIV. Remember, that to change thy mind upon occasion, and to follow him\par
that is able to rectify thee, is equally ingenuous, as to find out at\par
the first, what is right and just, without help. For of thee nothing is\par
required, ti, is beyond the extent of thine own deliberation and jun.\par
merit, and of thine own understanding.\par
\par
XV. If it were thine act and in thine own power, wouldest thou do\par
it? If it were not, whom dost tin accuse? the atoms, or the Gods? For to\par
do either, the part of a mad man. Thou must therefore blame nobody, but\par
if it be in thy power, redress what is amiss; if it be not, to what end\par
is it to complain? For nothing should be done but to some certain end.\par
\par
XVI. Whatsoever dieth and falleth, however and wheresoever it die\par
and fall, it cannot fall out of the world, here it have its abode\par
and change, here also shall it have its dissolution into its proper\par
elements. The same are the world's elements, and the elements of which\par
thou dost consist. And they when they are changed, they murmur not; why\par
shouldest thou?\par
\par
XVII. Whatsoever is, was made for something: as a horse, a vine. Why\par
wonderest thou? The sun itself will say of itself, I was made for\par
something; and so hath every god its proper function. What then were\par
then made for? to disport and delight thyself? See how even common sense\par
and reason cannot brook it.\par
\par
XVIII. Nature hath its end as well in the end and final consummation of\par
anything that is, as in the begin-nine and continuation of it.\par
\par
XIX. As one that tosseth up a ball. And what is a ball the better, if\par
the motion of it be upwards; or the worse if it be downwards; or if it\par
chance to fall upon the ground? So for the bubble; if it continue, what\par
it the better? and if it dissolve, what is it the worse And so is it of\par
a candle too. And so must thou reason with thyself, both in matter of\par
fame, and in matter of death. For as for the body itself, (the subject\par
of death) wouldest thou know the vileness of it? Turn it about that\par
thou mayest behold it the worst sides upwards as well, as in its more\par
ordinary pleasant shape; how doth it look, when it is old and withered?\par
when sick and pained? when in the act of lust, and fornication? And\par
as for fame. This life is short. Both he that praiseth, and he that is\par
praised; he that remembers, and he that is remembered, will soon be dust\par
and ashes. Besides, it is but in one corner of this part of the world\par
that thou art praised; and yet in this corner, thou hast not the joint\par
praises of all men; no nor scarce of any one constantly. And yet the\par
whole earth itself, what is it but as one point, in regard of the whole\par
world?\par
\par
XX. That which must be the subject of thy consideration, is either the\par
matter itself, or the dogma, or the operation, or the true sense and\par
signification.\par
\par
XXI. Most justly have these things happened unto thee: why dost not\par
thou amend? O but thou hadst rather become good to-morrow, than to be\par
so to-day.\par
\par
XXII. Shall I do it? I will; so the end of my action be to do good unto\par
men. Doth anything by way of cross or adversity happen unto me? I accept\par
it, with reference unto the Gods, and their providence; the fountain of\par
all things, from which whatsoever comes to pass, doth hang and depend.\par
\par
XXIII. By one action judge of the rest: this bathing which usually takes\par
up so much of our time, what is it? Oil, sweat, filth; or the sordes of\par
the body: an excrementitious viscosity, the excrements of oil and other\par
ointments used about the body, and mixed with the sordes of the body:\par
all base and loathsome. And such almost is every part of our life;\par
and every worldly object.\par
\par
XXIV. Lucilla buried Verus; then was Lucilla herself buried by others.\par
So Secunda Maximus, then Secunda herself. So Epitynchanus, Diotimus;\par
then Epitynchanus himself. So Antoninus Pius, Faustina his wife; then\par
Antoninus himself. This is the course of the world. First Celer,\par
Adrianus; then Adrianus himself. And those austere ones; those that\par
foretold other men's deaths; those that were so proud and stately, where\par
are they now? Those austere ones I mean, such as were Charax, and\par
Demetrius the Platonic, and Eudaemon, and others like unto those. They\par
were all but for one day; all dead and gone long since. Some of them no\par
sooner dead, than forgotten. Others soon turned into fables. Of others,\par
even that which was fabulous, is now long since forgotten. This\par
thereafter thou must remember, that whatsoever thou art compounded of,\par
shall soon be dispersed, and that thy life and breath, or thy soul,\par
shall either be no more or shall ranslated (sp.), and appointed to some\par
certain place and station.\par
\par
XXV. The true joy of a man, is to do that which properly belongs unto a\par
man. That which is most proper unto a man, is, first, to be kindly\par
affected towards them that are of the same kind and nature as he is\par
himself to contemn all sensual motions and appetites, to discern rightly\par
all plausible fancies and imaginations, to contemplate the nature of the\par
universe; both it, and things that are done in it. In which kind of\par
contemplation three several relations are to be observed The first, to\par
the apparent secondary cause. The Second to the first original cause,\par
God, from whom originally proceeds whatsoever doth happen in the world.\par
The third and last, to them that we live and converse with: what use may\par
be made of it, to their use and benefit.\par
\par
XXVI. If pain be an evil, either it is in regard of the body; (and that\par
cannot be, because the body of itself is altogether insensible:) or in\par
regard of the soul But it is in the power of the soul, to preserve her\par
own peace and tranquillity, and not to suppose that pain is evil. For\par
all judgment and deliberation; all prosecution, or aversation is from\par
within, whither the sense of evil (except it be let in by opinion)\par
cannot penetrate.\par
\par
XXVII. Wipe off all idle fancies, and say unto thyself incessantly; Now\par
if I will, it is in my power to keep out of this my soul all wickedness,\par
all lust, and concupiscences, all trouble and confusion. But on the\par
contrary to behold and consider all things according to their true\par
nature, and to carry myself towards everything according to its true\par
worth. Remember then this thy power that nature hath given thee.\par
\par
XXVIII. Whether thou speak in the Senate or whether thou speak to any\par
particular, let thy speech In always grave and modest. But thou must\par
not openly and vulgarly observe that sound and exact form of speaking,\par
concerning that which is truly good and truly civil; the vanity of\par
the world, and of worldly men: which otherwise truth and reason doth\par
prescribe.\par
\par
XXIX. Augustus his court; his wife, his daughter, his nephews, his\par
sons-in-law his sister, Agrippa, his kinsmen, his domestics, his\par
friends; Areus, Mæcenas, his slayers of beasts for sacrifice and\par
divination: there thou hast the death of a whole court together. Proceed\par
now on to the rest that have been since that of Augustus. Hath death\par
dwelt with them otherwise, though so many and so stately whilst they\par
lived, than it doth use to deal with any one particular man? Consider\par
now the death of a whole kindred and family, as of that of the Pompeys,\par
as that also that useth to be written upon some monuments, HE WAS THE\par
LAST OF HIS OWN KINDRED. O what care did his predecessors take, that\par
they might leave a successor, yet behold at last one or other must of\par
necessity be THE LAST. Here again therefore consider the death of a\par
whole kindred.\par
\par
XXX. Contract thy whole life to the measure and proportion of one single\par
action. And if in every particular action thou dost perform what is\par
fitting to the utmost of thy power, let it suffice thee. And who can\par
hinder thee, but that thou mayest perform what is fitting? But there may\par
be some outward let and impediment. Not any, that can hinder thee, but\par
that whatsoever thou dost, thou may do it, justly, temperately, and\par
with the praise of God. Yea, but there may be somewhat, whereby some\par
operation or other of thine may be hindered. And then, with that very\par
thing that doth hinder, thou mayest he well pleased, and so by this\par
gentle and equanimious conversion of thy mind unto that which may be,\par
instead of that which at first thou didst intend, in the room of that\par
former action there succeedeth another, which agrees as well with this\par
contraction of thy life, that we now speak of.\par
\par
XXXI. Receive temporal blessings without ostentation, when they are sent\par
and thou shalt be able to part with them with all readiness and facility\par
when they are taken from thee again.\par
\par
XXXII. If ever thou sawest either a hand, or a foot, or a head lying by\par
itself, in some place or other, as cut off from the rest of the body,\par
such must thou conceive him to make himself, as much as in him lieth,\par
that either is offended with anything that is happened, (whatsoever it\par
be) and as it were divides himself from it: or that commits anything\par
against the natural law of mutual correspondence, and society among men:\par
or, he that, commits any act of uncharitableness. Whosoever thou art,\par
thou art such, thou art cast forth I know not whither out of the general\par
unity, which is according to nature. Thou went born indeed a part, but\par
now thou hast cut thyself off. However, herein is matter of joy and\par
exultation, that thou mayst be united again. God hath not granted\par
it unto any other part, that once separated and cut off, it might be\par
reunited, and come together again. But, behold, that GOODNESS how great\par
and immense it is! which hath so much esteemed MAN. As at first he\par
was so made, that he needed not, except he would himself, have divided\par
himself from the whole; so once divided and cut off, IT hath so provided\par
and ordered it, that if he would himself, he might return, and grow\par
together again, and be admitted into its former rank and place of a\par
part, as he was before.\par
\par
XXXIII. As almost all her other faculties and properties the nature of\par
the universe hath imparted unto every reasonable creature, so this in\par
particular we have received from her, that as whatsoever doth oppose\par
itself unto her, and doth withstand her in her purposes and intentions,\par
she doth, though against its will and intention, bring it about to\par
herself, to serve herself of it in the execution of her own destinated\par
ends; and so by this though not intended co-operation of it with herself\par
makes it part of herself whether it will or no. So may every reasonable\par
creature, what crosses and impediments soever it meets with in the\par
course of this mortal life, it may use them as fit and proper objects,\par
to the furtherance of whatsoever it intended and absolutely proposed\par
unto itself as its natural end and happiness.\par
\par
XXXIV. Let not the general representation unto thyself of the\par
wretchedness of this our mortal life, trouble thee. Let not thy mind\par
wander up and down, and heap together in her thoughts the many troubles\par
and grievous calamities which thou art as subject unto as any other. But\par
as everything in particular doth happen, put this question unto thyself,\par
and say: What is it that in this present matter, seems unto thee so\par
intolerable? For thou wilt be ashamed to confess it. Then upon this\par
presently call to mind, that neither that which is future, nor that\par
which is past can hurt thee; but that only which is present. (And that\par
also is much lessened, if thou dost lightly circumscribe it:) and then\par
check thy mind if for so little a while, (a mere instant), it cannot\par
hold out with patience.\par
\par
XXXV. What? are either Panthea or Pergamus abiding to this day by their\par
masters' tombs? or either Chabrias or Diotimus by that of Adrianus? O\par
foolery! For what if they did, would their masters be sensible of It? or\par
if sensible, would they be glad of it? or if glad, were these immortal?\par
Was not it appointed unto them also (both men and women,) to become\par
old in time, and then to die? And these once dead, what would become of\par
these former? And when all is done, what is all this for, but for a mere\par
bag of blood and corruption?\par
\par
XXXVI. If thou beest quick-sighted, be so in matter of judgment, and\par
best discretion, saith he.\par
\par
XXXVII. In the whole constitution of man, I see not any virtue contrary\par
to justice, whereby it may be resisted and opposed. But one whereby\par
pleasure and voluptuousness may be resisted and opposed, I see:\par
continence.\par
\par
XXXVIII. If thou canst but withdraw conceit and opinion concerning that\par
which may seem hurtful and offensive, thou thyself art as safe, as safe\par
may be. Thou thyself? and who is that? Thy reason. 'Yea, but I am not\par
reason.' Well, be it so. However, let not thy reason or understanding\par
admit of grief, and if there be anything in thee that is grieved, let\par
that, (whatsoever it be,) conceive its own grief, if it can.\par
\par
XXXIX. That which is a hindrance of the senses, is an evil to the\par
sensitive nature. That which is a hindrance of the appetitive and\par
prosecutive faculty, is an evil to the sensitive nature. As of the\par
sensitive, so of the vegetative constitution, whatsoever is a hindrance\par
unto it, is also in that respect an evil unto the same. And so likewise,\par
whatsoever is a hindrance unto the mind and understanding, must needs\par
be the proper evil of the reasonable nature. Now apply all those things\par
unto thyself. Do either pain or pleasure seize on thee? Let the senses\par
look to that. Hast thou met with Some obstacle or other in thy purpose\par
and intention? If thou didst propose without due reservation and\par
exception now hath thy reasonable part received a blow indeed But if in\par
general thou didst propose unto thyself what soever might be, thou art\par
not thereby either hurt, nor properly hindered. For in those things that\par
properly belong unto the mind, she cannot be hindered by any man. It\par
is not fire, nor iron; nor the power of a tyrant nor the power of a\par
slandering tongue; nor anything else that can penetrate into her.\par
\par
XL. If once round and solid, there is no fear that ever it will change.\par
\par
XLI. Why should I grieve myself; who never did willingly grieve any\par
other! One thing rejoices one and another thing another. As for me, this\par
is my joy, if my understanding be right and sound, as neither averse\par
from any man, nor refusing any of those things which as a man I am\par
subject unto; if I can look upon all things in the world meekly and\par
kindly; accept all things and carry myself towards everything according\par
to to true worth of the thing itself.\par
\par
XLII. This time that is now present, bestow thou upon thyself. They that\par
rather hunt for fame after death, do not consider, that those men that\par
shall be hereafter, will be even such, as these whom now they can so\par
hardly bear with. And besides they also will be mortal men. But to\par
consider the thing in itself, if so many with so many voices, shall make\par
such and such a sound, or shall have such and such an opinion concerning\par
thee, what is it to thee?\par
\par
XLIII. Take me and throw me where thou wilt: I am indifferent. For there\par
also I shall have that spirit which is within me propitious; that is\par
well pleased and fully contented both in that constant disposition, and\par
with those particular actions, which to its own proper constitution are\par
suitable and agreeable.\par
\par
XLIV. Is this then a thing of that worth, that for it my soul should\par
suffer, and become worse than it was? as either basely dejected, or\par
disordinately affected, or confounded within itself, or terrified? What\par
can there be, that thou shouldest so much esteem?\par
\par
XLV. Nothing can happen unto thee, which is not incidental unto thee, as\par
thou art a man. As nothing can happen either to an ox, a vine, or to\par
a stone, which is not incidental unto them; unto every one in his own\par
kind. If therefore nothing can happen unto anything, which is not both\par
usual and natural; why art thou displeased? Sure the common nature\par
of all would not bring anything upon any, that were intolerable. If\par
therefore it be a thing external that causes thy grief, know, that it is\par
not that properly that doth cause it, but thine own conceit and opinion\par
concerning the thing: which thou mayest rid thyself of, when thou wilt.\par
But if it be somewhat that is amiss in thine own disposition, that doth\par
grieve thee, mayest thou not rectify thy moral tenets and opinions. But\par
if it grieve thee, that thou doest not perform that which seemeth unto\par
thee right and just, why doest not thou choose rather to perform it than\par
to grieve? But somewhat that is stronger than thyself doth hinder thee.\par
Let it not grieve thee then, if it be not thy fault that the thing is\par
not performed. 'Yea but it is a thing of that nature, as that thy life\par
is not worth the while, except it may be performed.' If it be so, upon\par
condition that thou be kindly and lovingly disposed towards all men,\par
thou mayest be gone. For even then, as much as at any time, art thou in\par
a very good estate of performance, when thou doest die in charity with\par
those, that are an obstacle unto thy performance.\par
\par
XLVI. Remember that thy mind is of that nature as that it becometh\par
altogether unconquerable, when once recollected in herself, she seeks no\par
other content than this, that she cannot be forced: yea though it so\par
fall out, that it be even against reason itself, that it cloth bandy.\par
How much less when by the help of reason she is able to judge of things\par
with discretion? And therefore let thy chief fort and place of defence\par
be, a mind free from passions. A stronger place, (whereunto to make his\par
refuge, and so to become impregnable) and better fortified than this,\par
hath no man. He that seeth not this is unlearned. He that seeth it, and\par
betaketh not himself to this place of refuge, is unhappy.\par
\par
XLVII. Keep thyself to the first bare and naked apprehensions of things,\par
as they present themselves unto thee, and add not unto them. It is\par
reported unto thee, that such a one speaketh ill of thee. Well; that he\par
speaketh ill of thee, so much is reported. But that thou art hurt\par
thereby, is not reported: that is the addition of opinion, which thou\par
must exclude. I see that my child is sick. That he is sick, I see, but\par
that he is in danger of his life also, I see it not. Thus thou must use\par
to keep thyself to the first motions and apprehensions of things, as\par
they present themselves outwardly; and add not unto them from within\par
thyself through mere conceit and opinion. Or rather add unto them: hut\par
as one that understandeth the true nature of all things that happen in\par
the world.\par
\par
XLVIII. Is the cucumber bitter? set it away. Brambles are in the way?\par
avoid them. Let this suffice. Add not presently speaking unto thyself,\par
What serve these things for in the world? For, this, one that is\par
acquainted with the mysteries of nature, will laugh at thee for it; as a\par
carpenter would or a shoemaker, if meeting in either of their shops with\par
some shavings, or small remnants of their work, thou shouldest blame\par
them for it. And yet those men, it is not for want of a place where to\par
throw them that they keep them in their shops for a while: but the\par
nature of the universe hath no such out-place; but herein doth consist\par
the wonder of her art and skill, that she having once circumscribed\par
herself within some certain bounds and limits, whatsoever is within her\par
that seems either corrupted, or old, or unprofitable, she can change it\par
into herself, and of these very things can make new things; so that she\par
needeth not to seek elsewhere out of herself either for a new supply of\par
matter and substance, or for a place where to throw out whatsoever is\par
irrecoverably putrid and corrupt. Thus she, as for place, so for matter\par
and art, is herself sufficient unto herself.\par
\par
XLIX. Not to be slack and negligent; or loose, and wanton in thy\par
actions; nor contentious, and troublesome in thy conversation; nor to\par
rove and wander in thy fancies and imaginations. Not basely to contract\par
thy soul; nor boisterously to sally out with it, or furiously to launch\par
out as it were, nor ever to want employment.\par
\par
L. 'They kill me, they cut my flesh; they persecute my person with\par
curses.' What then? May not thy mind for all this continue pure,\par
prudent, temperate, just? As a fountain of sweet and clear water, though\par
she be cursed by some stander by, yet do her springs nevertheless still\par
run as sweet and clear as before; yea though either dirt or dung be\par
thrown in, yet is it no sooner thrown, than dispersed, and she cleared.\par
She cannot be dyed or infected by it. What then must I do, that I\par
may have within myself an overflowing fountain, and not a well? Beget\par
thyself by continual pains and endeavours to true liberty with charity,\par
and true simplicity and modesty.\par
\par
LI. He that knoweth not what the world is, knoweth not where he himself\par
is. And he that knoweth not what the world was made for, cannot possibly\par
know either what are the qualities, or what is the nature of the world.\par
Now he that in either of these is to seek, for what he himself was made\par
is ignorant also. What then dost thou think of that man, who proposeth\par
unto himself, as a matter of great moment, the noise and applause\par
of men, who both where they are, and what they are themselves, are\par
altogether ignorant? Dost thou desire to be commended of that man, who\par
thrice in one hour perchance, doth himself curse himself? Dost thou\par
desire to please him, who pleaseth not himself? or dost thou think that\par
he pleaseth himself, who doth use to repent himself almost of everything\par
that he doth?\par
\par
LII. Not only now henceforth to have a common breath, or to hold\par
correspondency of breath, with that air, that compasseth us about; but\par
to have a common mind, or to hold correspondency of mind also with that\par
rational substance, which compasseth all things. For, that also is of\par
itself, and of its own nature (if a man can but draw it in as he should)\par
everywhere diffused; and passeth through all things, no less than the\par
air doth, if a man can but suck it in.\par
\par
LIII. Wickedness in general doth not hurt the world. Particular\par
wickedness doth not hurt any other: only unto him it is hurtful,\par
whosoever he be that offends, unto whom in great favour and mercy it is\par
granted, that whensoever he himself shall but first desire it, he may be\par
presently delivered of it. Unto my free-will my neighbour's free-will,\par
whoever he be, (as his life, or his bode), is altogether indifferent.\par
For though we are all made one for another, yet have our minds and\par
understandings each of them their own proper and limited jurisdiction.\par
For else another man's wickedness might be my evil which God would not\par
have, that it might not be in another man's power to make me unhappy:\par
which nothing now can do but mine own wickedness.\par
\par
LIV. The sun seemeth to be shed abroad. And indeed it is diffused but\par
not effused. For that diffusion of it is a τάσις or an extension. For\par
therefore are the beams of it called ἀκτῖνες from the word ἐκτείνεσθαι\par
to be stretched out and extended. Now what a sunbeam is, thou mayest\par
know if thou observe the light of the sun, when through some narrow\par
hole it pierceth into some room that is dark. For it is always in a\par
direct line. And as by any solid body, that it meets with in the way\par
that is not penetrable by air, it is divided and abrupted, and yet\par
neither slides off, or falls down, but stayeth there nevertheless: such\par
must the diffusion in the mind be; not an effusion, but an extension.\par
What obstacles and impediments soever she meeteth within her way, she\par
must not violently, and by way of an impetuous onset light upon them;\par
neither must she fall down; but she must stand, and give light unto\par
that which doth admit of it. For as for that which doth not, it is its\par
own fault and loss, if it bereave itself of her light.\par
\par
LV. He that feareth death, either feareth that he shall have no sense at\par
all, or that his senses will not be the same. Whereas, he should rather\par
comfort himself, that either no sense at all, and so no sense of evil;\par
or if any sense, then another life, and so no death properly.\par
\par
LVI. All men are made one for another: either then teach them better, or\par
bear with them.\par
\par
LVII. The motion of the mind is not as the motion of a dart. For\par
the mind when it is wary and cautelous, and by way of diligent\par
circumspection turneth herself many ways, may then as well be said to\par
go straight on to the object, as when it useth no such circumspection.\par
\par
\par
LVIII. To pierce and penetrate into the estate of every one's\par
understanding that thou hast to do with: as also to make the estate of\par
thine own open, and penetrable to any other.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
THE NINTH BOOK\par
\par
\par
I. He that is unjust, is also impious. For the nature of the universe,\par
having made all reasonable creatures one for another, to the end that\par
they should do one another good; more or less according to the several\par
persons and occasions but in nowise hurt one another: it is manifest\par
that he that doth transgress against this her will, is guilty of impiety\par
towards the most ancient and venerable of all the deities. For the\par
nature of the universe, is the nature the common parent of all, and\par
therefore piously to be observed of all things that are, and that which\par
now is, to whatsoever first was, and gave it its being, hath relation\par
of blood and kindred. She is also called truth and is the first cause\par
of all truths. He therefore that willingly and wittingly doth lie, is\par
impious in that he doth receive, and so commit injustice: but he that\par
against his will, in that he disagreeth from the nature of the universe,\par
and in that striving with the nature of the world he doth in his\par
particular, violate the general order of the world. For he doth no\par
better than strive and war against it, who contrary to his own nature\par
applieth himself to that which is contrary to truth. For nature had\par
before furnished him with instincts and opportunities sufficient for the\par
attainment of it; which he having hitherto neglected, is not now able\par
to discern that which is false from that which is true. He also that\par
pursues after pleasures, as that which is truly good and flies from\par
pains, as that which is truly evil: is impious. For such a one must of\par
necessity oftentimes accuse that common nature, as distributing many\par
things both unto the evil, and unto the good, not according to the\par
deserts of either: as unto the bad oftentimes pleasures, and the causes\par
of pleasures; so unto the good, pains, and the occasions of pains.\par
Again, he that feareth pains and crosses in this world, feareth some of\par
those things which some time or other must needs happen in the world.\par
And that we have already showed to be impious. And he that pursueth\par
after pleasures, will not spare, to compass his desires, to do that\par
which is unjust, and that is manifestly impious. Now those things which\par
unto nature are equally indifferent (for she had not created both, both\par
pain and pleasure, if both had not been unto her equally indifferent):\par
they that will live according to nature, must in those things (as being\par
of the same mind and disposition that she is) be as equally indifferent.\par
Whosoever therefore in either matter of pleasure and pain; death and\par
life; honour and dishonour, (which things nature in the administration\par
of the world, indifferently doth make use of), is not as indifferent,\par
it is apparent that he is impious. When I say that common nature\par
doth indifferently make use of them, my meaning is, that they happen\par
indifferently in the ordinary course of things, which by a necessary\par
consequence, whether as principal or accessory, come to pass in the\par
world, according to that first and ancient deliberation of Providence,\par
by which she from some certain beginning, did resolve upon the creation\par
of such a world, conceiving then in her womb as it were some certain\par
rational generative seeds and faculties of things future, whether\par
subjects, changes, successions; both such and such, and just so many.\par
\par
II. It were indeed more happy and comfortable, for a man to depart out\par
of this world, having lived all his life long clear from all falsehood,\par
dissimulation, voluptuousness, and pride. But if this cannot be, yet it\par
is some comfort for a man joyfully to depart as weary, and out of love\par
with those; rather than to desire to live, and to continue long in those\par
wicked courses. Hath not yet experience taught thee to fly from the\par
plague? For a far greater plague is the corruption of the mind, than any\par
certain change and distemper of the common air can be. This is a plague\par
of creatures, as they are living creatures; but that of men as they are\par
men or reasonable.\par
\par
III. Thou must not in matter of death carry thyself scornfully, but as\par
one that is well pleased with it, as being one of those things that\par
nature hath appointed. For what thou dost conceive of these, of a boy to\par
become a young man, to wax old, to grow, to ripen, to get teeth, or a\par
beard, or grey hairs to beget, to bear, or to be delivered; or what\par
other action soever it be, that is natural unto man according to the\par
several seasons of his life; such a thing is it also to be dissolved. It\par
is therefore the part of a wise man, in matter of death, not in any wise\par
to carry himself either violently, or proudly but patiently to wait for\par
it, as one of nature's operations: that with the same mind as now thou\par
dost expect when that which yet is but an embryo in thy wife's belly\par
shall come forth, thou mayst expect also when thy soul shall fall off\par
from that outward coat or skin: wherein as a child in the belly it lieth\par
involved and shut up. But thou desirest a more popular, and though not\par
so direct and philosophical, yet a very powerful and penetrative recipe\par
against the fear of death, nothing can make they more willing to part\par
with thy life, than if thou shalt consider, both what the subjects\par
themselves are that thou shalt part with, and what manner of disposition\par
thou shalt no more have to do with. True it is, that, offended with them\par
thou must not be by no means, but take care of them, and meekly bear\par
with them However, this thou mayst remember, that whensoever it happens\par
that thou depart, it shall not be from men that held the same opinions\par
that thou dost. For that indeed, (if it were so) is the only thing that\par
might make thee averse from death, and willing to continue here, if it\par
were thy hap to live with men that had obtained the same belief that\par
thou hast. But now, what a toil it is for thee to live with men of\par
different opinions, thou seest: so that thou hast rather occasion to\par
say, Hasten, I thee pray, O Death; lest I also in time forget myself.\par
\par
IV. He that sinneth, sinneth unto himself. He that is unjust, hurts\par
himself, in that he makes himself worse than he was before. Not he only\par
that committeth, but he also that omitteth something, is oftentimes\par
unjust.\par
\par
V. If my present apprehension of the object be right, and my present\par
action charitable, and this, towards whatsoever doth proceed from God,\par
be my present disposition, to be well pleased with it, it sufficeth.\par
\par
VI. To wipe away fancy, to use deliberation, to quench concupiscence, to\par
keep the mind free to herself.\par
\par
VII. Of all unreasonable creatures, there is but one unreasonable soul;\par
and of all that are reasonable, but one reasonable soul, divided betwixt\par
them all. As of all earthly things there is but one earth, and but one\par
light that we see by; and but one air that we breathe in, as many as\par
either breathe or see. Now whatsoever partakes of some common thing,\par
naturally affects and inclines unto that whereof it is part, being of\par
one kind and nature with it. Whatsoever is earthly, presseth downwards\par
to the common earth. Whatsoever is liquid, would flow together. And\par
whatsoever is airy, would be together likewise. So that without some\par
obstacle, and some kind of violence, they cannot well be kept asunder.\par
Whatsoever is fiery, doth not only by reason of the elementary fire tend\par
upwards; but here also is so ready to join, and to burn together, that\par
whatsoever doth want sufficient moisture to make resistance, is easily\par
set on fire. Whatsoever therefore is partaker of that reasonable common\par
nature, naturally doth as much and more long after his own kind. For by\par
how much in its own nature it excels all other things, by so much more\par
is it desirous to be joined and united unto that, which is of its own\par
nature. As for unreasonable creatures then, they had not long been, but\par
presently begun among them swarms, and flocks, and broods of young ones,\par
and a kind of mutual love and affection. For though but unreasonable,\par
yet a kind of soul these had, and therefore was that natural desire of\par
union more strong and intense in them, as in creatures of a more\par
excellent nature, than either in plants, or stones, or trees. But among\par
reasonable creatures, begun commonwealths, friendships, families, public\par
meetings, and even in their wars, conventions, and truces. Now among\par
them that were yet of a more excellent nature, as the stars and planets,\par
though by their nature far distant one from another, yet even among them\par
began some mutual correspondency and unity. So proper is it to\par
excellency in a high degree to affect unity, as that even in things so\par
far distant, it could operate unto a mutual sympathy. But now behold,\par
what is now come to pass. Those creatures that are reasonable, are now\par
the only creatures that have forgotten their natural affection and\par
inclination of one towards another. Among them alone of all other things\par
that are of one kind, there is not to be found a general disposition to\par
flow together. But though they fly from nature, yet are they stopt in\par
their course, and apprehended. Do they what they can, nature doth\par
prevail. And so shalt thou confess, if thou dost observe it. For sooner\par
mayst thou find a thing earthly, where no earthly thing is, than find a\par
man that naturally can live by himself alone.\par
\par
VIII. Man, God, the world, every one in their kind, bear some fruits.\par
All things have their proper time to bear. Though by custom, the word\par
itself is in a manner become proper unto the vine, and the like, yet is\par
it so nevertheless, as we have said. As for reason, that beareth both\par
common fruit for the use of others; and peculiar, which itself doth\par
enjoy. Reason is of a diffusive nature, what itself is in itself, it\par
begets in others, and so doth multiply.\par
\par
IX. Either teach them better if it be in thy power; or if it be not,\par
remember that for this use, to bear with them patiently, was mildness\par
and goodness granted unto thee. The Gods themselves are good unto such;\par
yea and in some things, (as in matter of health, of wealth, of honour,)\par
are content often to further their endeavours: so good and gracious are\par
they. And mightest thou not be so too? or, tell me, what doth hinder\par
thee?\par
\par
X. Labour not as one to whom it is appointed to be wretched, nor as one\par
that either would be pitied, or admired; but let this be thine only care\par
and desire; so always and in all things to prosecute or to forbear, as\par
the law of charity, or mutual society doth require.\par
\par
XI. This day I did come out of all my trouble. Nay I have cast out all\par
my trouble; it should rather be for that which troubled thee, whatsoever\par
it was, was not without anywhere that thou shouldest come out of it, but\par
within in thine own opinions, from whence it must be cast out, before\par
thou canst truly and constantly be at ease.\par
\par
XII. All those things, for matter of experience are usual and ordinary;\par
for their continuance but for a day; and for their matter, most base and\par
filthy. As they were in the days of those whom we have buried, so are\par
they now also, and no otherwise.\par
\par
XIII. The things themselves that affect us, they stand without doors,\par
neither knowing anything themselves nor able to utter anything unto\par
others concerning themselves. What then is it, that passeth verdict on\par
them? The understanding.\par
\par
XIV. As virtue and wickedness consist not in passion, but in action; so\par
neither doth the true good or evil of a reasonable charitable man\par
consist in passion, but in operation and action.\par
\par
XV. To the stone that is cast up, when it comes down it is no hurt unto\par
it; as neither benefit, when it doth ascend.\par
\par
XVI. Sift their minds and understandings, and behold what men they be,\par
whom thou dost stand in fear of what they shall judge of thee, what they\par
themselves judge of themselves.\par
\par
XVII. All things that are in the world, are always in the estate\par
of alteration. Thou also art in a perpetual change, yea and under\par
corruption too, in some part: and so is the whole world.\par
\par
XVIII. it is not thine, but another man's sin. Why should it trouble\par
thee? Let him look to it, whose sin it is.\par
\par
XIX. Of an operation and of a purpose there is an ending, or of an\par
action and of a purpose we say commonly, that it is at an end: from\par
opinion also there is an absolute cessation, which is as it were the\par
death of it. In all this there is no hurt. Apply this now to a man's\par
age, as first, a child; then a youth, then a young man, then an old man;\par
every change from one age to another is a kind of death And all this\par
while here no matter of grief yet. Pass now unto that life first, that\par
which thou livedst under thy grandfather, then under thy mother, then\par
under thy father. And thus when through the whole course of thy life\par
hitherto thou hast found and observed many alterations, many changes,\par
many kinds of endings and cessations, put this question to thyself What\par
matter of grief or sorrow dost thou find in any of these? Or what doest\par
thou suffer through any of these? If in none of these, then neither\par
in the ending and consummation of thy whole life, which is also but a\par
cessation and change.\par
\par
XX. As occasion shall require, either to thine own understanding, or to\par
that of the universe, or to his, whom thou hast now to do with, let thy\par
refuge be with all speed. To thine own, that it resolve upon nothing\par
against justice. To that of the universe, that thou mayest remember,\par
part of whom thou art. Of his, that thou mayest consider whether in the\par
estate of ignorance, or of knowledge. And then also must thou call to\par
mind, that he is thy kinsman.\par
\par
XXI. As thou thyself, whoever thou art, were made for the perfection and\par
consummation, being a member of it, of a common society; so must every\par
action of thine tend to the perfection and consummation of a life that\par
is truly sociable. What action soever of thine therefore that either\par
immediately or afar off, hath not reference to the common good, that is\par
an exorbitant and disorderly action; yea it is seditious; as one among\par
the people who from such and such a consent and unity, should factiously\par
divide and separate himself.\par
\par
XXII. Children's anger, mere babels; wretched souls bearing up dead\par
bodies, that they may not have their fall so soon: even as it is in that\par
common dirge song.\par
\par
XXIII. Go to the quality of the cause from which the effect doth\par
proceed. Behold it by itself bare and naked, separated from all that is\par
material. Then consider the utmost bounds of time that that cause, thus\par
and thus qualified, can subsist and abide.\par
\par
XXIV. Infinite are the troubles and miseries, that thou hast already\par
been put to, by reason of this only, because that for all happiness\par
it did not suffice thee, or, that thou didst not account it sufficient\par
happiness, that thy understanding did operate according to its natural\par
constitution.\par
\par
XXV. When any shall either impeach thee with false accusations, or\par
hatefully reproach thee, or shall use any such carriage towards thee,\par
get thee presently to their minds and understandings, and look in them,\par
and behold what manner of men they be. Thou shalt see, that there is no\par
such occasion why it should trouble thee, what such as they are think of\par
thee. Yet must thou love them still, for by nature they are thy friends.\par
And the Gods themselves, in those things that they seek from them as\par
matters of great moment, are well content, all manner of ways, as by\par
dreams and oracles, to help them as well as others.\par
\par
XXVI. Up and down, from one age to another, go the ordinary things of\par
the world; being still the same. And either of everything in particular\par
before it come to pass, the mind of the universe doth consider with\par
itself and deliberate: and if so, then submit for shame unto the\par
determination of such an excellent understanding: or once for all it did\par
resolve upon all things in general; and since that whatsoever happens,\par
happens by a necessary consequence, and all things indivisibly in a\par
manner and inseparably hold one of another. In sum, either there is a\par
God, and then all is well; or if all things go by chance and fortune,\par
yet mayest thou use thine own providence in those things that concern\par
thee properly; and then art thou well.\par
\par
XXVII. Within a while the earth shall cover us all, and then she herself\par
shall have her change. And then the course will be, from one period of\par
eternity unto another, and so a perpetual eternity. Now can any man\par
that shall consider with himself in his mind the several rollings or\par
successions of so many changes and alterations, and the swiftness of all\par
these rulings; can he otherwise but contemn in his heart and despise\par
all worldly things? The cause of the universe is as it were a strong\par
torrent, it carrieth all away.\par
\par
XXVIII. And these your professed politicians, the only true practical\par
philosophers of the world, (as they think of themselves) so full of\par
affected gravity, or such professed lovers of virtue and honesty, what\par
wretches be they in very deed; how vile and contemptible in themselves?\par
O man! what ado doest thou keep? Do what thy nature doth now require.\par
Resolve upon it, if thou mayest: and take no thought, whether anybody\par
shall know it or no. Yea, but sayest thou, I must not expect a Plato's\par
commonwealth. If they profit though never so little, I must be content;\par
and think much even of that little progress. Doth then any of them\par
forsake their former false opinions that I should think they profit? For\par
without a change of opinions, alas! what is all that ostentation, but\par
mere wretchedness of slavish minds, that groan privately, and yet would\par
make a show of obedience to reason, and truth? Go too now and tell me\par
of Alexander and Philippus, and Demetrius Phalereus. Whether they\par
understood what the common nature requireth, and could rule themselves\par
or no, they know best themselves. But if they kept a life, and\par
swaggered; I (God be thanked) am not bound to imitate them. The effect\par
of true philosophy is, unaffected simplicity and modesty. Persuade me\par
not to ostentation and vainglory.\par
\par
XXIX. From some high place as it were to look down, and to behold\par
here flocks, and there sacrifices, without number; and all kind of\par
navigation; some in a rough and stormy sea, and some in a calm: the\par
general differences, or different estates of things, some, that are now\par
first upon being; the several and mutual relations of those things that\par
are together; and some other things that are at their last. Their lives\par
also, who were long ago, and theirs who shall be hereafter, and the\par
present estate and life of those many nations of barbarians that are\par
now in the world, thou must likewise consider in thy mind. And how many\par
there be, who never so much as heard of thy name, how many that will\par
soon forget it; how many who but even now did commend thee, within a\par
very little while perchance will speak ill of thee. So that neither\par
fame, nor honour, nor anything else that this world doth afford, is\par
worth the while. The sum then of all; whatsoever doth happen unto thee,\par
whereof God is the cause, to accept it contentedly: whatsoever thou\par
doest, whereof thou thyself art the cause, to do it justly: which will\par
be, if both in thy resolution and in thy action thou have no further\par
end, than to do good unto others, as being that, which by thy natural\par
constitution, as a man, thou art bound unto.\par
\par
XXX. Many of those things that trouble and straiten thee, it is in thy\par
power to cut off, as wholly depending from mere conceit and opinion; and\par
then thou shalt have room enough.\par
\par
XXXI. To comprehend the whole world together in thy mind, and the whole\par
course of this present age to represent it unto thyself, and to fix thy\par
thoughts upon the sudden change of every particular object. How short\par
the time is from the generation of anything, unto the dissolution of\par
the same; but how immense and infinite both that which was before the\par
generation, and that which after the generation of it shall be. All\par
things that thou seest, will soon be perished, and they that see their\par
corruptions, will soon vanish away themselves. He that dieth a hundred\par
years old, and he that dieth young, shall come all to one.\par
\par
XXXII. What are their minds and understandings; and what the things that\par
they apply themselves unto: what do they love, and what do they hate\par
for? Fancy to thyself the estate of their souls openly to be seen. When\par
they think they hurt them shrewdly, whom they speak ill of; and when\par
they think they do them a very good turn, whom they commend and extol: O\par
how full are they then of conceit, and opinion!\par
\par
XXXIII. Loss and corruption, is in very deed nothing else but change and\par
alteration; and that is it, which the nature of the universe doth most\par
delight in, by which, and according to which, whatsoever is done, is\par
well done. For that was the estate of worldly things from the beginning,\par
and so shall it ever be. Or wouldest thou rather say, that all things\par
in the world have gone ill from the beginning for so many ages, and\par
shall ever go ill? And then among so many deities, could no divine power\par
be found all this while, that could rectify the things of the world? Or\par
is the world, to incessant woes and miseries, for ever condemned?\par
\par
XXXIV. How base and putrid, every common matter is! Water, dust, and\par
from the mixture of these bones, and all that loathsome stuff that our\par
bodies do consist of: so subject to be infected, and corrupted. And\par
again those other things that are so much prized and admired, as marble\par
stones, what are they, but as it were the kernels of the earth? gold and\par
silver, what are they, but as the more gross faeces of the earth? Thy\par
most royal apparel, for matter, it is but as it were the hair of a silly\par
sheep, and for colour, the very blood of a shell-fish; of this nature\par
are all other things. Thy life itself, is some such thing too; a mere\par
exhalation of blood: and it also, apt to be changed into some other\par
common thing.\par
\par
XXXV. Will this querulousness, this murmuring, this complaining and\par
dissembling never be at an end? What then is it, that troubleth thee?\par
Doth any new thing happen unto thee? What doest thou so wonder at? At\par
the cause, or the matter? Behold either by itself, is either of that\par
weight and moment indeed? And besides these, there is not anything. But\par
thy duty towards the Gods also, it is time thou shouldst acquit thyself\par
of it with more goodness and simplicity.\par
\par
XXXVI. It is all one to see these things for a hundred of years together\par
or but for three years.\par
\par
XXXVII. If he have sinned, his is the harm, not mine. But perchance he\par
hath not.\par
\par
XXXVIII. Either all things by the providence of reason happen unto every\par
particular, as a part of one general body; and then it is against reason\par
that a part should complain of anything that happens for the good of the\par
whole; or if, according to Epicurus, atoms be the cause of all things\par
and that life be nothing else but an accidentary confusion of things,\par
and death nothing else, but a mere dispersion and so of all other\par
things: what doest thou trouble thyself for?\par
\par
XXXIX. Sayest thou unto that rational part, Thou art dead; corruption\par
hath taken hold on thee? Doth it then also void excrements? Doth it like\par
either oxen, or sheep, graze or feed; that it also should be mortal, as\par
well as the body?\par
\par
XL. Either the Gods can do nothing for us at all, or they can still and\par
allay all the distractions and distempers of thy mind. If they can do\par
nothing, why doest thou pray? If they can, why wouldst not thou rather\par
pray, that they will grant unto thee, that thou mayst neither fear, nor\par
lust after any of those worldly things which cause these distractions\par
and distempers of it? Why not rather, that thou mayst not at either\par
their absence or presence, be grieved and discontented: than either that\par
thou mayst obtain them, or that thou mayst avoid them? For certainly\par
it must needs be, that if the Gods can help us in anything, they may in\par
this kind also. But thou wilt say perchance, 'In those things the Gods\par
have given me my liberty: and it is in mine own power to do what I\par
will.' But if thou mayst use this liberty, rather to set thy mind at\par
true liberty, than wilfully with baseness and servility of mind to\par
affect those things, which either to compass or to avoid is not in thy\par
power, wert not thou better? And as for the Gods, who hath told thee,\par
that they may not help us up even in those things that they have put in\par
our own power? whether it be so or no, thou shalt soon perceive, if\par
thou wilt but try thyself and pray. One prayeth that he may compass his\par
desire, to lie with such or such a one, pray thou that thou mayst not\par
lust to lie with her. Another how he may be rid of such a one; pray thou\par
that thou mayst so patiently bear with him, as that thou have no such\par
need to be rid of him. Another, that he may not lose his child. Pray\par
thou that thou mayst not fear to lose him. To this end and purpose, let\par
all thy prayer be, and see what will be the event.\par
\par
XLI. 'In my sickness' (saith Epicurus of himself:) 'my discourses were\par
not concerning the nature of my disease, neither was that, to them that\par
came to visit me, the subject of my talk; but in the consideration and\par
contemplation of that, which was of especial weight and moment, was all\par
my time bestowed and spent, and among others in this very thing, how my\par
mind, by a natural and unavoidable sympathy partaking in some sort with\par
the present indisposition of my body, might nevertheless keep herself\par
free from trouble, and in present possession of her own proper\par
happiness. Neither did I leave the ordering of my body to the physicians\par
altogether to do with me what they would, as though I expected any\par
great matter from them, or as though I thought it a matter of such great\par
consequence, by their means to recover my health: for my present estate,\par
methought, liked me very well, and gave me good content.' Whether\par
therefore in sickness (if thou chance to sicken) or in what other kind\par
of extremity soever, endeavour thou also to be in thy mind so affected,\par
as he doth report of himself: not to depart from thy philosophy for\par
anything that can befall thee, nor to give ear to the discourses of\par
silly people, and mere naturalists.\par
\par
XLII. It is common to all trades and professions to mind and intend that\par
only, which now they are about, and the instrument whereby they work.\par
\par
XLIII. When at any time thou art offended with any one's impudency, put\par
presently this question to thyself: 'What? Is it then possible, that\par
there should not be any impudent men in the world! Certainly it is not\par
possible.' Desire not then that which is impossible. For this one, (thou\par
must think) whosoever he be, is one of those impudent ones, that\par
the world cannot be without. So of the subtile and crafty, so of the\par
perfidious, so of every one that offendeth, must thou ever be ready to\par
reason with thyself. For whilst in general thou dost thus reason with\par
thyself, that the kind of them must needs be in the world, thou wilt be\par
the better able to use meekness towards every particular. This also\par
thou shalt find of very good use, upon every such occasion, presently\par
to consider with thyself, what proper virtue nature hath furnished man\par
with, against such a vice, or to encounter with a disposition vicious\par
in this kind. As for example, against the unthankful, it hath given\par
goodness and meekness, as an antidote, and so against another vicious\par
in another kind some other peculiar faculty. And generally, is it not\par
in thy power to instruct him better, that is in an error? For whosoever\par
sinneth, doth in that decline from his purposed end, and is certainly\par
deceived, And again, what art thou the worse for his sin? For thou shalt\par
not find that any one of these, against whom thou art incensed, hath in\par
very deed done anything whereby thy mind (the only true subject of\par
thy hurt and evil) can be made worse than it was. And what a matter of\par
either grief or wonder is this, if he that is unlearned, do the deeds of\par
one that is unlearned? Should not thou rather blame thyself, who, when\par
upon very good grounds of reason, thou mightst have thought it very\par
probable, that such a thing would by such a one be committed, didst not\par
only not foresee it, but moreover dost wonder at it, that such a thing\par
should be. But then especially, when thou dost find fault with either an\par
unthankful, or a false man, must thou reflect upon thyself. For without\par
all question, thou thyself art much in fault, if either of one that were\par
of such a disposition, thou didst expect that he should be true unto\par
thee: or when unto any thou didst a good turn, thou didst not there\par
bound thy thoughts, as one that had obtained his end; nor didst not\par
think that from the action itself thou hadst received a full reward of\par
the good that thou hadst done. For what wouldst thou have more? Unto him\par
that is a man, thou hast done a good turn: doth not that suffice thee?\par
What thy nature required, that hast thou done. Must thou be rewarded for\par
it? As if either the eye for that it seeth, or the feet that they go,\par
should require satisfaction. For as these being by nature appointed for\par
such an use, can challenge no more, than that they may work according\par
to their natural constitution: so man being born to do good unto others\par
whensoever he doth a real good unto any by helping them out of error; or\par
though but in middle things, as in matter of wealth, life, preferment,\par
and the like, doth help to further their desires he doth that for which\par
he was made, and therefore can require no more.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
THE TENTH BOOK\par
\par
\par
I. O my soul, the time I trust will be, when thou shalt be good, simple,\par
single, more open and visible, than that body by which it is enclosed.\par
Thou wilt one day be sensible of their happiness, whose end is love, and\par
their affections dead to all worldly things. Thou shalt one day be full,\par
and in want of no external thing: not seeking pleasure from anything,\par
either living or insensible, that this world can afford; neither wanting\par
time for the continuation of thy pleasure, nor place and opportunity,\par
nor the favour either of the weather or of men. When thou shalt have\par
content in thy present estate, and all things present shall add to thy\par
content: when thou shalt persuade thyself, that thou hast all things;\par
all for thy good, and all by the providence of the Gods: and of things\par
future also shalt be as confident, that all will do well, as tending to\par
the maintenance and preservation in some sort, of his perfect welfare\par
and happiness, who is perfection of life, of goodness, and beauty; who\par
begets all things, and containeth all things in himself, and in himself\par
doth recollect all things from all places that are dissolved, that of\par
them he may beget others again like unto them. Such one day shall be thy\par
disposition, that thou shalt be able, both in regard of the Gods, and\par
in regard of men, so to fit and order thy conversation, as neither\par
to complain of them at any time, for anything that they do; nor to do\par
anything thyself, for which thou mayest justly be condemned.\par
\par
II. As one who is altogether governed by nature, let it be thy care to\par
observe what it is that thy nature in general doth require. That\par
done, if thou find not that thy nature, as thou art a living sensible\par
creature, will be the worse for it, thou mayest proceed. Next then thou\par
must examine, what thy nature as thou art a living sensible creature,\par
doth require. And that, whatsoever it be, thou mayest admit of and do\par
it, if thy nature as thou art a reasonable living creature, will not be\par
the worse for it. Now whatsoever is reasonable, is also sociable, Keep\par
thyself to these rules, and trouble not thyself about idle things.\par
\par
III. Whatsoever doth happen unto thee, thou art naturally by thy natural\par
constitution either able, or not able to bear. If thou beest able, be\par
not offended, but bear it according to thy natural constitution, or as\par
nature hath enabled thee. If thou beest not able, be not offended. For\par
it will soon make an end of thee, and itself, (whatsoever it be) at the\par
same time end with thee. But remember, that whatsoever by the strength\par
of opinion, grounded upon a certain apprehension of both true profit and\par
duty, thou canst conceive tolerable; that thou art able to bear that by\par
thy natural constitution.\par
\par
IV. Him that offends, to teach with love and meek ness, and to show him\par
his error. But if thou canst not, then to blame thyself; or rather not\par
thyself neither, if thy will and endeavours have not been wanting.\par
\par
V. Whatsoever it be that happens unto thee, it is that which from all\par
time was appointed unto thee. For by the same coherence of causes, by\par
which thy substance from all eternity was appointed to be, was also\par
whatsoever should happen unto it, destinated and appointed.\par
\par
VI. Either with Epicurus, we must fondly imagine the atoms to be the\par
cause of all things, or we must needs grant a nature. Let this then be\par
thy first ground, that thou art part of that universe, which is governed\par
by nature. Then secondly, that to those parts that are of the same kind\par
and nature as thou art, thou hast relation of kindred. For of these,\par
if I shall always be mindful, first as I am a part, I shall never be\par
displeased with anything, that falls to my particular share of the\par
common chances of the world. For nothing that is behoveful unto the\par
whole, can be truly hurtful to that which is part of it. For this\par
being the common privilege of all natures, that they contain nothing in\par
themselves that is hurtful unto them; it cannot be that the nature of\par
the universe (whose privilege beyond other particular natures, is,\par
that she cannot against her will by any higher external cause be\par
constrained,) should beget anything and cherish it in her bosom that\par
should tend to her own hurt and prejudice. As then I bear in mind that\par
I am a part of such an universe, I shall not be displeased with anything\par
that happens. And as I have relation of kindred to those parts that\par
are of the same kind and nature that I am, so I shall be careful to\par
do nothing that is prejudicial to the community, but in all my\par
deliberations shall they that are of my kind ever be; and the common\par
good, that, which all my intentions and resolutions shall drive unto,\par
as that which is contrary unto it, I shall by all means endeavour to\par
prevent and avoid. These things once so fixed and concluded, as thou\par
wouldst think him a happy citizen, whose constant study and practice\par
were for the good and benefit of his fellow citizens, and the carriage\par
of the city such towards him, that he were well pleased with it; so must\par
it needs be with thee, that thou shalt live a happy life.\par
\par
VII. All parts of the world, (all things I mean that are contained\par
within the whole world), must of necessity at some time or other come to\par
corruption. Alteration I should say, to speak truly and properly; but\par
that I may be the better understood, I am content at this time to use\par
that more common word. Now say I, if so be that this be both hurtful\par
unto them, and yet unavoidable, would not, thinkest thou, the whole\par
itself be in a sweet case, all the parts of it being subject to\par
alteration, yea and by their making itself fitted for corruption, as\par
consisting of things different and contrary? And did nature then either\par
of herself thus project and purpose the affliction and misery of her\par
parts, and therefore of purpose so made them, not only that haply they\par
might, but of necessity that they should fall into evil; or did not she\par
know what she did, when she made them? For either of these two to say,\par
is equally absurd. But to let pass nature in general, and to reason of\par
things particular according to their own particular natures; how absurd\par
and ridiculous is it, first to say that all parts of the whole are, by\par
their proper natural constitution, subject to alteration; and then when\par
any such thing doth happen, as when one doth fall sick and dieth, to\par
take on and wonder as though some strange thing had happened? Though\par
this besides might move not so grievously to take on when any such thing\par
doth happen, that whatsoever is dissolved, it is dissolved into those\par
things, whereof it was compounded. For every dissolution is either\par
a mere dispersion, of the elements into those elements again whereof\par
everything did consist, or a change, of that which is more solid into\par
earth; and of that which is pure and subtile or spiritual, into air.\par
So that by this means nothing is lost, but all resumed again into those\par
rational generative seeds of the universe; and this universe, either\par
after a certain period of time to lie consumed by fire, or by continual\par
changes to be renewed, and so for ever to endure. Now that solid and\par
spiritual that we speak of, thou must not conceive it to be that very\par
same, which at first was, when thou wert born. For alas! all this that\par
now thou art in either kind, either for matter of substance, or of life,\par
hath but two or three days ago partly from meats eaten, and partly from\par
air breathed in, received all its influx, being the same then in no\par
other respect, than a running river, maintained by the perpetual influx\par
and new supply of waters, is the same. That therefore which thou hast\par
since received, not that which came from thy mother, is that which\par
comes to change and corruption. But suppose that that for the general\par
substance, and more solid part of it, should still cleave unto thee\par
never so close, yet what is that to the proper qualities and affections\par
of it, by which persons are distinguished, which certainly are quite\par
different?\par
\par
VIII. Now that thou hast taken these names upon thee of good, modest,\par
true; of ἔμφρων, σύμφρων, ὑπέρφρων; take heed lest at any times by\par
doing anything that is contrary, thou be but improperly so called, and\par
lose thy right to these appellations. Or if thou do, return unto them\par
again with all possible speed. And remember, that the word ἔμφρων notes\par
unto thee an intent and intelligent consideration of every object that\par
presents itself unto thee, without distraction. And the word σύμφρων, a\par
ready and contented acceptation of whatsoever by the appointment of the\par
common nature, happens unto thee. And the word ὑπέρφρων, a\par
super-extension, or a transcendent, and outreaching disposition of thy\par
mind, whereby it passeth by all bodily pains and pleasures, honour and\par
credit, death and whatsoever is of the same nature, as matters of\par
absolute indifferency, and in no wise to be stood upon by a wise man.\par
These then if inviolably thou shalt observe, and shalt not be ambitious\par
to be so called by others, both thou thyself shalt become a new man,\par
and thou shalt begin a new life. For to continue such as hitherto thou\par
hast been, to undergo those distractions and distempers as thou must\par
needs for such a life as hitherto thou hast lived, is the part of one\par
that is very foolish, and is overfond of his life. Whom a man might\par
compare to one of those half-eaten wretches, matched in the\par
amphitheatre with wild beasts; who as full as they are all the body\par
over with wounds and blood, desire for a great favour, that they may be\par
reserved till the next day, then also, and in the same estate to be\par
exposed to the same nails and teeth as before. Away therefore, ship\par
thyself; and from the troubles and distractions of thy former life\par
convey thyself as it were unto these few names; and if thou canst abide\par
in them, or be constant in the practice and possession of them,\par
continue there as glad and joyful as one that were translated unto some\par
such place of bliss and happiness as that which by Hesiod and Plato is\par
called the Islands of the Blessed, by others called the Elysian Fields.\par
And whensoever thou findest thyself; that thou art in danger of a\par
relapse, and that thou art not able to master and overcome those\par
difficulties and temptations that present themselves in thy present\par
station: get thee into any private corner, where thou mayst be better\par
able. Or if that will not serve forsake even thy life rather. But so\par
that it be not in passion but in a plain voluntary modest way: this\par
being the only commendable action of thy whole life that thus thou art\par
departed, or this having been the main work and business of thy whole\par
life, that thou mightest thus depart. Now for the better remembrance of\par
those names that we have spoken of, thou shalt find it a very good\par
help, to remember the Gods as often as may be: and that, the thing\par
which they require at our hands of as many of us, as are by nature\par
reasonable creation is not that with fair words, and outward show of\par
piety and devotion we should flatter them, but that we should become\par
like unto them: and that as all other natural creatures, the fig tree\par
for example; the dog the bee: both do, all of them, and apply\par
themselves unto that which by their natural constitution, is proper\par
unto them; so man likewise should do that, which by his nature, as he\par
is a man, belongs unto him.\par
\par
IX. Toys and fooleries at home, wars abroad: sometimes terror, sometimes\par
torpor, or stupid sloth: this is thy daily slavery. By little and\par
little, if thou doest not better look to it, those sacred dogmata will\par
be blotted out of thy mind. How many things be there, which when as\par
a mere naturalist, thou hast barely considered of according to their\par
nature, thou doest let pass without any further use? Whereas thou\par
shouldst in all things so join action and contemplation, that thou\par
mightest both at the same time attend all present occasions, to perform\par
everything duly and carefully and yet so intend the contemplative part\par
too, that no part of that delight and pleasure, which the contemplative\par
knowledge of everything according to its true nature doth of itself\par
afford, might be lost. Or, that the true and contemnplative knowledge\par
of everything according to its own nature, might of itself, (action\par
being subject to many lets and impediments) afford unto thee sufficient\par
pleasure and happiness. Not apparent indeed, but not concealed. And when\par
shalt thou attain to the happiness of true simplicity, and unaffected\par
gravity? When shalt thou rejoice in the certain knowledge of every\par
particular object according to its true nature: as what the matter and\par
substance of it is; what use it is for in the world: how long it can\par
subsist: what things it doth consist of: who they be that are capable of\par
it, and who they that can give it, and take it away?\par
\par
X. As the spider, when it hath caught the fly that it hunted after, is\par
not little proud, nor meanly conceited of herself: as he likewise that\par
hath caught an hare, or hath taken a fish with his net: as another for\par
the taking of a boar, and another of a bear: so may they be proud,\par
and applaud themselves for their valiant acts against the Sarmatai, or\par
northern nations lately defeated. For these also, these famous soldiers\par
and warlike men, if thou dost look into their minds and opinions, what\par
do they for the most part but hunt after prey?\par
\par
XI. To find out, and set to thyself some certain way and method of\par
contemplation, whereby thou mayest clearly discern and represent unto\par
thyself, the mutual change of all things, the one into the other. Bear\par
it in thy mind evermore, and see that thou be throughly well exercised\par
in this particular. For there is not anything more effectual to beget\par
true magnanimity.\par
\par
XII. He hath got loose from the bonds of his body, and perceiving that\par
within a very little while he must of necessity bid the world farewell,\par
and leave all these things behind him, he wholly applied himself, as to\par
righteousness in all his actions, so to the common nature in all things\par
that should happen unto him. And contenting himself with these two\par
things, to do all things justly, and whatsoever God doth send to like\par
well of it: what others shall either say or think of him, or shall do\par
against him, he doth not so much as trouble his thoughts with it. To go\par
on straight, whither right and reason directed him, and by so doing to\par
follow God, was the only thing that he did mind, that, his only business\par
and occupation.\par
\par
XIII. What use is there of suspicion at all? or, why should thoughts\par
of mistrust, and suspicion concerning that which is future, trouble thy\par
mind at all? What now is to be done, if thou mayest search and inquiry\par
into that, what needs thou care for more? And if thou art well able to\par
perceive it alone, let no man divert thee from it. But if alone thou\par
doest not so well perceive it, suspend thine action, and take advice\par
from the best. And if there be anything else that doth hinder thee, go\par
on with prudence and discretion, according to the present occasion\par
and opportunity, still proposing that unto thyself, which thou doest\par
conceive most right and just. For to hit that aright, and to speed in\par
the prosecution of it, must needs be happiness, since it is that only\par
which we can truly and properly be said to miss of, or miscarry in.\par
\par
XIV. What is that that is slow, and yet quick? merry, and yet grave? He\par
that in all things doth follow reason for his guide.\par
\par
XV. In the morning as soon as thou art awaked, when thy judgment, before\par
either thy affections, or external objects have wrought upon it, is yet\par
most free and impartial: put this question to thyself, whether if that\par
which is right and just be done, the doing of it by thyself, or by\par
others when thou art not able thyself; be a thing material or no. For\par
sure it is not. And as for these that keep such a life, and stand so\par
much upon the praises, or dispraises of other men, hast thou forgotten\par
what manner of men they be? that such and such upon their beds, and such\par
at their board: what their ordinary actions are: what they pursue after,\par
and what they fly from: what thefts and rapines they commit, if not with\par
their hands and feet, yet with that more precious part of theirs, their\par
minds: which (would it but admit of them) might enjoy faith, modesty,\par
truth, justice, a good spirit.\par
\par
XVI. Give what thou wilt, and take away what thou wilt, saith he that is\par
well taught and truly modest, to Him that gives, and takes away. And it\par
is not out of a stout and peremptory resolution, that he saith it, but\par
in mere love, and humble submission.\par
\par
XVII. So live as indifferent to the world and all worldly objects, as\par
one who liveth by himself alone upon some desert hill. For whether here,\par
or there, if the whole world be but as one town, it matters not much for\par
the place. Let them behold and see a man, that is a man indeed, living\par
according to the true nature of man. If they cannot bear with me, let\par
them kill me. For better were it to die, than so to live as they would\par
have thee.\par
\par
XVIII. Make it not any longer a matter of dispute or discourse, what are\par
the signs and proprieties of a good man, but really and actually to be\par
such.\par
\par
XIX. Ever to represent unto thyself; and to set before thee, both the\par
general age and time of the world, and the whole substance of it. And\par
how all things particular in respect of these are for their substance,\par
as one of the least seeds that is: and for their duration, as the\par
turning of the pestle in the mortar once about. Then to fix thy mind\par
upon every particular object of the world, and to conceive it, (as it\par
is indeed,) as already being in the state of dissolution, and of change;\par
tending to some kind of either putrefaction or dispersion; or whatsoever\par
else it is, that is the death as it were of everything in his own kind.\par
\par
XX. Consider them through all actions and occupations, of their lives:\par
as when they eat, and when they sleep: when they are in the act of\par
necessary exoneration, and when in the act of lust. Again, when they\par
either are in their greatest exultation; and in the middle of all\par
their pomp and glory; or being angry and displeased, in great state and\par
majesty, as from an higher place, they chide and rebuke. How base and\par
slavish, but a little while ago, they were fain to be, that they might\par
come to this; and within a very little while what will be their estate,\par
when death hath once seized upon them.\par
\par
XXI. That is best for every one, that the common nature of all doth send\par
unto every one, and then is it best, when she doth send it.\par
\par
XXII. The earth, saith the poet, doth often long after the rain. So is\par
the glorious sky often as desirous to fall upon the earth, which argues\par
a mutual kind of love between them. And so (say I) doth the world bear\par
a certain affection of love to whatsoever shall come to pass With thine\par
affections shall mine concur, O world. The same (and no other) shall the\par
object of my longing be which is of thine. Now that the world doth love\par
it is true indeed so is it as commonly said, and acknowledged ledged,\par
when, according to the Greek phrase, imitated by the Latins, of things\par
that used to be, we say commonly, that they love to be.\par
\par
XXIII. Either thou dost Continue in this kind of life and that is it,\par
which so long thou hast been used unto and therefore tolerable: or thou\par
doest retire, or leave the world, and that of thine own accord, and then\par
thou hast thy mind: or thy life is cut off; and then mayst thou\par
rejoice that thou hast ended thy charge. One of these must needs be.\par
Be therefore of good comfort.\par
\par
XXIV Let it always appear and be manifest unto thee that solitariness,\par
and desert places, by many philosophers so much esteemed of and\par
affected, are of themselves but thus and thus; and that all things are\par
them to them that live in towns, and converse with others as they are\par
the same nature everywhere to be seen and observed: to them that have\par
retired themselves to the top of mountains, and to desert havens, or\par
what other desert and inhabited places soever. For anywhere it thou wilt\par
mayest thou quickly find and apply that to thyself; which Plato saith of\par
his philosopher, in a place: as private and retired, saith he, as if he\par
were shut up and enclosed about in some shepherd's lodge, on the top of\par
a hill. There by thyself to put these questions to thyself or to enter\par
in these considerations: What is my chief and principal part, which hath\par
power over the rest? What is now the present estate of it, as I use it;\par
and what is it, that I employ it about? Is it now void of reason ir no?\par
Is it free, and separated; or so affixed, so congealed and grown\par
together as it were with the flesh, that it is swayed by the motions and\par
inclinations of it?\par
\par
XXV. He that runs away from his master is a fugitive. But the law is\par
every man's master. He therefore that forsakes the law, is a fugitive.\par
So is he, whosoever he be, that is either sorry, angry, or afraid, or\par
for anything that either hath been, is, or shall be by his appointment,\par
who is the Lord and Governor of the universe. For he truly and properly\par
is Νόμος, or the law, as the only νέμων, or distributor and dispenser\par
of all things that happen unto any one in his lifetime--Whatsoever then\par
is either sorry, angry, or afraid, is a fugitive.\par
\par
XXVI. From man is the seed, that once cast into the womb man hath no\par
more to do with it. Another cause succeedeth, and undertakes the\par
work, and in time brings a child (that wonderful effect from such a\par
beginning!) to perfection. Again, man lets food down through his\par
throat; and that once down, he hath no more to do with it. Another\par
cause succeedeth and distributeth this food into the senses, and the\par
affections: into life, and into strength; and doth with it those other\par
many and marvellous things, that belong unto man. These things therefore\par
that are so secretly and invisibly wrought and brought to pass, thou\par
must use to behold and contemplate; and not the things themselves only,\par
but the power also by which they are effected; that thou mayst behold\par
it, though not with the eyes of the body, yet as plainly and visibly as\par
thou canst see and discern the outward efficient cause of the depression\par
and elevation of anything.\par
\par
XXVII. Ever to mind and consider with thyself; how all things that now\par
are, have been heretofore much after the same sort, and after the same\par
fashion that now they are: and so to think of those things which shall\par
be hereafter also. Moreover, whole dramata, and uniform scenes, or\par
scenes that comprehend the lives and actions of men of one calling and\par
profession, as many as either in thine own experience thou hast known,\par
or by reading of ancient histories; (as the whole court of Adrianus,\par
the whole court of Antoninus Pius, the whole court of Philippus, that of\par
Alexander, that of Crœsus): to set them all before thine eyes. For thou\par
shalt find that they are all but after one sort and fashion: only that\par
the actors were others.\par
\par
XXVIII. As a pig that cries and flings when his throat is cut, fancy to\par
thyself every one to be, that grieves for any worldly thing and takes\par
on. Such a one is he also, who upon his bed alone, doth bewail\par
the miseries of this our mortal life. And remember this, that Unto\par
reasonable creatures only it is granted that they may willingly and\par
freely submit unto Providence: but absolutely to submit, is a necessity\par
imposed upon all creatures equally.\par
\par
XXIX. Whatsoever it is that thou goest about, consider of it by thyself,\par
and ask thyself, What? because I shall do this no more when I am dead,\par
should therefore death seem grievous unto me?\par
\par
XXX. When thou art offended with any man's transgression, presently\par
reflect upon thyself; and consider what thou thyself art guilty of in\par
the same kind. As that thou also perchance dost think it a happiness\par
either to be rich, or to live in pleasure, or to be praised and\par
commended, and so of the rest in particular. For this if thou shalt call\par
to mind, thou shalt soon forget thine anger; especially when at the same\par
time this also shall concur in thy thoughts, that he was constrained by\par
his error and ignorance so to do: for how can he choose as long as he\par
is of that opinion? Do thou therefore if thou canst, take away that from\par
him, that forceth him to do as he doth.\par
\par
XXXI. When thou seest Satyro, think of Socraticus and Eutyches, or\par
Hymen, and when Euphrates, think of Eutychio, and Sylvanus, when\par
Alciphron, of Tropaeophorus, when Xenophon, of Crito, or Severus. And\par
when thou doest look upon thyself, fancy unto thyself some one or other\par
of the Cæsars; and so for every one, some one or other that hath been\par
for estate and profession answerable unto him. Then let this come to thy\par
mind at the same time; and where now are they all? Nowhere or anywhere?\par
For so shalt thou at all time be able to perceive how all worldly\par
things are but as the smoke, that vanisheth away: or, indeed, mere\par
nothing. Especially when thou shalt call to mind this also, that\par
whatsoever is once changed, shall never be again as long as the world\par
endureth. And thou then, how long shalt thou endure? And why doth it not\par
suffice thee, if virtuously, and as becometh thee, thou mayest pass that\par
portion of time, how little soever it be, that is allotted unto thee?\par
\par
XXXII. What a subject, and what a course of life is it, that thou doest\par
so much desire to be rid of. For all these things, what are they, but\par
fit objects for an understanding, that beholdeth everything according to\par
its true nature, to exercise itself upon? Be patient, therefore, until\par
that (as a strong stomach that turns all things into his own nature; and\par
as a great fire that turneth in flame and light, whatsoever thou doest\par
cast into it) thou have made these things also familiar, and as it were\par
natural unto thee.\par
\par
XXXIII. Let it not be in any man's power, to say truly of thee, that\par
thou art not truly simple, or sincere and open, or not good. Let him be\par
deceived whosoever he be that shall have any such opinion of thee. For\par
all this doth depend of thee. For who is it that should hinder thee from\par
being either truly simple or good? Do thou only resolve rather not to\par
live, than not to be such. For indeed neither doth it stand with reason\par
that he should live that is not such. What then is it that may upon this\par
present occasion according to best reason and discretion, either be said\par
or done? For whatsoever it be, it is in thy power either to do it, or\par
to say it, and therefore seek not any pretences, as though thou wert\par
hindered. Thou wilt never cease groaning and complaining, until such\par
time as that, what pleasure is unto the voluptuous, be unto thee, to do\par
in everything that presents itself, whatsoever may be done conformably\par
and agreeably to the proper constitution of man, or, to man as he is a\par
man. For thou must account that pleasure, whatsoever it be, that thou\par
mayest do according to thine own nature. And to do this, every place\par
will fit thee. Unto the _cylindrus_, or roller, it is not granted to\par
move everywhere according to its own proper motion, as neither unto\par
the water, nor unto the fire, nor unto any other thing, that either is\par
merely natural, or natural and sensitive; but not rational for many\par
things there be that can hinder their operations. But of the mind and\par
understanding this is the proper privilege, that according to its own\par
nature, and as it will itself, it can pass through every obstacle that\par
it finds, and keep straight on forwards. Setting therefore before thine\par
eyes this happiness and felicity of thy mind, whereby it is able to pass\par
through all things, and is capable of all motions, whether as the fire,\par
upwards; or as the stone downwards, or as the _cylindrus_ through that\par
which is sloping: content thyself with it, and seek not after any other\par
thing. For all other kind of hindrances that are not hindrances of thy\par
mind either they are proper to the body, or merely proceed from the\par
opinion, reason not making that resistance that it should, but basely,\par
and cowardly suffering itself to be foiled; and of themselves can\par
neither wound, nor do any hurt at all. Else must he of necessity,\par
whosoever he be that meets with any of them, become worse than he was\par
before. For so is it in all other subjects, that that is thought hurtful\par
unto them, whereby they are made worse. But here contrariwise, man (if\par
he make that good use of them that he should) is rather the better\par
and the more praiseworthy for any of those kind of hindrances, than\par
otherwise. But generally remember that nothing can hurt a natural\par
citizen, that is not hurtful unto the city itself, nor anything hurt\par
the city, that is not hurtful unto the law itself. But none of these\par
casualties, or external hindrances, do hurt the law itself; or, are\par
contrary to that course of justice and equity, by which public societies\par
are maintained: neither therefore do they hurt either city or citizen.\par
\par
XXXIV. As he that is bitten by a mad dog, is afraid of everything almost\par
that he seeth: so unto him, whom the dogmata have once bitten, or in\par
whom true knowledge hath made an impression, everything almost that\par
he sees or reads be it never so short or ordinary, doth afford a good\par
memento; to put him out of all grief and fear, as that of the poet, 'The\par
winds blow upon the trees, and their leaves fall upon the ground. Then\par
do the trees begin to bud again, and by the spring-time they put forth\par
new branches. So is the generation of men; some come into the world, and\par
others go out of it.' Of these leaves then thy children are. And they\par
also that applaud thee so gravely, or, that applaud thy speeches, with\par
that their usual acclamation, ἀξιοπίστως, O wisely spoken I and speak\par
well of thee, as on the other side, they that stick not to curse thee,\par
they that privately and secretly dispraise and deride thee, they also\par
are but leaves. And they also that shall follow, in whose memories\par
the names of men famous after death, is preserved, they are but leaves\par
neither. For even so is it of all these worldly things. Their spring\par
comes, and they are put forth. Then blows the wind, and they go down.\par
And then in lieu of them grow others out of the wood or common matter\par
of all things, like unto them. But, to endure but for a while, is common\par
unto all. Why then shouldest thou so earnestly either seek after these\par
things, or fly from them, as though they should endure for ever? Yet a\par
little while, and thine eyes will be closed up, and for him that carries\par
thee to thy grave shall another mourn within a while after.\par
\par
XXXV. A good eye must be good to see whatsoever is to be seen, and not\par
green things only. For that is proper to sore eyes. So must a good\par
ear, and a good smell be ready for whatsoever is either to be heard,\par
or smelt: and a good stomach as indifferent to all kinds of food, as\par
a millstone is, to whatsoever she was made for to grind. As ready\par
therefore must a sound understanding be for whatsoever shall happen. But\par
he that saith, O that my children might live! and, O that all men might\par
commend me for whatsoever I do! is an eye that seeks after green things;\par
or as teeth, after that which is tender.\par
\par
XXXVI. There is not any man that is so happy in his death, but that some\par
of those that are by him when he dies, will be ready to rejoice at his\par
supposed calamity. Is it one that was virtuous and wise indeed? will\par
there not some one or other be found, who thus will say to himself;\par
'Well now at last shall I be at rest from this pedagogue. He did not\par
indeed otherwise trouble us much: but I know well enough that in his\par
heart, he did much condemn us.' Thus will they speak of the virtuous.\par
But as for us, alas I how many things be there, for which there be many\par
that glad would be to be rid of us. This therefore if thou shalt think\par
of whensoever thou diest, thou shalt die the more willingly, when thou\par
shalt think with thyself; I am now to depart from that world, wherein\par
those that have been my nearest friends and acquaintances, they whom I\par
have so much suffered for, so often prayed for, and for whom I have\par
taken such care, even they would have me die, hoping that after my death\par
they shall live happier, than they did before. What then should any man\par
desire to continue here any longer? Nevertheless, whensoever thou diest,\par
thou must not be less kind and loving unto them for it; but as before,\par
see them, continue to be their friend, to wish them well, and meekly,\par
and gently to carry thyself towards them, but yet so that on the other\par
side, it make thee not the more unwilling to die. But as it fareth with\par
them that die an easy quick death, whose soul is soon separated from\par
their bodies, so must thy separation from them be. To these had nature\par
joined and annexed me: now she parts us; I am ready to depart, as from\par
friends and kinsmen, but yet without either reluctancy or compulsion.\par
For this also is according to Nature.\par
\par
XXXVII. Use thyself; as often, as thou seest any man do anything,\par
presently (if it be possible) to say unto thyself, What is this man's\par
end in this his action? But begin this course with thyself first of all,\par
and diligently examine thyself concerning whatsoever thou doest.\par
\par
XXXVIII. Remember, that that which sets a man at work, and hath power\par
over the affections to draw them either one way, or the other way, is\par
not any external thing properly, but that which is hidden within every\par
man's dogmata, and opinions: That, that is rhetoric; that is life; that\par
(to speak true) is man himself. As for thy body, which as a vessel, or\par
a case, compasseth thee about, and the many and curious instruments\par
that it hath annexed unto it, let them not trouble thy thoughts. For\par
of themselves they are but as a carpenter's axe, but that they are born\par
with us, and naturally sticking unto us. But otherwise, without the\par
inward cause that hath power to move them, and to restrain them, those\par
parts are of themselves of no more use unto us, than the shuttle is\par
of itself to the weaver, or the pen to the writer, or the whip to the\par
coachman.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
THE ELEVENTH BOOK\par
\par
\par
I. The natural properties, and privileges of a reasonable soul are: That\par
she seeth herself; that she can order, and compose herself: that\par
she makes herself as she will herself: that she reaps her own fruits\par
whatsoever, whereas plants, trees, unreasonable creatures, what fruit\par
soever (be it either fruit properly, or analogically only) they bear,\par
they bear them unto others, and not to themselves. Again; whensoever,\par
and wheresoever, sooner or later, her life doth end, she hath her own\par
end nevertheless. For it is not with her, as with dancers and players,\par
who if they be interrupted in any part of their action, the whole action\par
must needs be imperfect: but she in what part of time or action soever\par
she be surprised, can make that which she hath in her hand whatsoever it\par
be, complete and full, so that she may depart with that comfort, 'I have\par
lived; neither want I anything of that which properly did belong unto\par
me.' Again, she compasseth the whole world, and penetrateth into the\par
vanity, and mere outside (wanting substance and solidity) of it, and\par
stretcheth herself unto the infiniteness of eternity; and the revolution\par
or restoration of all things after a certain period of time, to the same\par
state and place as before, she fetcheth about, and doth comprehend in\par
herself; and considers withal, and sees clearly this, that neither they\par
that shall follow us, shall see any new thing, that we have not seen,\par
nor they that went before, anything more than we: but that he that is\par
once come to forty (if he have any wit at all) can in a manner (for\par
that they are all of one kind) see all things, both past and future. As\par
proper is it, and natural to the soul of man to love her neighbour, to\par
be true and modest; and to regard nothing so much as herself: which is\par
also the property of the law: whereby by the way it appears, that sound\par
reason and justice comes all to one, and therefore that justice is the\par
chief thing, that reasonable creatures ought to propose unto themselves\par
as their end.\par
\par
II. A pleasant song or dance; the Pancratiast's exercise, sports that\par
thou art wont to be much taken with, thou shalt easily contemn; if\par
the harmonious voice thou shalt divide into so many particular sounds\par
whereof it doth consist, and of every one in particular shall ask\par
thyself; whether this or that sound is it, that doth so conquer thee.\par
For thou wilt be ashamed of it. And so for shame, if accordingly thou\par
shalt consider it, every particular motion and posture by itself: and\par
so for the wrestler's exercise too. Generally then, whatsoever it be,\par
besides virtue, and those things that proceed from virtue that thou art\par
subject to be much affected with, remember presently thus to divide\par
it, and by this kind of division, in each particular to attain unto the\par
contempt of the whole. This thou must transfer and apply to thy whole\par
life also.\par
\par
III. That soul which is ever ready, even now presently (if need be) from\par
the body, whether by way of extinction, or dispersion, or continuation\par
in another place and estate to be separated, how blessed and happy is\par
it! But this readiness of it, it must proceed, not from an obstinate and\par
peremptory resolution of the mind, violently and passionately set upon\par
Opposition, as Christians are wont; but from a peculiar judgment; with\par
discretion and gravity, so that others may be persuaded also and drawn\par
to the like example, but without any noise and passionate exclamations.\par
\par
IV. Have I done anything charitably? then am I benefited by it. See\par
that this upon all occasions may present itself unto thy mind, and never\par
cease to think of it. What is thy profession? to be good. And how should\par
this be well brought to pass, but by certain theorems and doctrines;\par
some Concerning the nature of the universe, and some Concerning the\par
proper and particular constitution of man?\par
\par
V. Tragedies were at first brought in and instituted, to put men in mind\par
of worldly chances and casualties: that these things in the ordinary\par
course of nature did so happen: that men that were much pleased and\par
delighted by such accidents upon this stage, would not by the same\par
things in a greater stage be grieved and afflicted: for here you see\par
what is the end of all such things; and that even they that cry out\par
so mournfully to Cithaeron, must bear them for all their cries and\par
exclamations, as well as others. And in very truth many good things are\par
spoken by these poets; as that (for example) is an excellent passage:\par
'But if so be that I and my two children be neglected by the Gods, they\par
have some reason even for that,' &c. And again, 'It will but little\par
avail thee to storm and rage against the things themselves,' &c. Again,\par
'To reap one's life, as a ripe ear of corn;' and whatsoever else is\par
to be found in them, that is of the same kind. After the tragedy, the\par
ancient comedy was brought in, which had the liberty to inveigh against\par
personal vices; being therefore through this her freedom and liberty\par
of speech of very good use and effect, to restrain men from pride\par
and arrogancy. To which end it was, that Diogenes took also the same\par
liberty. After these, what were either the Middle, or New Comedy\par
admitted for, but merely, (Or for the most part at least) for the\par
delight and pleasure of curious and excellent imitation? 'It will steal\par
away; look to it,' &c. Why, no man denies, but that these also have some\par
good things whereof that may be one: but the whole drift and foundation\par
of that kind of dramatical poetry, what is it else, but as we have said?\par
\par
VI. How clearly doth it appear unto thee, that no other course of thy\par
life could fit a true philosopher's practice better, than this very\par
course, that thou art now already in?\par
\par
VII. A branch cut off from the continuity of that which was next unto\par
it, must needs be cut off from the whole tree: so a man that is divided\par
from another man, is divided from the whole society. A branch is cut off\par
by another, but he that hates and is averse, cuts himself off from his\par
neighbour, and knows not that at the same time he divides himself from\par
the whole body, or corporation. But herein is the gift and mercy of God,\par
the Author of this society, in that, once cut off we may grow together\par
and become part of the whole again. But if this happen often the misery\par
is that the further a man is run in this division, the harder he is to\par
be reunited and restored again: and however the branch which, once cut\par
of afterwards was graffed in, gardeners can tell you is not like that\par
which sprouted together at first, and still continued in the unity of\par
the body.\par
\par
VIII. To grow together like fellow branches in matter of good\par
correspondence and affection; but not in matter of opinions. They that\par
shall oppose thee in thy right courses, as it is not in their power to\par
divert thee from thy good action, so neither let it be to divert thee\par
from thy good affection towards them. But be it thy care to keep thyself\par
constant in both; both in a right judgment and action, and in true\par
meekness towards them, that either shall do their endeavour to hinder\par
thee, or at least will be displeased with thee for what thou hast done.\par
For to fail in either (either in the one to give over for fear, or in\par
the other to forsake thy natural affection towards him, who by nature is\par
both thy friend and thy kinsman) is equally base, and much savouring of\par
the disposition of a cowardly fugitive soldier.\par
\par
IX. It is not possible that any nature should be inferior unto art,\par
since that all arts imitate nature. If this be so; that the most perfect\par
and general nature of all natures should in her operation come short of\par
the skill of arts, is most improbable. Now common is it to all arts, to\par
make that which is worse for the better's sake. Much more then doth the\par
common nature do the same. Hence is the first ground of justice. From\par
justice all other virtues have their existence. For justice cannot be\par
preserved, if either we settle our minds and affections upon worldly\par
things; or be apt to be deceived, or rash, and inconstant.\par
\par
X. The things themselves (which either to get or to avoid thou art put\par
to so much trouble) come not unto thee themselves; but thou in a manner\par
goest unto them. Let then thine own judgment and opinion concerning\par
those things be at rest; and as for the things themselves, they stand\par
still and quiet, without any noise or stir at all; and so shall all\par
pursuing and flying cease.\par
\par
XI. Then is the soul as Empedocles doth liken it, like unto a sphere or\par
globe, when she is all of one form and figure: when she neither greedily\par
stretcheth out herself unto anything, nor basely contracts herself, or\par
lies flat and dejected; but shineth all with light, whereby she does see\par
and behold the true nature, both that of the universe, and her own in\par
particular.\par
\par
XII. Will any contemn me? let him look to that, upon what grounds he\par
does it: my care shall be that I may never be found either doing or\par
speaking anything that doth truly deserve contempt. Will any hate me?\par
let him look to that. I for my part will be kind and loving unto all,\par
and even unto him that hates me, whom-soever he be, will I be ready to\par
show his error, not by way of exprobation or ostentation of my patience,\par
but ingenuously and meekly: such as was that famous Phocion, if so be\par
that he did not dissemble. For it is inwardly that these things must be:\par
that the Gods who look inwardly, and not upon the outward appearance,\par
may behold a man truly free from all indignation and grief. For what\par
hurt can it be unto thee whatsoever any man else doth, as long as thou\par
mayest do that which is proper and suitable to thine own nature? Wilt\par
not thou (a man wholly appointed to be both what, and as the common good\par
shall require) accept of that which is now seasonable to the nature\par
of the universe?\par
\par
XIII. They contemn one another, and yet they seek to please one another:\par
and whilest they seek to surpass one another in worldly pomp and\par
greatness, they most debase and prostitute themselves in their better\par
part one to another.\par
\par
XIV. How rotten and insincere is he, that saith, I am resolved to carry\par
myself hereafter towards you with all ingenuity and simplicity. O man,\par
what doest thou mean! what needs this profession of thine? the thing\par
itself will show it. It ought to be written upon thy forehead. No sooner\par
thy voice is heard, than thy countenance must be able to show what is in\par
thy mind: even as he that is loved knows presently by the looks of his\par
sweetheart what is in her mind. Such must he be for all the world, that\par
is truly simple and good, as he whose arm-holes are offensive, that\par
whosoever stands by, as soon as ever he comes near him, may as it were\par
smell him whether he will or no. But the affectation of simplicity\par
is nowise laudable. There is nothing more shameful than perfidious\par
friendship. Above all things, that must be avoided. However true\par
goodness, simplicity, and kindness cannot so be hidden, but that as\par
we have already said in the very eyes and countenance they will show\par
themselves.\par
\par
XV. To live happily is an inward power of the soul, when she is affected\par
with indifferency, towards those things that are by their nature\par
indifferent. To be thus affected she must consider all worldly objects\par
both divided and whole: remembering withal that no object can of itself\par
beget any opinion in us, neither can come to us, but stands without\par
still and quiet; but that we ourselves beget, and as it were print in\par
ourselves opinions concerning them. Now it is in our power, not to print\par
them; and if they creep in and lurk in some corner, it is in our\par
power to wipe them off. Remembering moreover, that this care and\par
circumspection of thine, is to continue but for a while, and then thy\par
life will be at an end. And what should hinder, but that thou mayest do\par
well with all these things? For if they be according to nature, rejoice\par
in them, and let them be pleasing and acceptable unto thee. But if\par
they be against nature, seek thou that which is according to thine own\par
nature, and whether it be for thy credit or no, use all possible speed\par
for the attainment of it: for no man ought to be blamed, for seeking his\par
own good and happiness.\par
\par
XVI. Of everything thou must consider from whence it came, of what\par
things it doth consist, and into what it will be changed: what will be\par
the nature of it, or what it will be like unto when it is changed; and\par
that it can suffer no hurt by this change. And as for other men's either\par
foolishness or wickedness, that it may not trouble and grieve thee;\par
first generally thus; What reference have I unto these? and that we are\par
all born for one another's good: then more particularly after another\par
consideration; as a ram is first in a flock of sheep, and a bull in a\par
herd of cattle, so am I born to rule over them. Begin yet higher, even\par
from this: if atoms be not the beginning of all things, than which to\par
believe nothing can be more absurd, then must we needs grant that there\par
is a nature, that doth govern the universe. If such a nature, then are\par
all worse things made for the better's sake; and all better for one\par
another's sake. Secondly, what manner of men they be, at board, and upon\par
their beds, and so forth. But above all things, how they are forced by\par
their opinions that they hold, to do what they do; and even those things\par
that they do, with what pride and self-conceit they do them. Thirdly,\par
that if they do these things rightly, thou hast no reason to be grieved.\par
But if not rightly, it must needs be that they do them against their\par
wills, and through mere ignorance. For as, according to Plato's opinion,\par
no soul doth willingly err, so by consequent neither doth it anything\par
otherwise than it ought, but against her will. Therefore are they\par
grieved, whensoever they hear themselves charged, either of injustice,\par
or unconscionableness, or covetousness, or in general, of any injurious\par
kind of dealing towards their neighbours. Fourthly, that thou thyself\par
doest transgress in many things, and art even such another as they are.\par
And though perchance thou doest forbear the very act of some sins, yet\par
hast thou in thyself an habitual disposition to them, but that either\par
through fear, or vainglory, or some such other ambitious foolish\par
respect, thou art restrained. Fifthly, that whether they have sinned or\par
no, thou doest not understand perfectly. For many things are done by\par
way of discreet policy; and generally a man must know many things\par
first, before he be able truly and judiciously to judge of another\par
man's action. Sixthly, that whensoever thou doest take on grievously, or\par
makest great woe, little doest thou remember then that a man's life is\par
but for a moment of time, and that within a while we shall all be in our\par
graves. Seventhly, that it is not the sins and transgressions themselves\par
that trouble us properly; for they have their existence in their\par
minds and understandings only, that commit them; but our own opinions\par
concerning those sins. Remove then, and be content to part with that\par
conceit of thine, that it is a grievous thing, and thou hast removed\par
thine anger. But how should I remove it? How? reasoning with thyself\par
that it is not shameful. For if that which is shameful, be not the only\par
true evil that is, thou also wilt be driven whilest thou doest follow\par
the common instinct of nature, to avoid that which is evil, to commit\par
many unjust things, and to become a thief, and anything, that will\par
make to the attainment of thy intended worldly ends. Eighthly, how many\par
things may and do oftentimes follow upon such fits of anger and grief;\par
far more grievous in themselves, than those very things which we are so\par
grieved or angry for. Ninthly, that meekness is a thing unconquerable,\par
if it be true and natural, and not affected or hypocritical. For how\par
shall even the most fierce and malicious that thou shalt conceive, be\par
able to hold on against thee, if thou shalt still continue meek and\par
loving unto him; and that even at that time, when he is about to do\par
thee wrong, thou shalt be well disposed, and in good temper, with all\par
meekness to teach him, and to instruct him better? As for example; My\par
son, we were not born for this, to hurt and annoy one another; it will\par
be thy hurt not mine, my son: and so to show him forcibly and fully,\par
that it is so in very deed: and that neither bees do it one to another,\par
nor any other creatures that are naturally sociable. But this thou must\par
do, not scoffingly, not by way of exprobation, but tenderly without\par
any harshness of words. Neither must thou do it by way of exercise, or\par
ostentation, that they that are by and hear thee, may admire thee: but\par
so always that nobody be privy to it, but himself alone: yea, though\par
there be more present at the same time. These nine particular heads, as\par
so many gifts from the Muses, see that thou remember well: and begin one\par
day, whilest thou art yet alive, to be a man indeed. But on the other\par
side thou must take heed, as much to flatter them, as to be angry with\par
them: for both are equally uncharitable, and equally hurtful. And in thy\par
passions, take it presently to thy consideration, that to be angry is\par
not the part of a man, but that to be meek and gentle, as it savours of\par
more humanity, so of more manhood. That in this, there is strength\par
and nerves, or vigour and fortitude: whereof anger and indignation is\par
altogether void. For the nearer everything is unto unpassionateness,\par
the nearer it is unto power. And as grief doth proceed from weakness,\par
so doth anger. For both, both he that is angry and that grieveth, have\par
received a wound, and cowardly have as it were yielded themselves unto\par
their affections. If thou wilt have a tenth also, receive this tenth\par
gift from Hercules the guide and leader of the Muses: that is a mad\par
man's part, to look that there should be no wicked men in the world,\par
because it is impossible. Now for a man to brook well enough, that there\par
should be wicked men in the world, but not to endure that any\par
should transgress against himself, is against all equity, and indeed\par
tyrannical.\par
\par
XVII. Four several dispositions or inclinations there be of the mind and\par
understanding, which to be aware of, thou must carefully observe: and\par
whensoever thou doest discover them, thou must rectify them, saying to\par
thyself concerning every one of them, This imagination is not necessary;\par
this is uncharitable: this thou shalt speak as another man's slave, or\par
instrument; than which nothing can be more senseless and absurd: for\par
the fourth, thou shalt sharply check and upbraid thyself; for that\par
thou doest suffer that more divine part in thee, to become subject and\par
obnoxious to that more ignoble part of thy body, and the gross lusts\par
and concupiscences thereof.\par
\par
XVIII. What portion soever, either of air or fire there be in thee,\par
although by nature it tend upwards, submitting nevertheless to the\par
ordinance of the universe, it abides here below in this mixed body. So\par
whatsoever is in thee, either earthy, or humid, although by nature it\par
tend downwards, yet is it against its nature both raised upwards, and\par
standing, or consistent. So obedient are even the elements themselves to\par
the universe, abiding patiently wheresoever (though against their\par
nature) they are placed, until the sound as it were of their retreat,\par
and separation. Is it not a grievous thing then, that thy reasonable\par
part only should be disobedient, and should not endure to keep its\par
place: yea though it be nothing enjoined that is contrary unto it, but\par
that only which is according to its nature? For we cannot say of it when\par
it is disobedient, as we say of the fire, or air, that it tends upwards\par
towards its proper element, for then goes it the quite contrary way. For\par
the motion of the mind to any injustice, or incontinency, or to sorrow,\par
or to fear, is nothing else but a separation from nature. Also when the\par
mind is grieved for anything that is happened by the divine providence,\par
then doth it likewise forsake its own place. For it was ordained unto\par
holiness and godliness, which specially consist in an humble submission\par
to God and His providence in all things; as well as unto justice: these\par
also being part of those duties, which as naturally sociable, we are\par
bound unto; and without which we cannot happily converse one with\par
another: yea and the very ground and fountain indeed of all just\par
actions.\par
\par
XIX. He that hath not one and the self-same general end always as long\par
as he liveth, cannot possibly be one and the self-same man always. But\par
this will not suffice except thou add also what ought to be this general\par
end. For as the general conceit and apprehension of all those things\par
which upon no certain ground are by the greater part of men deemed good,\par
cannot be uniform and agreeable, but that only which is limited and\par
restrained by some certain proprieties and conditions, as of community:\par
that nothing be conceived good, which is not commonly and publicly\par
good: so must the end also that we propose unto ourselves, be common\par
and sociable. For he that doth direct all his own private motions and\par
purposes to that end, all his actions will be agreeable and uniform; and\par
by that means will be still the same man.\par
\par
XX. Remember the fable of the country mouse and the city mouse, and the\par
great fright and terror that this was put into.\par
\par
XXI. Socrates was wont to call the common conceits and opinions of men,\par
the common bugbears of the world: the proper terror of silly children.\par
\par
XXII. The Lacedæmonians at their public spectacles were wont to appoint\par
seats and forms for their strangers in the shadow, they themselves were\par
content to sit anywhere.\par
\par
XXIII. What Socrates answered unto Perdiccas, why he did not come unto\par
him, Lest of all deaths I should die the worst kind of death, said he:\par
that is, not able to requite the good that hath been done unto me.\par
\par
XXIV. In the ancient mystical letters of the Ephesians, there was an\par
item, that a man should always have in his mind some one or other of the\par
ancient worthies.\par
\par
XXV. The Pythagoreans were wont betimes in the morning the first thing\par
they did, to look up unto the heavens, to put themselves in mind of them\par
who constantly and invariably did perform their task: as also to put\par
themselves in mind of orderliness, or good order, and of purity, and of\par
naked simplicity. For no star or planet hath any cover before it.\par
\par
XXVI. How Socrates looked, when he was fain to gird himself with a\par
skin, Xanthippe his wife having taken away his clothes, and carried them\par
abroad with her, and what he said to his fellows and friends, who were\par
ashamed; and out of respect to him, did retire themselves when they saw\par
him thus decked.\par
\par
XXVII. In matter of writing or reading thou must needs be taught before\par
thou can do either: much more in matter of life. 'For thou art born a\par
mere slave, to thy senses and brutish affections;' destitute without\par
teaching of all true knowledge and sound reason.\par
\par
XXVIII. 'My heart smiled within me.' 'They will accuse even virtue\par
herself; with heinous and opprobrious words.'\par
\par
XXIX. As they that long after figs in winter when they cannot be had; so\par
are they that long after children, before they be granted them.\par
\par
XXX. 'As often as a father kisseth his child, he should say secretly\par
with himself' (said Epictetus,) 'tomorrow perchance shall he die.' But\par
these words be ominous. No words ominous (said he) that signify anything\par
that is natural: in very truth and deed not more ominous than this, 'to\par
cut down grapes when they are ripe.' Green grapes, ripe grapes, dried\par
grapes, or raisins: so many changes and mutations of one thing, not into\par
that which was not absolutely, but rather so many several changes and\par
mutations, not into that which hath no being at all, but into that which\par
is not yet in being.\par
\par
XXXI. 'Of the free will there is no thief or robber:' out of Epictetus;\par
Whose is this also: that we should find a certain art and method of\par
assenting; and that we should always observe with great care and heed\par
the inclinations of our minds, that they may always be with their due\par
restraint and reservation, always charitable, and according to the\par
true worth of every present object. And as for earnest longing, that we\par
should altogether avoid it: and to use averseness in those things only,\par
that wholly depend of our own wills. It is not about ordinary petty\par
matters, believe it, that all our strife and contention is, but whether,\par
with the vulgar, we should be mad, or by the help of philosophy wise and\par
sober, said he. XXXII. Socrates said, 'What will you have? the souls of\par
reasonable, or unreasonable creatures? Of reasonable. But what? Of those\par
whose reason is sound and perfect? or of those whose reason is vitiated\par
and corrupted? Of those whose reason is sound and perfect. Why then\par
labour ye not for such? Because we have them already. What then do ye so\par
strive and contend between you?'\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
THE TWELFTH BOOK\par
\par
\par
I. Whatsoever thou doest hereafter aspire unto, thou mayest even now\par
enjoy and possess, if thou doest not envy thyself thine own happiness.\par
And that will be, if thou shalt forget all that is past, and for the\par
future, refer thyself wholly to the Divine Providence, and shalt bend\par
and apply all thy present thoughts and intentions to holiness and\par
righteousness. To holiness, in accepting willingly whatsoever is sent\par
by the Divine Providence, as being that which the nature of the universe\par
hath appointed unto thee, which also hath appointed thee for that,\par
whatsoever it be. To righteousness, in speaking the truth freely, and\par
without ambiguity; and in doing all things justly and discreetly. Now in\par
this good course, let not other men's either wickedness, or opinion, or\par
voice hinder thee: no, nor the sense of this thy pampered mass of flesh:\par
for let that which suffers, look to itself. If therefore whensoever the\par
time of thy departing shall come, thou shalt readily leave all things,\par
and shalt respect thy mind only, and that divine part of thine, and this\par
shall be thine only fear, not that some time or other thou shalt cease\par
to live, but thou shalt never begin to live according to nature: then\par
shalt thou be a man indeed, worthy of that world, from which thou hadst\par
thy beginning; then shalt thou cease to be a stranger in thy country,\par
and to wonder at those things that happen daily, as things strange and\par
unexpected, and anxiously to depend of divers things that are not in thy\par
power.\par
\par
II. God beholds our minds and understandings, bare and naked from these\par
material vessels, and outsides, and all earthly dross. For with His\par
simple and pure understanding, He pierceth into our inmost and purest\par
parts, which from His, as it were by a water pipe and channel, first\par
flowed and issued. This if thou also shalt use to do, thou shalt\par
rid thyself of that manifold luggage, wherewith thou art round about\par
encumbered. For he that does regard neither his body, nor his clothing,\par
nor his dwelling, nor any such external furniture, must needs gain unto\par
himself great rest and ease. Three things there be in all, which thou\par
doest consist of; thy body, thy life, and thy mind. Of these the two\par
former, are so far forth thine, as that thou art bound to take care for\par
them. But the third alone is that which is properly thine. If then thou\par
shalt separate from thyself, that is from thy mind, whatsoever other men\par
either do or say, or whatsoever thou thyself hast heretofore either\par
done or said; and all troublesome thoughts concerning the future, and\par
whatsoever, (as either belonging to thy body or life:) is without the\par
jurisdiction of thine own will, and whatsoever in the ordinary course\par
of human chances and accidents doth happen unto thee; so that thy\par
mind (keeping herself loose and free from all outward coincidental\par
entanglements; always in a readiness to depart:) shall live by herself,\par
and to herself, doing that which is just, accepting whatsoever doth\par
happen, and speaking the truth always; if, I say, thou shalt separate\par
from thy mind, whatsoever by sympathy might adhere unto it, and all time\par
both past and future, and shalt make thyself in all points and respects,\par
like unto Empedocles his allegorical sphere, 'all round and circular,'\par
&c., and shalt think of no longer life than that which is now present:\par
then shalt thou be truly able to pass the remainder of thy days without\par
troubles and distractions; nobly and generously disposed, and in good\par
favour and correspondency, with that spirit which is within thee.\par
\par
III. I have often wondered how it should come to pass, that every man\par
loving himself best, should more regard other men's opinions concerning\par
himself than his own. For if any God or grave master standing by,\par
should command any of us to think nothing by himself but what he should\par
presently speak out; no man were able to endure it, though but for one\par
day. Thus do we fear more what our neighbours will think of us, than\par
what we ourselves.\par
\par
IV. how come it to pass that the Gods having ordered all other things\par
so well and so lovingly, should be overseen in this one only thing, that\par
whereas then hath been some very good men that have made many covenants\par
as it were with God and by many holy actions and outward services\par
contracted a kind of familiarity with Him; that these men when once they\par
are dead, should never be restored to life, but be extinct for ever. But\par
this thou mayest be sure of, that this (if it be so indeed) would\par
never have been so ordered by the Gods, had it been fit otherwise. For\par
certainly it was possible, had it been more just so and had it been\par
according to nature, the nature of the universe would easily have borne\par
it. But now because it is not so, (if so be that it be not so indeed) be\par
therefore confident that it was not fit it should be so for thou seest\par
thyself, that now seeking after this matter, how freely thou doest argue\par
and contest with God. But were not the Gods both just and good in the\par
highest degree, thou durst not thus reason with them. Now if just and\par
good, it could not be that in the creation of the world, they should\par
either unjustly or unreasonably oversee anything.\par
\par
V. Use thyself even unto those things that thou doest at first despair\par
of. For the left hand we see, which for the most part lieth idle because\par
not used; yet doth it hold the bridle with more strength than the right,\par
because it hath been used unto it.\par
\par
VI. Let these be the objects of thy ordinary meditation: to consider,\par
what manner of men both for soul and body we ought to be, whensoever\par
death shall surprise us: the shortness of this our mortal life: the\par
immense vastness of the time that hath been before, and will he after\par
us: the frailty of every worldly material object: all these things to\par
consider, and behold clearly in themselves, all disguisement of external\par
outside being removed and taken away. Again, to consider the efficient\par
causes of all things: the proper ends and references of all actions:\par
what pain is in itself; what pleasure, what death: what fame or\par
honour, how every man is the true and proper ground of his own rest and\par
tranquillity, and that no man can truly be hindered by any other: that\par
all is but conceit and opinion. As for the use of thy dogmata, thou must\par
carry thyself in the practice of them, rather like unto a pancratiastes,\par
or one that at the same time both fights and wrestles with hands and\par
feet, than a gladiator. For this, if he lose his sword that he fights\par
with, he is gone: whereas the other hath still his hand free, which he\par
may easily turn and manage at his will.\par
\par
VII. All worldly things thou must behold and consider, dividing them\par
into matter, form, and reference, or their proper end.\par
\par
VIII. How happy is man in this his power that hath been granted unto\par
him: that he needs not do anything but what God shall approve, and\par
that he may embrace contentedly, whatsoever God doth send unto him?\par
\par
IX. Whatsoever doth happen in the ordinary course and consequence of\par
natural events, neither the Gods, (for it is not possible, that they\par
either wittingly or unwittingly should do anything amiss) nor men, (for\par
it is through ignorance, and therefore against their wills that they do\par
anything amiss) must be accused. None then must be accused.\par
\par
X. How ridiculous and strange is he, that wonders at anything that\par
happens in this life in the ordinary course of nature!\par
\par
XI. Either fate, (and that either an absolute necessity, and unavoidable\par
decree; or a placable and flexible Providence) or all is a mere\par
casual confusion, void of all order and government. If an absolute and\par
unavoidable necessity, why doest thou resist? If a placable and exorable\par
Providence, make thyself worthy of the divine help and assistance. If\par
all be a mere confusion without any moderator, or governor, then hast\par
thou reason to congratulate thyself; that in such a general flood of\par
confusion thou thyself hast obtained a reasonable faculty, whereby thou\par
mayest govern thine own life and actions. But if thou beest carried\par
away with the flood, it must be thy body perchance, or thy life, or some\par
other thing that belongs unto them that is carried away: thy mind and\par
understanding cannot. Or should it be so, that the light of a candle\par
indeed is still bright and lightsome until it be put out: and should\par
truth, and righteousness, and temperance cease to shine in thee whilest\par
thou thyself hast any being?\par
\par
XII. At the conceit and apprehension that such and such a one hath\par
sinned, thus reason with thyself; What do I know whether this be a sin\par
indeed, as it seems to be? But if it be, what do I know but that he\par
himself hath already condemned himself for it? And that is all one as\par
if a man should scratch and tear his own face, an object of compassion\par
rather than of anger. Again, that he that would not have a vicious man\par
to sin, is like unto him that would not have moisture in the fig, nor\par
children to welp nor a horse to neigh, nor anything else that in the\par
course of nature is necessary. For what shall he do that hath such an\par
habit? If thou therefore beest powerful and eloquent, remedy it if thou\par
canst.\par
\par
XIII. If it be not fitting, do it not. If it be not true, speak it not.\par
Ever maintain thine own purpose and resolution free from all compulsion\par
and necessity.\par
\par
XIV. Of everything that presents itself unto thee, to consider what the\par
true nature of it is, and to unfold it, as it were, by dividing it into\par
that which is formal: that which is material: the true use or end of it,\par
and the just time that it is appointed to last.\par
\par
XV. It is high time for thee, to understand that there is somewhat in\par
thee, better and more divine than either thy passions, or thy sensual\par
appetites and affections. What is now the object of my mind, is it fear,\par
or suspicion, or lust, or any such thing? To do nothing rashly without\par
some certain end; let that be thy first care. The next, to have no other\par
end than the common good. For, alas! yet a little while, and thou art no\par
more: no more will any, either of those things that now thou seest, or\par
of those men that now are living, be any more. For all things are by\par
nature appointed soon to be changed, turned, and corrupted, that other\par
things might succeed in their room.\par
\par
XVI. Remember that all is but opinion, and all opinion depends of the\par
mind. Take thine opinion away, and then as a ship that hath stricken\par
in within the arms and mouth of the harbour, a present calm; all things\par
safe and steady: a bay, not capable of any storms and tempests: as the\par
poet hath it.\par
\par
XVII. No operation whatsoever it he, ceasing for a while, can be truly\par
said to suffer any evil, because it is at an end. Neither can he that\par
is the author of that operation; for this very respect, because his\par
operation is at an end, be said to suffer any evil. Likewise then,\par
neither can the whole body of all our actions (which is our life) if in\par
time it cease, be said to suffer any evil for this very reason, because\par
it is at an end; nor he truly be said to have been ill affected, that\par
did put a period to this series of actions. Now this time or certain\par
period, depends of the determination of nature: sometimes of particular\par
nature, as when a man dieth old; but of nature in general, however; the\par
parts whereof thus changing one after another, the whole world still\par
continues fresh and new. Now that is ever best and most seasonable,\par
which is for the good of the whole. Thus it appears that death of\par
itself can neither be hurtful to any in particular, because it is not a\par
shameful thing (for neither is it a thing that depends of our own will,\par
nor of itself contrary to the common good) and generally, as it is both\par
expedient and seasonable to the whole, that in that respect it must\par
needs be good. It is that also, which is brought unto us by the order\par
and appointment of the Divine Providence; so that he whose will and\par
mind in these things runs along with the Divine ordinance, and by this\par
concurrence of his will and mind with the Divine Providence, is led\par
and driven along, as it were by God Himself; may truly be termed and\par
esteemed the θεοφόρητος, or divinely led and inspired.\par
\par
XVIII. These three things thou must have always in a readiness: first\par
concerning thine own actions, whether thou doest nothing either idly,\par
or otherwise, than justice and equity do require: and concerning those\par
things that happen unto thee externally, that either they happen unto\par
thee by chance, or by providence; of which two to accuse either, is\par
equally against reason. Secondly, what like unto our bodies are\par
whilest yet rude and imperfect, until they be animated: and from their\par
animation, until their expiration: of what things they are compounded,\par
and into what things they shall be dissolved. Thirdly, how vain all\par
things will appear unto thee when, from on high as it were, looking\par
down thou shalt contemplate all things upon earth, and the wonderful\par
mutability, that they are subject unto: considering withal, the infinite\par
both greatness and variety of things aerial and things celestial that\par
are round about it. And that as often as thou shalt behold them, thou\par
shalt still see the same: as the same things, so the same shortness of\par
continuance of all those things. And, behold, these be the things that\par
we are so proud and puffed up for.\par
\par
XIX. Cast away from thee opinion, and thou art safe. And what is it that\par
hinders thee from casting of it away? When thou art grieved at anything,\par
hast thou forgotten that all things happen according to the nature\par
of the universe; and that him only it concerns, who is in fault; and\par
moreover, that what is now done, is that which from ever hath been done\par
in the world, and will ever be done, and is now done everywhere: how\par
nearly all men are allied one to another by a kindred not of blood, nor\par
of seed, but of the same mind. Thou hast also forgotten that every man's\par
mind partakes of the Deity, and issueth from thence; and that no man can\par
properly call anything his own, no not his son, nor his body, nor his\par
life; for that they all proceed from that One who is the giver of all\par
things: that all things are but opinion; that no man lives properly, but\par
that very instant of time which is now present. And therefore that no\par
man whensoever he dieth can properly be said to lose any more, than an\par
instant of time.\par
\par
XX. Let thy thoughts ever run upon them, who once for some one thing or\par
other, were moved with extraordinary indignation; who were once in\par
the highest pitch of either honour, or calamity; or mutual hatred and\par
enmity; or of any other fortune or condition whatsoever. Then consider\par
what's now become of all those things. All is turned to smoke; all to\par
ashes, and a mere fable; and perchance not so much as a fable. As also\par
whatsoever is of this nature, as Fabius Catulinus in the field; Lucius\par
Lupus, and Stertinius, at Baiæ Tiberius at Capreæ and Velius Rufus,\par
and all such examples of vehement prosecution in worldly matters; let\par
these also run in thy mind at the same time; and how vile every object\par
of such earnest and vehement prosecution is; and how much more agreeable\par
to true philosophy it is, for a man to carry himself in every matter\par
that offers itself; justly, and moderately, as one that followeth the\par
Gods with all simplicity. For, for a man to be proud and high conceited,\par
that he is not proud and high conceited, is of all kind of pride and\par
presumption, the most intolerable.\par
\par
XXI. To them that ask thee, Where hast thou seen the Gods, or how\par
knowest thou certainly that there be Gods, that thou art so devout in\par
their worship? I answer first of all, that even to the very eye, they\par
are in some manner visible and apparent. Secondly, neither have I ever\par
seen mine own soul, and yet I respect and honour it. So then for the\par
Gods, by the daily experience that I have of their power and providence\par
towards myself and others, I know certainly that they are, and therefore\par
worship them.\par
\par
XXII. Herein doth consist happiness of life, for a man to know\par
thoroughly the true nature of everything; what is the matter, and what\par
is the form of it: with all his heart and soul, ever to do that which is\par
just, and to speak the truth. What then remaineth but to enjoy thy life\par
in a course and coherence of good actions, one upon another immediately\par
succeeding, and never interrupted, though for never so little a while?\par
\par
XXIII. There is but one light of the sun, though it be intercepted by\par
walls and mountains, and other thousand objects. There is but one common\par
substance of the whole world, though it be concluded and restrained into\par
several different bodies, in number infinite. There is but one common\par
soul, though divided into innumerable particular essences and natures.\par
So is there but one common intellectual soul, though it seem to be\par
divided. And as for all other parts of those generals which we have\par
mentioned, as either sensitive souls or subjects, these of themselves\par
(as naturally irrational) have no common mutual reference one unto\par
another, though many of them contain a mind, or reasonable faculty in\par
them, whereby they are ruled and governed. But of every reasonable mind,\par
this the particular nature, that it hath reference to whatsoever is\par
of her own kind, and desireth to be united: neither can this common\par
affection, or mutual unity and correspondency, be here intercepted or\par
divided, or confined to particulars as those other common things are.\par
\par
XXIV. What doest thou desire? To live long. What? To enjoy the\par
operations of a sensitive soul; or of the appetitive faculty? or wouldst\par
thou grow, and then decrease again? Wouldst thou long be able to talk,\par
to think and reason with thyself? Which of all these seems unto thee a\par
worthy object of thy desire? Now if of all these thou doest find that\par
they be but little worth in themselves, proceed on unto the last, which\par
is, in all things to follow God and reason. But for a man to grieve that\par
by death he shall be deprived of any of these things, is both against\par
God and reason.\par
\par
XXV. What a small portion of vast and infinite eternity it is, that is\par
allowed unto every one of us, and how soon it vanisheth into the general\par
age of the world: of the common substance, and of the common soul also\par
what a small portion is allotted unto us: and in what a little clod of\par
the whole earth (as it were) it is that thou doest crawl. After thou\par
shalt rightly have considered these things with thyself; fancy not\par
anything else in the world any more to be of any weight and moment\par
but this, to do that only which thine own nature doth require; and to\par
conform thyself to that which the common nature doth afford.\par
\par
XXVI. What is the present estate of my understanding? For herein lieth\par
all indeed. As for all other things, they are without the compass of\par
mine own will: and if without the compass of my will, then are they as\par
dead things unto me, and as it were mere smoke.\par
\par
XXVII. To stir up a man to the contempt of death this among other\par
things, is of good power and efficacy, that even they who esteemed\par
pleasure to be happiness, and pain misery, did nevertheless many of them\par
contemn death as much as any. And can death be terrible to him, to\par
whom that only seems good, which in the ordinary course of nature is\par
seasonable? to him, to whom, whether his actions be many or few, so they\par
be all good, is all one; and who whether he behold the things of the\par
world being always the same either for many years, or for few years\par
only, is altogether indifferent? O man! as a citizen thou hast lived,\par
and conversed in this great city the world. Whether just for so many\par
years, or no, what is it unto thee? Thou hast lived (thou mayest be\par
sure) as long as the laws and orders of the city required; which may be\par
the common comfort of all. Why then should it be grievous unto thee, if\par
(not a tyrant, nor an unjust judge, but) the same nature that brought\par
thee in, doth now send thee out of the world? As if the praetor should\par
fairly dismiss him from the stage, whom he had taken in to act a while.\par
Oh, but the play is not yet at an end, there are but three acts yet\par
acted of it? Thou hast well said: for in matter of life, three acts is\par
the whole play. Now to set a certain time to every man's acting, belongs\par
unto him only, who as first he was of thy composition, so is now the\par
cause of thy dissolution. As for thyself; thou hast to do with\par
neither. Go thy ways then well pleased and contented: for so is He that\par
dismisseth thee.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
APPENDIX\par
\par
CORRESPONDENCE OF M. AURELIUS ANTONINUS AND M. CORNELIUS FRONTO[1]\par
\par
M. CORNELIUS FRONTO was a Roman by descent, but of provincial birth,\par
being native to Cirta, in Numidia. Thence he migrated to Rome in the\par
reign of Hadrian, and became the most famous rhetorician of his day.\par
As a pleader and orator he was counted by his contemporaries hardly\par
inferior to Tully himself, and as a teacher his aid was sought for the\par
noblest youths of Rome. To him was entrusted the education of M.\par
\par
Aurelius and of his colleague L. Verus in their boyhood; and he was\par
rewarded for his efforts by a seat in the Senate and the consular rank\par
(A.D. 143). By the exercise of his profession he became wealthy; and if\par
he speaks of his means as not great,[2] he must be comparing his wealth\par
with the grandees of Rome, not with the ordinary citizen.\par
\par
Before the present century nothing was known of the works of Fronto,\par
except a grammatical treatise; but in 1815 Cardinal Mai published a\par
number of letters and some short essays of Fronto, which he had\par
discovered in a palimpsest at Milan. Other parts of the same MS. he\par
found later in the Vatican, the whole being collected\par
\par
[1] References are made to the edition of Naber, Leipzig (Trübner),\par
1867.\par
\par
[2] Ad Verum imp. Aur. Caes., ii, 7. and edited in the year 1823.\par
\par
We now possess parts of his correspondence with Antoninus Pius, with M.\par
Aurelius, with L. Verus, and with certain of his friends, and also\par
several rhetorical and historical fragments. Though none of the more\par
ambitious works of Fronto have survived, there are enough to give proof\par
of his powers. Never was a great literary reputation less deserved. It\par
would be hard to conceive of anything more vapid than the style and\par
conception of these letters; clearly the man was a pedant without\par
imagination or taste. Such indeed was the age he lived in, and it is no\par
marvel that he was like to his age. But there must have been more in him\par
than mere pedantry; there was indeed a heart in the man, which Marcus\par
found, and he found also a tongue which could speak the truth. Fronto's\par
letters are by no means free from exaggeration and laudation, but they\par
do not show that loathsome flattery which filled the Roman court. He\par
really admires what he praises, and his way of saying so is not unlike\par
what often passes for criticism at the present day. He is not afraid to\par
reprove what he thinks amiss; and the astonishment of Marcus at this\par
will prove, if proof were needed, that he was not used to plain dealing.\par
"How happy I am," he writes, "that my friend Marcus Cornelius, so\par
distinguished as an orator and so noble as a man, thinks me worth\par
praising and blaming."[3] In another place he deems himself blest\par
because Pronto had taught him to speak the truth[4] although the context\par
shows him to be speaking of expression, it is still a point in favour of\par
Pronto. A sincere heart is better than literary taste; and if Fronto had\par
not done his duty by the young prince, it is not easy to understand the\par
friendship which remained between them up to the last.\par
\par
[3] Ad M. Caes iii. 17\par
\par
[4] Ad M. Caes iii. 12\par
\par
An example of the frankness which was between them is given by a\par
difference they had over the case of Herodes Atticus. Herodes was a\par
Greek rhetorician who had a school at Rome, and Marcus Aurelius was\par
among his pupils. Both Marcus and the Emperor Antoninus had a high\par
opinion of Herodes; and all we know goes to prove he was a man of high\par
character and princely generosity. When quite young he was made\par
administrator of the free cities in Asia, nor is it surprising to find\par
that he made bitter enemies there; indeed, a just ruler was sure to make\par
enemies. The end of it was that an Athenian deputation, headed by the\par
orators Theodotus and Demostratus, made serious accusations against his\par
honour. There is no need to discuss the merits of the case here; suffice\par
it to say, Herodes succeeded in defending himself to the satisfaction of\par
the emperor. Pronto appears to have taken the delegates' part, and to\par
have accepted a brief for the prosecution, urged to some extent by\par
personal considerations; and in this cause Marcus Aurelius writes to\par
Fronto as follows:—\par
\par
'AURELIUS CÆSAR to his friend FRONTO, greeting.[5]\par
\par
'I know you have often told me you were anxious to find how you might\par
best please me. Now is the time; now you can increase my love towards\par
you, if it can be increased. A trial is at hand, in which people seem\par
likely not only to hear your speech with pleasure, but to see your\par
indignation with impatience. I see no one who dares give you a hint in\par
the matter; for those who are less friendly, prefer to see you act with\par
some inconsistency; and those who are more friendly, fear to seem too\par
friendly to your opponent if they should dissuade you from your\par
accusation; then again, in case you have prepared something neat for\par
the occasion, they cannot endure to rob you of your harangue by\par
silencing you. Therefore, whether you think me a rash counsellor, or a\par
bold boy, or too kind to your opponent, not because I think it better,\par
I will offer my counsel with some caution. But why have I said, offer\par
my counsel? No, I demand it from you; I demand it boldly, and if I\par
succeed, I promise to remain under your obligation. What? you will say\par
if I am attackt, shall I not pay tit for tat? Ah, but you will get\par
greater glory, if even when attackt you answer nothing. Indeed, if he\par
begins it, answer as you will and you will have fair excuse; but I have\par
demanded of him that he shall not begin, and I think I have succeeded.\par
I love each of you according to your merits and I know that lie was\par
educated in the house of P. Calvisius, my grandfather, and that I was\par
educated by you; therefore I am full of anxiety that this most\par
disagreeable business shall be managed as honourably as possible. I\par
trust you may approve my advice, for my intention you will approve. At\par
least I prefer to write unwisely rather than to be silent unkindly.'\par
\par
[5] Ad M. Caes ii., 2.\par
\par
Fronto replied, thanking the prince for his advice, and promising that\par
he will confine himself to the facts of the case. But he points out that\par
the charges brought against Herodes were such, that they can hardly be\par
made agreeable; amongst them being spoliation, violence, and murder.\par
However, he is willing even to let some of these drop if it be the\par
prince's pleasure. To this Marcus returned the following answer:--[6]\par
'This one thing, my dearest Fronto, is enough to make me truly grateful\par
to you, that so far from rejecting my counsel, you have even approved\par
it. As to the question you raise in your kind letter, my opinion is\par
this: all that concerns the case which you are supporting must be\par
clearly brought forward; what concerns your own feelings, though you may\par
have had just provocation, should be left unsaid.' The story does credit\par
to both. Fronto shows no loss of temper at the interference, nor shrinks\par
from stating his case with frankness; and Marcus, with forbearance\par
remarkable in a prince, does not command that his friend be left\par
unmolested, but merely stipulates for a fair trial on the merits of the\par
case.\par
\par
[6] Ad. M. Caes., iii. 5.\par
\par
Another example may be given from a letter of Fronto's[7] Here is\par
something else quarrelsome and querulous. I have sometimes found fault\par
with you in your absence somewhat seriously in the company of a few\par
of my most intimate friends: at times, for example, when you mixt in\par
society with a more solemn look than was fitting, or would read books\par
in the theatre or in a banquet; nor did I absent myself from theatre\par
or banquet when you did.[8] Then I used to call you a hard man, no good\par
company, even disagreeable, sometimes, when anger got the better of me.\par
But did any one else in the same banquet speak against you, I could\par
not endure to hear it with equanimity. Thus it was easier for me to say\par
something to your disadvantage myself, than to hear others do it; just\par
as I could more easily bear to chastise my daughter Gratia, than to see\par
her chastised by another.'\par
\par
[7] Ad. M. Caes., iv. 12.\par
\par
[8] The text is obscure\par
\par
The affection between them is clear from every page of the\par
correspondence. A few instances are now given, which were written at\par
different periods\par
\par
To MY MASTER.[9]\par
\par
'This is how I have past the last few days. My sister was suddenly\par
seized with an internal pain, so violent that I was horrified at her\par
looks; my mother in her trepidation on that account accidentally\par
bruised her side on a corner of the wall; she and we were greatly\par
troubled about that blow. For myself; on going to rest I found a\par
scorpion in my bed; but I did not lie down upon him, I killed him\par
first. If you are getting on better, that is a consolation. My mother\par
is easier now, thanks be to God. Good-bye, best and sweetest master. My\par
lady sends you greeting.'\par
\par
[9] Ad M. Caes., v. 8.\par
\par
[10]'What words can I find to fit my had luck, or how shall I upbraid as\par
it deserves the hard constraint which is laid upon me? It ties me fast\par
here, troubled my heart is, and beset by such anxiety; nor does it allow\par
me to make haste to my Fronto, my life and delight, to be near him at\par
such a moment of ill-health in particular, to hold his hands, to chafe\par
gently that identical foot, so far as may be done without discomfort, to\par
attend him in the bath, to support his steps with my arm.'\par
\par
[10] Ad M. Caes., i. 2.\par
\par
[11]'This morning I did not write to you, because I heard you were\par
better, and because I was myself engaged in other business, and I\par
cannot ever endure to write anything to you unless with mind at ease and\par
untroubled and free. So if we are all right, let me know: what I desire,\par
you know, and how properly I desire it, I know. Farewell, my master,\par
always in every chance first in my mind, as you deserve to be. My\par
master, see I am not asleep, and I compel myself to sleep, that you may\par
not be angry with me. You gather I am writing this late at night.'\par
\par
[11] iii. 21.\par
\par
[12]'What spirit do you suppose is in me, when I remember how long it\par
is since I have seen you, and why I have not seen you! and it may be\par
I shall not see you for a few days yet, while you are strengthening\par
yourself; as you must. So while you lie on the sick-bed, my spirit also\par
will lie low anti, whenas,[13] by God's mercy you shall stand upright,\par
my spirit too will stand firm, which is now burning with the strongest\par
desire for you. Farewell, soul of your prince, your pupil.'\par
\par
[14]O my dear Fronto, most distinguished Consul! I yield, you have\par
conquered: all who have ever loved before, you have conquered out and\par
out in love's contest. Receive the victor's wreath; and the herald\par
shall proclaim your victory aloud before your own tribunal: "M.\par
Cornelius Fronto, Consul, wins, and is crowned victor in the Open\par
International Love-race."[15] But beaten though I may be, I shall\par
neither slacken nor relax my own zeal. Well, you shall love me more\par
than any man loves any other man; but I, who possess a faculty of\par
loving less strong, shall love you more than any one else loves you;\par
more indeed than you love yourself. Gratia and I will have to fight for\par
it; I doubt I shall not get the better of her. For, as Plautus says,\par
her love is like rain, whose big drops not only penetrate the dress,\par
but drench to the very marrow.'\par
\par
[12] Ad M. Caes., iii. 19.\par
\par
[13] The writer sometimes uses archaisms such as _quom_, which I render\par
'whenas'.\par
\par
[14] Ad M. Caes., ii. 2.\par
\par
[15] The writer parodies the proclamation at the Greek games; the words\par
also are Greek.\par
\par
Marcus Aurelius seems to have been about eighteen years of age when\par
the correspondence begins, Fronto being some thirty years older.[16] The\par
systematic education of the young prince seems to have been finisht, and\par
Pronto now acts more as his adviser than his tutor. He recommends\par
the prince to use simplicity in his public speeches, and to avoid\par
affectation.[17] Marcus devotes his attention to the old authors who then\par
had a great vogue at Rome: Ennius, Plautus, Nævius, and such orators\par
as Cato and Gracchus.[18] Pronto urges on him the study of Cicero, whose\par
letters, he says, are all worth reading.\par
\par
[16] From internal evidence: the letters are not arranged in order of\par
time. See Naher's _Prolegomena_, p. xx. foll.\par
\par
[17] Ad M. Caes., iii. x.\par
\par
[18] Ad M. Caes ii. 10,; iii. 18,; ii. 4.\par
\par
When he wishes to compliment Marcus he declares one or other of his\par
letters has the true Tullian ring. Marcus gives his nights to reading\par
when he ought to be sleeping. He exercises himself in verse composition\par
and on rhetorical themes.\par
\par
'It is very nice of you,' he writes to Fronto,[19] 'to ask for my\par
hexameters; I would have sent them at once if I had them by me. The fact\par
is my secretary, Anicetus-you know who I mean-did not pack up any of my\par
compositions for me to take away with me. He knows my weakness; he was\par
afraid that if I got hold of them I might, as usual, make smoke of them.\par
However, there was no fear for the hexameters. I must confess the truth\par
to my master: I love them. I study at night, since the day is taken up\par
with the theatre. I am weary of an evening, and sleepy in the daylight,\par
and so I don't do much. Yet I have made extracts from sixty books, five\par
volumes of them, in these latter days. But when you read remember\par
that the "sixty" includes plays of Novius, and farces, and some little\par
speeches of Scipio; don't be too much startled at the number. You\par
remember your Polemon; but I pray you do not remember Horace, who has\par
died with Pollio as far as I am concerned.[20] Farewell, my dearest\par
and most affectionate friend, most distinguished consul and my beloved\par
master, whom I have not seen these two years. Those who say two months,\par
count the days. Shall I ever see you again?'\par
\par
[19] Ad M. Caes., ii. 10.\par
\par
[20] He implies, as in i. 6, that he has ceased to study Horace.\par
\par
Sometimes Fronto sends him a theme to work up, as thus: 'M. Lucilius\par
tribune of the people violently throws into prison a free Roman citizen,\par
against the opinion of his colleagues who demand his release. For this\par
act he is branded by the censor. Analyse the case, and then take both\par
sides in turn, attacking and defending.'[21] Or again: 'A Roman consul,\par
doffing his state robe, dons the gauntlet and kills a lion amongst\par
the young men at the Quinquatrus in full view of the people of Rome.\par
Denunciation before the censors.'[22] The prince has a fair knowledge of\par
Greek, and quotes from Homer, Plato, Euripides, but for some reason\par
Fronto dissuaded him from this study.[23] His _Meditations_ are written in\par
Greek. He continued his literary studies throughout his life, and after\par
he became emperor we still find him asking his adviser for copies of\par
Cicero's Letters, by which he hopes to improve his vocabulary.[24] Pronto\par
helps him with a supply of similes, which, it seems, he did not think of\par
readily. It is to be feared that the fount of Marcus's eloquence was\par
pumped up by artificial means.\par
\par
[21] Pollio was a grammarian, who taught Marcus.\par
\par
[22] Ad M. Caes., v. 27,; V. 22.\par
\par
[23] Ep. Gracae, 6.\par
\par
[24] Ad Anton. Imp., II. 4.\par
\par
Some idea of his literary style may be gathered from the letter which\par
follows:[25]\par
\par
'I heard Polemo declaim the other day, to say something of things\par
sublunary. If you ask what I thought of him, listen. He seems to me an\par
industrious farmer, endowed with the greatest skill, who has cultivated\par
a large estate for corn and vines only, and indeed with a rich return\par
of fine crops. But yet in that land of his there is no Pompeian fig or\par
Arician vegetable, no Tarentine rose, or pleasing coppice, or thick\par
grove, or shady plane tree; all is for use rather than for pleasure,\par
such as one ought rather to commend, but cares not to love.\par
\par
[25] Ad M. Caes, ii. 5.\par
\par
A pretty bold idea, is it not, and rash judgment, to pass censure on a\par
man of such reputation? But whenas I remember that I am writing to you,\par
I think I am less bold than you would have me.\par
\par
'In that point I am wholly undecided.\par
\par
'There's an unpremeditated hendecasyllable for you. So before I begin to\par
poetize, I'll take an easy with you. Farewell, my heart's desire, your\par
Verus's best beloved, most distinguisht consul, master most sweet.\par
Farewell I ever pray, sweetest soul.\par
\par
What a letter do you think you have written me I could make bold to\par
say, that never did she who bore me and nurst me, write anything SO\par
delightful, so honey-sweet. And this does not come of your fine style\par
and eloquence: otherwise not my mother only, but all who breathe.'\par
\par
To the pupil, never was anything on earth so fine as his master's\par
eloquence; on this theme Marcus fairly bubbles over with enthusiasm.\par
\par
[26]'Well, if the ancient Greeks ever wrote anything like this, let\par
those who know decide it: for me, if I dare say so, I never read any\par
invective of Cato's so fine as your encomtum. O if my Lord[27] could be\par
sufficiently praised, sufficiently praised he would have been\par
undoubtedly by you! This kind of thing is not done nowadays.[28] It\par
were easier to match Pheidias, easier to match Apelles, easier in a\par
word to match Demosthenes himself, or Cato himself; than to match this\par
finisht and perfect work. Never have I read anything more refined,\par
anything more after the ancient type, anything more delicious, anything\par
more Latin. O happy you, to be endowed with eloquence so great! O happy\par
I, to be tinder the charge of such a master! O arguments,[29] O\par
arrangement, O elegance, O wit, O beauty, O words, O brilliancy, O\par
subtilty, O grace, O treatment, O everything! Mischief take me, if you\par
ought not to have a rod put in your hand one day, a diadem on your\par
brow, a tribunal raised for you; then the herald would summon us\par
all-why do I say "us"? Would summnon all, those scholars and orators:\par
one by one you would beckon them forward with your rod and admonish\par
them. Hitherto I have had no fear of this admonition; many things help\par
me to enter within your school. I write this in the utmost haste; for\par
whenas I am sending you so kindly a letter from my Lord, what needs a\par
longer letter of mine? Farewell then, glory of Roman eloquence, boast\par
of your friends, magnifico, most delightful man, most distinguished\par
consul, master most sweet.\par
\par
[26] Ad M. Caes., ii. 3.\par
\par
[27] The Emperor Antoninus Pius is spoken of as _dominus meus_.\par
\par
[28] This sentence is written in Greek.\par
\par
[29] Several of these words are Greek, and the meaning is not quite\par
clear.\par
\par
'After this you will take care not to tell so many fibs of me,\par
especially in the Senate. A monstrous fine speech this is! O if I could\par
kiss your head at every heading of it! You have looked down on all with\par
a vengeance. This oration once read, in vain shall we study, in vain\par
shall we toil, in vain strain every nerve. Farewell always, most sweet\par
master.'\par
\par
Sometimes Fronto descends from the heights of eloquence to offer\par
practical advice; as when he suggests how Marcus should deal with his\par
suite. It is more difficult, he admits, to keep courtiers in harmony\par
than to tame lions with a lute; but if it is to be done, it must be by\par
eradicating jealousy. 'Do not let your friends,' says Fronto,'[30] 'envy\par
each other, or think that what you give to another is filched from them.\par
\par
[30] Ad M Caes., iv. 1.\par
\par
Keep away envy from your suite, and you will find your friends kindly\par
and harmonious.'\par
\par
Here and there we meet with allusions to his daily life, which we could\par
wish to be more frequent. He goes to the theatre or the law-courts,[31]\par
or takes part in court ceremony, but his heart is always with his\par
books. The vintage season, with its religious rites, was always spent by\par
Antoninus Pius in the country. The following letters give sonic notion\par
of a day's occupation at that time:(3)\par
\par
[31] ii. 14\par
\par
[32] iv. 5,6.\par
\par
'MY DEAREST MASTER,--I am well. To-day I studied from the ninth hour of\par
the night to the second hour of day, after taking food. I then put on\par
my slippers, and from time second to the third hour had a most\par
enjoyable walk up and down before my chamber. Then booted and\par
cloaked-for so we were commanded to appear-I went to wait upon my lord\par
the emperor. We went a-hunting, did doughty deeds, heard a rumour that\par
boars had been caught, but there was nothing to see. However, we\par
climbed a pretty steep hill, and in the afternoon returned home. I went\par
straight to my books. Off with the boots, down with the cloak; I spent\par
a couple of hours in bed. I read Cato's speech on the Property of\par
Pulchra, and another in which he impeaches a tribune. Ho, ho! I hear\par
you cry to your man, Off with you as fast as you can, and bring me\par
these speeches from the library of Apollo. No use to send: I have those\par
books with me too. You must get round the Tiberian librarian; you will\par
have to spend something on the matter; and when I return to town, I\par
shall expect to go shares with him. Well, after reading these speeches\par
I wrote a wretched trifle, destined for drowning or burning. No, indeed\par
my attempt at writing did not come off at all to-day; the composition\par
of a hunter or a vintager, whose shouts are echoing through my chamber,\par
hateful and wearisome as the law-courts. What have I said? Yes, it was\par
rightly said, for my master is an orator. I think I have caught cold,\par
whether from walking in slippers or from writing badly, I do not know.\par
I am always annoyed with phlegm, but to-day I seem to snivel more than\par
usual. Well, I will pour oil on my head and go off to sleep. I don't\par
mean to put one drop in my lamp to-day, so weary am I from riding and\par
sneezing. Farewell, dearest and most beloved master, whom I miss, I may\par
say, more than Rome itself.'\par
\par
'MY BELOVED MASTER,-I am well. I slept a little more than usual for my\par
slight cold, which seems to be well again. So I spent the time from the\par
eleventh hour of the night to the third of the day partly in reading in\par
Cato's Agriculture, partly in writing, not quite so badly as yesterday\par
indeed. Then, after waiting upon my father, I soothed my throat with\par
honey-water, ejecting it without swallowing: I might say _gargle_, but I\par
won't, though I think the word is found in Novius and elsewhere. After\par
attending to my throat I went to my father, and stood by his side as he\par
sacrificed. Then to luncheon. What do you think I had to eat? A bit of\par
bread so big, while I watched others gobbling boiled beans, onions,\par
and fish full of roe. Then we set to work at gathering the grapes,\par
with plenty of sweat and shouting, and, as the quotation runs, "A few\par
high-hanging clusters did we leave survivors of the vintage." After the\par
sixth hour we returned home. I did a little work, and poor work at that.\par
Then I had a long gossip with my dear mother sitting on the bed. My\par
conversation was: What do you think my friend Fronto is doing just now?\par
She said: And what do you think of my friend Gratia?'[33] My turn now:\par
And what of our little Gratia,[34] the sparrowkin? After this kind of\par
talk, and an argument as to which of you loved the other most, the gong\par
sounded, the signal that my father had gone to the bath. We supped,\par
after ablutions in the oil-cellar-I mean we supped after ablutions, not\par
after ablutions in the oil-cellar; and listened with enjoyment to the\par
rustics gibing. After returning, before turning on my side to snore, I\par
do my task and give an account of the day to my delightful master, whom\par
if I could long for a little more, I should not mind growing a trifle\par
thinner. Farewell, Fronto, wherever you are, honey-sweet, my darling, my\par
delight. Why do I want you? I can love you while far away.'\par
\par
[33] Fronto's wife.\par
\par
[34] Fronto's daughter\par
\par
One anecdote puts Marcus before us in a new light:[35]\par
\par
[35] Ad M. Caes ii. 12.\par
\par
'When my father returned home from the vineyards, I mounted my horse as\par
usual, and rode on ahead some little way. Well, there on the road was a\par
herd of sheep, standing all crowded together as though the place were\par
a desert, with four dogs and two shepherds, but nothing else. Then one\par
shepherd said to another shepherd, on seeing a number of horsemen: 'I\par
say,' says he, 'look you at those horsemen; they do a deal of robbery.'\par
When I heard this, I clap spurs to my horse, and ride straight for the\par
sheep. In consternation the sheep scatter; hither and thither they are\par
fleeting and bleating. A shepherd throws his fork, and the fork falls\par
on the horseman who came next to me. We make our escape.' We like Marcus\par
none the worse for this spice of mischief.\par
\par
Another letter[36] describes a visit to a country town, and shows the\par
antiquarian spirit of the writer:—\par
\par
'M. CÆSAR to his MASTER M. FRONTO, greeting.\par
\par
'After I entered the carriage, after I took leave of you, we made a\par
journey comfortable enough, but we had a few drops of rain to wet us.\par
But before coming to the country-house, we broke our journey at Anagnia,\par
a mile or so from the highroad. Then we inspected that ancient town, a\par
miniature it is, but has in it many antiquities, temples, and religious\par
ceremonies quite out of the way. There is not a corner without its\par
shrine, or fane, or temple; besides, many books written on linen, which\par
belongs to things sacred. Then on the gate as we came out was written\par
twice, as follows: "Priest don the fell."[37] I asked one of the\par
inhabitants what that word was. He said it was the word in the Hernican\par
dialect for the victim's skin, which the priest puts over his conical\par
cap when he enters the city. I found out many other things which I\par
desired to know, but the only thing I do not desire is that you should\par
be absent from me; that is my chief anxiety. Now for yourself, when you\par
left that place, did you go to Aurelia or to Campania? Be sure to write\par
to me, and say whether you have opened the vintage, or carried a host of\par
books to the country-house; this also, whether you miss me; I am foolish\par
to ask it, whenas you tell it me of yourself. Now if you miss me and\par
if you love me, send me your letters often, which is a comfort and\par
consolation to me. Indeed I should prefer ten times to read your letters\par
than all the vines of Gaurus or the Marsians; for these Signian vines\par
have grapes too rank and fruit too sharp in the taste, but I prefer wine\par
to must for drinking. Besides, those grapes are nicer to eat dried than\par
fresh-ripe; I vow I would rather tread them under foot than put my teeth\par
in them. But I pray they may be gracious and forgiving, and grant me\par
free pardon for these jests of mine. Farewell, best friend, dearest,\par
most learned, sweetest master. When you see the must ferment in the vat,\par
remember that just so in my heart the longing for you is gushing and\par
flowing and bubbling. Good-bye.'\par
\par
[36] Ad Verum. Imp ii. 1, s. fin.\par
\par
[37] Santentum\par
\par
Making all allowances for conventional exaggerations, it is clear from\par
the correspondence that there was deep love between Marcus and his\par
preceptor. The letters cover several years in succession, but soon after\par
the birth of Marcus's daughter, Faustina, there is a large gap. It does\par
not follow that the letters ceased entirely, because we know part of\par
the collection is lost; but there was probably less intercourse between\par
Marcus and Fronto after Marcus took to the study of philosophy under the\par
guidance of Rusticus.\par
\par
When Marcus succeeded to the throne in 161, the letters begin again,\par
with slightly increased formality on Fronto's part, and they go on for\par
some four years, when Fronto, who has been continually complaining of\par
ill-health, appears to have died. One letter of the later period gives\par
some interesting particulars of the emperor's public life, which are\par
worth quoting. Fronto speaks of Marcus's victories and eloquence in the\par
usual strain of high praise, and then continues.[38]\par
\par
'The army when you took it in hand was sunk in luxury and revelry, and\par
corrupted with long inactivity. At Antiochia the soldiers had been Wont\par
to applaud at the stage plays, knew more of the gardens at the nearest\par
restaurant than of the battlefield. Horses were hairy from lack of\par
grooming, horsemen smooth because their hairs had been pulled out by\par
the roots[39] a rare thing it was to see a soldier with hair on arm or\par
leg. Moreover, they were better drest than armed; so much so, that\par
Laelianus Pontius, a strict man of the old discipline, broke the\par
cuirasses of some of them with his finger-tips, and observed cushions\par
on the horses' backs. At his direction the tufts were cut through, and\par
out of the horsemen's saddles came what appeared to be feathers pluckt\par
from geese. Few of the men could vault on horseback, the rest clambered\par
up with difficulty by aid of heel and knee and leg not many could throw\par
a lance hurtling, most did it without force or power, as though they\par
were things of wool-dicing was common in the camp, sleep lasted all\par
night, or if they kept watch it was over the winecup. By what\par
regulations to restrain such soldiers as these, and to turn them to\par
honesty and industry, did you not learn from Hannibal's sternness, the\par
discipline of Africanus, the acts of Metellus recorded in history.\par
\par
[38] Ad Verum. imp., ii. I, s.fin.\par
\par
[39] A common mark of the effeminate at Rome.\par
\par
After the preceptorial letters cease the others are concerned with\par
domestic events, health and sickness, visits or introductions, birth or\par
death. Thus the empperor writes to his old friend, who had shown some\par
diffidence in seeking an interview:[40]\par
\par
[40] Ad Verum. Imp. Aur. Caes., i. 3.\par
\par
'To MY MASTER.\par
\par
'I have a serious grievance against you, my dear master, yet indeed my\par
grief is more than my grievance, because after so long a time I neither\par
embraced you nor spoke to you, though you visited the palace, and the\par
moment after I had left the prince my brother. I reproached my brother\par
severely for not recalling me; nor durst he deny the fault.' Fronto\par
again writes on one occasion: 'I have seen your daughter. It was like\par
seeing you and Faustina in infancy, so much that is charming her face\par
has taken from each of yours.' Or again, at a later date:[41] I have seen\par
your chicks, most delightful sight that ever I saw in my life, so like\par
you that nothing is more like than the likeness.... By the mercy of\par
Heaven they have a healthy colour and strong lungs. One held a piece of\par
white bread, like a little prince, the other a common piece, like a true\par
philosophers son.'\par
\par
[41] Ad Ant. Imp i., 3.\par
\par
Marcus, we know, was devoted to his children. They were delicate in\par
health, in spite of Fronto's assurance, and only one son survived the\par
father. We find echoes of this affection now and again in the letters.\par
'We have summer heat here still,' writes Marcus, 'but since my little\par
girls are pretty well, if I may say so, it is like the bracing climate\par
of spring to us.'[42] When little Faustina came back from the valley of\par
the shadow of death, her father at once writes to inform Fronto.[43]\par
The sympathy he asks he also gives, and as old age brings more and more\par
infirmity, Marcus becomes even more solicitous for his beloved teacher.\par
The poor old man suffered a heavy blow in the death of his grandson, on\par
which Marcus writes:[44] 'I have just heard of your misfortune. Feeling\par
grieved as I do when one of your joints gives you pain, what do you\par
think I feel, dear master, when you have pain of mind?' The old man's\par
reply, in spite of a certain self-consciousness, is full of pathos. He\par
recounts with pride the events of a long and upright life, in which he\par
has wronged no man, and lived in harmony with his friends and family.\par
His affectations fall away from him, as the cry of pain is forced from\par
his heart:--\par
\par
[42] Ad M. Caes., v. 19\par
\par
[43] iv. 11\par
\par
[44] De Nepote Amissa\par
\par
[45]'Many such sorrows has fortune visited me with all my life long. To\par
pass by my other afflictions, I have lost five children under the most\par
pitiful conditions possible: for the five I lost one by one when each\par
was my only child, suffering these blows of bereavement in such a manner\par
that each child was born to one already bereaved. Thus I ever lost my\par
children without solace, and got them amidst fresh grief.....'\par
\par
[45] De Nepote Amissa 2\par
\par
The letter continues with reflections on the nature of death, 'more to\par
be rejoiced at than bewailed, the younger one dies,' and an arraignment\par
of Providence not without dignity, wrung from him as it were by this\par
last culminating misfortune. It concludes with a summing-up of his life\par
in protest against the blow which has fallen on his grey head.\par
\par
'Through my long life I have committed nothing which might bring\par
dishonour, or disgrace, or shame: no deed of avarice or treachery have\par
I done in all my day's: nay, but much generosity, much kindness, much\par
truth and faithfulness have I shown, often at the risk of my own life.\par
I have lived in amity with my good brother, whom I rejoice to see in\par
possession of the highest office by your father's goodness, and by your\par
friendship at peace and perfect rest. The offices which I have myself\par
obtained I never strove for by any underhand means. I have cultivated\par
my mind rather than my body; the pursuit of learning I have preferred to\par
increasing my wealth. I preferred to be poor rather than bound by any'\par
man's obligation, even to want rather than to beg. I have never been\par
extravagant in spending money, I have earned it sometimes because I\par
must. I have scrupulously spoken the truth, and have been glad to hear\par
it spoken to me. I have thought it better to be neglected than to fawn,\par
to be dumb than to feign, to be seldom a friend than to be often a\par
flatterer. I have sought little, deserved not little. So far as I could,\par
I have assisted each according to my means. I have given help readily\par
to the deserving, fearlessly to the undeserving. No one by proving to be\par
ungrateful has made me more slow to bestow promptly all benefits I could\par
give, nor have I ever been harsh to ingratitude. (A fragmentary passage\par
follows, in which he appears to speak of his desire for a peaceful\par
end, and the desolation of his house.) I have suffered long and painful\par
sickness, my beloved Marcus. Then I was visited by pitiful misfortunes:\par
my wife I have lost, my grandson I have lost in Germany:[46] woe is me!\par
I have lost my Decimanus. If I were made of iron, at this tine I could\par
write no more.'\par
\par
[46] In the war against the Catti.\par
\par
It is noteworthy that in his _Meditations_ Marcus Aurelius mentions\par
Fronto only once.[47] All his literary studies, his oratory and\par
criticism (such as it was) is forgotten; and, says he, 'Fronto taught\par
me not to expect natural affection from the highly-born.' Fronto really\par
said more than this: that 'affection' is not a Roman quality, nor has\par
it a Latin name.[48] Roman or not Roman, Marcus found affection in\par
Fronto; and if he outgrew his master's intellectual training, he never\par
lost touch with the true heart of the man it is that which Fronto's\par
name brings up to his remembrance, not dissertations on compound verbs\par
or fatuous criticisms of style.\par
\par
[47] Book I., 8.\par
\par
[48] Ad Verum, ii. 7\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
NOTES\par
\par
This being neither a critical edition of the text nor an emended edition\par
of Casaubon's translation, it has not been thought necessary to add full\par
notes. Casaubon's own notes have been omitted, because for the most part\par
they are discursive, and not necessary to an understanding of what is\par
written. In those which here follow, certain emendations of his\par
are mentioned, which he proposes in his notes, and follows in the\par
translation. In addition, one or two corrections are made where he has\par
mistaken the Greek, and the translation might be misleading. Those which\par
do not come under these two heads will explain themselves.\par
\par
The text itself has been prepared by a comparison of the editions of\par
1634 and 1635. It should be borne in mind that Casaubon's is often\par
rather a paraphrase than a close translation; and it did not seem worth\par
while to notice every variation or amplification of the original. In\par
the original editions all that Casaubon conceives as understood, but\par
not expressed, is enclosed in square brackets. These brackets are here\par
omitted, as they interfere with the comfort of the reader; and so have\par
some of the alternative renderings suggested by the translator. In a few\par
cases, Latin words in the text have been replaced by English.\par
\par
Numbers in brackets refer to the Teubner text of Stich, but the\par
divisions of the text are left unaltered. For some of the references\par
identified I am indebted to Mr. G. H. Rendall's _Marcus Aurelius_.\par
\par
BOOK II "Both to frequent" (4). Gr. τὸ μή, C. conjectures τὸ μὲ. The\par
text is probably right: "I did not frequent public lectures, and I was\par
taught at home."\par
\par
VI Idiots.... philosophers (9). The reading is doubtful, but the meaning\par
seems to be: "simple and unlearned men"\par
\par
XII "Claudius Maximus" (15). The reading of the Palatine MS. (now lost)\par
was paraklhsiz Maximon, which C. supposes to conceal the letters kl as\par
an abbreviation of Claudius.\par
\par
XIII "Patient hearing... He would not" (16). C. translates his\par
conjectural reading epimonon ollan. on proapsth Stich suggests a reading\par
with much the same sense: .....epimonon all antoi "Strict and rigid\par
dealing" (16). C. translates tonvn (Pal. MS.) as though from tonoz,\par
in the sense of "strain." "rigour." The reading of other MSS. tonvn is\par
preferable.\par
\par
XIII "Congiaries" (13). dianomais, "doles."\par
\par
XIV "Cajeta" (17). The passage is certainly corrupt. C. spies a\par
reference to Chryses praying by the sea-shore in the Illiad, and\par
supposes M. Aurelius to have done the like. None of the emendations\par
suggested is satisfactory. At § XV. Book II. is usually reckoned to\par
begin. BOOK II III. "Do, soul" (6). If the received reading be right,\par
it must be sarcastic; but there are several variants which show how\par
unsatisfactory it is. C. translates "en gar o bioz ekasty so par eanty",\par
which I do not understand. The sense required is: "Do not violence to\par
thyself, for thou hast not long to use self-respect. Life is not (v. 1.\par
so long for each, and this life for thee is all but done."\par
\par
X. "honour and credit do proceed" (12). The verb has dropt out of the\par
text, but C. has supplied one of the required meaning.\par
\par
XI. "Consider," etc. (52). This verb is not in the Greek, which means:\par
"(And reason also shows) how man, etc."\par
\par
BOOK IV XV. "Agathos" (18): This is probably not a proper name, but the\par
text seems to be unsound. The meaning may be "the good man ought"\par
\par
XVI. oikonomian (16) is a "practical benefit," a secondary end. XXXIX.\par
"For herein lieth all...." (~3). C. translates his conjecture olan for\par
ola.\par
\par
BOOK V XIV. katorqwseiz (15): Acts of "rightness" or "straightness."\par
XXIII. "Roarer" (28): Gr. "tragedian." Ed. 1 has whoremonger,' ed.\par
2 corrects to "harlot," but omits to alter' the word at its second\par
occurrence.\par
\par
XXV. "Thou hast... them" (33): A quotation from Homer, Odyssey, iv. 690.\par
\par
XXVII. "One of the poets" (33): Hesiod, Op. et Dies, 197.\par
\par
XXIX and XXX. (36). The Greek appears to contain quotations from sources\par
not known, and the translation is a paraphrase. (One or two alterations\par
are here made on the authority of the second edition.) BOOK VI XIII.\par
"Affected and qualified" (i4): exis, the power of cohesion shown in\par
things inanimate; fusiz, power of growth seen in plants and the like.\par
\par
XVII. "Wonder at them" (18): i.e. mankind.\par
\par
XXXVII. "Chrysippus" (42): C. refers to a passage of Plutarch De\par
Communibus Notitiis (c. xiv.), where Chrysippus is represented as saying\par
that a coarse phrase may be vile in itself, yet have due place in a\par
comedy as contributing to a certain effect.\par
\par
XL. "Man or men..." There is no hiatus in the Greek, which means:\par
"Whatever (is beneficial) for a man is so for other men also."\par
\par
XLII. There is no hiatus in the Greek.\par
\par
BOOK VII IX. C. translates his conjecture mh for h. The Greek means\par
"straight, or rectified," with a play on the literal and metaphorical\par
meaning of ortoz.\par
\par
XIV. endaimonia. contains the word daimwn in composition. XXII. The text\par
is corrupt, but the words "or if it be but few" should be "that is\par
little enough."\par
\par
XXIII. "Plato": Republic, vi. p. 486 A.\par
\par
XXV. "It will," etc. Euripides, Belerophon, frag. 287 (Nauck).\par
\par
"Lives," etc. Euripides, Hypsipyle, frag. 757 (Nauck). "As long," etc.\par
Aristophanes, Acharne, 66 i.\par
\par
"Plato" Apology, p. 28 B.\par
\par
"For thus" Apology, p. 28 F.\par
\par
XXVI. "But, O noble sir," etc. Plato, Gorgias, 512 D. XXVII. "And as\par
for those parts," etc. A quotation from Euripides, Chryssipus, frag. 839\par
(Nauck).\par
\par
"With meats," etc. From Euripides, Supplices, 1110. XXXIII. "They both,"\par
i.e. life and wrestling.\par
\par
"Says he" (63): Plato, quoted by Epictetus, Arr. i. 28, 2 and 22.\par
\par
XXXVII. "How know we," etc. The Greek means: "how know we whether\par
Telauges were not nobler in character than Sophocles?" The allusion is\par
unknown.\par
\par
XXVII. "Frost" The word is written by Casaubon as a proper name,\par
"Pagus.'\par
\par
"The hardihood of Socrates was famous"; see Plato, Siymposium, p. 220.\par
\par
BOOK X XXII. The Greek means, "paltry breath bearing up corpses, so that\par
the tale of Dead Man's Land is clearer."\par
\par
XXII. "The poet" (21): Euripides, frag. 898 (Nauck); compare Aeschylus,\par
Danaides, frag. 44.\par
\par
XXIV. "Plato" (23): Theaetetus, p. 174 D.\par
\par
XXXIV. "The poet" (34): Homer, Iliad, vi. 147.\par
\par
XXXIV. "Wood": A translation of ulh, "matter."\par
\par
XXXVIII. "Rhetoric" (38): Rather "the gift of speech"; or perhaps the\par
"decree" of the reasoning faculty.\par
\par
BOOK XI V. "Cithaeron" (6): Oedipus utters this cry after discovering\par
that he has fulfilled his awful doom, he was exposed on Cithaeron as\par
an infant to die, and the cry implies that he wishes he had died there.\par
Sophocles, Oedipus Tyrannus, 1391.\par
\par
V. "New Comedy...," etc. C. has here strayed from the Greek rather\par
widely. Translate: "and understand to what end the New Comedy was\par
adopted, which by small degrees degenerated into a mere show of skill\par
in mimicry." C. writes Comedia Vetus, Media, Nova. XII. "Phocion" (13):\par
When about to be put to death he charged his son to bear no malice\par
against the Athenians.\par
\par
XXVIII. "My heart," etc. (31): From Homer, Odyssey ix. 413. "They will"\par
From Hesiod, Opera et Dies, 184.\par
\par
"Epictetus" Arr. i. II, 37.\par
\par
XXX. "Cut down grapes" (35): Correct "ears of corn." "Epictetus"(36):\par
Arr. 3, 22, 105.\par
\par
