GLOSSARY\par
\par
This Glossary includes all proper names (excepting a few which are\par
insignificant or unknown) and all obsolete or obscure words. ADRIANUS,\par
or Hadrian (76-138 A. D.), 14th Roman Emperor.\par
\par
Agrippa, M. Vipsanius (63-12 B.C.), a distinguished soldier under\par
Augustus.\par
\par
Alexander the Great, King of Macedonia, and Conqueror of the East,\par
356-323 B.C.\par
\par
Antisthenes of Athens, founder of the sect of Cynic philosophers, and an\par
opponent of Plato, 5th century B.C Antoninus Pius, 15th Roman Emperor,\par
138-161 AD. one of the best princes that ever mounted a throne.\par
\par
Apathia: the Stoic ideal was calmness in all circumstance an\par
insensibility to pain, and absence of all exaltation at, pleasure or\par
good fortune.\par
\par
Apelles, a famous painter of antiquity.\par
\par
Apollonius of Alexandria, called Dyscolus, or the 'ill-tempered,'\par
a great grammarian.\par
\par
Aposteme, tumour, excrescence.\par
\par
Archimedes of Syracuse 287-212 B.C., the most famous mathematician of\par
antiquity.\par
\par
Athos, a mountain promontory at the N. of the Aegean Sea.\par
\par
Augustus, first Roman Emperor (ruled 31 B.C.-14 AD.).\par
\par
Avoid, void.\par
\par
BACCHIUS: there Were several persons of this name, and the one meant is\par
perhaps the musician.\par
\par
Brutus (1) the liberator of the Roman people from their kings, and (2)\par
the murderer of Cæsar.\par
\par
Both names were household words.\par
\par
Cæsar, Caius, Julius, the Dictator and Conqueror.\par
\par
Caieta, a town in Latium.\par
\par
Camillus, a famous dictator in the early days of the Roman Republic.\par
\par
Carnuntum, a town on the Danube in Upper Pannonia.\par
\par
Cato, called of Utica, a Stoic who died by his own hand after the battle\par
of Thapsus, 46 B.C. His name was proverbial for virtue and courage.\par
\par
Cautelous, cautious.\par
\par
Cecrops, first legendary King of Athens.\par
\par
Charax, perhaps the priestly historian of that name, whose date is\par
unknown, except that it must be later than Nero.\par
\par
Chirurgeon, surgeon.\par
\par
Chrysippus, 280-207 B.C., a Stoic philosopher, and the founder of\par
Stoicism as a systematic philosophy.\par
\par
Circus, the Circus Maximus at Rome, where games were held.\par
There were four companies who contracted to provide horses, drivers,\par
etc. These were called Factiones, and each had its distinguishing\par
colour: russata (red), albata (white), veneta (blue), prasina (green).\par
There was high rivalry between them, and riots and bloodshed not\par
infrequently.\par
\par
Cithaeron, a mountain range N. of Attica.\par
\par
Comedy, ancient; a term applied to the Attic comedy of Aristophanes and\par
his time, which criticised persons and politics, like a modern comic\par
journal, such as Punck. See New Comedy.\par
\par
Compendious, short.\par
\par
Conceit, opinion.\par
\par
Contentation, contentment.\par
\par
Crates, a Cynic philosopher of the 4th century B.C.\par
\par
Crœsus, King of Lydia, proverbial for wealth; he reigned 560-546 B.C.\par
\par
Cynics, a school of philosophers, founded by Antisthenes. Their texts\par
were a kind of caricature of Socraticism. Nothing was good but virtue,\par
nothing bad but vice. The Cynics repudiated all civil and social claims,\par
and attempted to return to what they called a state of nature. Many of\par
them were very disgusting in their manners.\par
\par
DEMETRIUS of Phalerum, an Athenian orator, statesman, philosopher, and\par
poet. Born 345 B.C.\par
\par
Democritus of Abdera (460-361 B.C.), celebrated as the 'laughing\par
philosopher,' whose constant thought was 'What fools these mortals be.'\par
He invented the Atomic Theory.\par
\par
Dio of Syracuse, a disciple of Plato, and afterwards tyrant of Syracuse.\par
Murdered 353 B.C.\par
\par
Diogenes, the Cynic, born about 412 B.C., renowned for his rudeness and\par
hardihood.\par
\par
Diognetus, a painter.\par
\par
Dispense with, put up with.\par
\par
Dogmata, pithy sayings, or philosophical rules of life.\par
\par
EMPEDOCLES of Agrigentum, fl.\par
5th century B.C., a philosopher, who first laid down that there were\par
"four elements." He believed in the transmigration of souls, and the\par
indestructibility of matter.\par
\par
Epictetus, a famous Stoic philosopher. He was of Phrygia, at first a\par
slave, then freedman, lame, poor, and contented.\par
The work called Encheiridion was compiled by a pupil from his\par
discourses.\par
\par
Epicureans, a sect of philosophers founded by Epicurus, who "combined\par
the physics of Democritus," i.e. the atomic theory, "with the ethics of\par
Aristippus."\par
\par
They proposed to live for happiness, but the word did not bear that\par
coarse and vulgar sense originally which it soon took.\par
\par
Epicurus of Samos, 342-270 B.C.\par
\par
Lived at Athens in his "gardens," an urbane and kindly, if somewhat\par
useless, life. His character was simple and temperate, and had none of\par
the vice or indulgence which was afterwards associated with the name of\par
Epicurean.\par
\par
Eudoxus of Cnidus, a famous astronomer and physician of the 4th century\par
B. C.\par
\par
FATAL, fated.\par
\par
Fortuit, chance (adj.).\par
\par
Fronto, M. Cornelius, a rhetorician and pleader, made consul in 143 A.D.\par
A number of his letters to M, Aur. and others are extant.\par
\par
GRANUA, a tributary of the Danube.\par
\par
HELICE, ancient capital city of Achaia, swallowed up by an earthquake,\par
373 B.C.\par
\par
Helvidius Priscus, son-in-law of Thrasea Paetus, a noble man and a lover\par
of liberty. He was banished by Nero, and put to death by Vespasian.\par
\par
Heraclitus of Ephesus, who lived in the 6th century B.C. He wrote on\par
philosophy and natural science.\par
\par
Herculaneum, near Mount Vesuvius, buried by the eruption of 79 AD.\par
\par
Hercules, p. 167, should be Apollo. See Muses.\par
\par
Hiatus, gap.\par
\par
Hipparchus of Bithynia, an astronomer of the 2nd century B.C., "The true\par
father of astronomy."\par
\par
Hippocrates of Cos, about 460-357 B.C. One of the most famous physicians\par
of antiquity.\par
\par
IDIOT, means merely the non-proficient in anything, the "layman," he who\par
was not technically trained in any art, craft, or calling.\par
\par
LEONNATUS, a distinguished general under Alexander the Great.\par
\par
Lucilla, daughter of M. Aurelius, and wife of Verus, whom she survived.\par
\par
MÆCENAS, a trusted adviser of Augustus, and a munificent patron of wits\par
and literary men.\par
\par
Maximus, Claudius, a Stoic philosopher.\par
\par
Menippus, a Cynic philosopher.\par
\par
Meteores, ta metewrologika, "high philosophy," used specially of\par
astronomy and natural philosophy, which were bound up with other\par
speculations.\par
\par
Middle Comedy, something midway between the Old and New Comedy. See\par
Comedy, Ancient, and New Comedy.\par
\par
Middle things, Book 7, XXV. The Stoics divided all things into virtue,\par
vice, and indifferent things; but as "indifferent" they regarded most of\par
those things which the world regards as good or bad, such as wealth or\par
poverty. Of these, some were "to be desired," some "to be rejected."\par
\par
Muses, the nine deities who presided over various kinds of poesy, music,\par
etc. Their leader was Apollo, one of whose titles is Musegetes, the\par
Leader of the Muses.\par
\par
NERVES, strings.\par
\par
New Comedy, the Attic Comedy of Menander and his school, which\par
criticised not persons but manners, like a modern comic opera. See\par
Comedy, Ancient.\par
\par
PALESTRA, wrestling school.\par
\par
Pancratiast, competitor in the pancratium, a combined contest which\par
comprised boxing and wrestling.\par
\par
Parmularii, gladiators armed with a small round shield (parma).\par
\par
Pheidias, the most famous sculptor of antiquity.\par
\par
Philippus, founder of the Macedonian supremacy, and father of Alexander\par
the Great.\par
\par
Phocion, an Athenian general and statesman, a noble and high-minded man,\par
4th century B.C.\par
\par
He was called by Demosthenes, "the pruner of my periods."\par
\par
He was put to death by the State in 317, on a false suspicion, and left\par
a message for his son "to bear no grudge against the Athenians."\par
\par
Pine, torment.\par
\par
Plato of Athens, 429-347 B.C. He used the dialectic method invented by\par
his master Socrates.\par
\par
He was, perhaps, as much poet as philosopher. He is generally identified\par
with the Theory of Ideas, that things are what they are by participation\par
with our eternal Idea. His "Commonwealth" was a kind of Utopia.\par
\par
Platonics, followers of Plato.\par
\par
Pompeii, near Mount Vesuvius, buried in the eruption of 79 A. D.\par
\par
Pompeius, C. Pompeius Magnus, a very successful general at the end of\par
the Roman Republic (106-48 B.C.).\par
\par
Prestidigitator, juggler.\par
\par
Pythagoras of Samos, a philosopher, scientist, and moralist of the 6th\par
century B.C.\par
\par
QUADI, a tribe of S. Germany.\par
\par
M. Aurelius carried on war against them, and part of this book was\par
written in the field.\par
\par
RICTUS, gape, jaws.\par
\par
Rusticus, Q. Junius, or Stoic philosopher, twice made consul by M.\par
Aurelius.\par
\par
SACRARY, shrine.\par
\par
Salaminius, Book 7, XXXVII. Leon of Sala-mis. Socrates was ordered by\par
the Thirty Tyrants to fetch him before them, and Socrates, at his own\par
peril, refused.\par
\par
Sarmatae, a tribe dwelling in Poland.\par
\par
Sceletum, skeleton.\par
\par
Sceptics, a school of philosophy founded by Pyrrho (4th century B.C.).\par
He advocated "suspension of judgment," and taught the relativity of\par
knowledge and impossibility of proof. The school is not unlike the\par
Agnostic school.\par
\par
Scipio, the name of two great soldiers, P. Corn. Scipio Africanus,\par
conqueror of Hannibal, and P.\par
\par
Corn. Sc. Afr. Minor, who came into the family by adoption, who\par
destroyed Carthage.\par
\par
Secutoriani (a word coined by C.), the Sececutores, light-armed\par
gladiators, who were pitted against others with net and trident.\par
\par
Sextus of Chaeronea, a Stoic philosopher, nephew of Plutarch.\par
\par
Silly, simple, common.\par
\par
Sinuessa, a town in Latium.\par
\par
Socrates, an Athenian philosopher (469-399 B.C.), founder of the\par
dialectic method. Put to death on a trumped-up charge by his countrymen.\par
\par
Stint, limit (without implying niggardliness).\par
\par
Stoics, a philosophic system founded by Zeno (4th century B.C.), and\par
systematised by Chrysippus (3rd century B.C.). Their physical theory\par
was a pantheistic materialism, their summum bonum "to live according\par
to nature." Their wise man needs nothing, he is sufficient to himself;\par
virtue is good, vice bad, external things indifferent.\par
\par
THEOPHRASTUS, a philosopher, pupil of Aristotle, and his successor as\par
president of the Lyceum. He wrote a large number of works on philosophy\par
and natural history. Died 287 B.C.\par
\par
Thrasea, P. Thrasea Pactus, a senator and Stoic philosopher, a noble and\par
courageous man. He was condemned to death by Nero.\par
\par
Tiberius, 2nd Roman Emperor (14-31 AD.). He spent the latter part of his\par
life at Capreae (Capri), off Naples, in luxury or debauchery, neglecting\par
his imperial duties.\par
\par
To-torn, torn to pieces.\par
\par
Trajan, 13th Roman Emperor, 52-117 A.D.\par
\par
VERUS, Lucius Aurelius, colleague of M. Aurelius in the Empire.\par
\par
He married Lucilla, daughter of M. A., and died 169 A.D.\par
\par
Vespasian, 9th Roman Emperor XENOCRATES of Chalcedon, 396-314 B.C., a\par
philosopher, and president of the Academy.\par
